<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch9-phasoranalysis">
	<title>Analysis of Phasor-Transformed Circuits</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>Now that the concepts of phasors and impedances have been explained, it is possible to use those quantities to analyze the characteristics of linear AC circuits.</p>
	
  	</introduction>

  	<section xml:id="ch9-complexanalysis">
		<title>Complex Circuit Analysis Techniques</title>

		<introduction>
		
			<p>All linear circuits can be analyzed using the following circuit analysis techniques. All of these techniques have been covered earlier in this textbook in the context of DC circuits. Phasors and impedances will be used to implement these tools to solve AC circuits. All sources will be converted into phasor quantities and all passive circuit elements will be converted into impedances.</p>
			
			<p>Because all of these concepts have already been covered, the discussions that follow will not be exhaustive as those that were included in <xref ref="ch2-circuit-laws" /> and <xref ref="ch3-circuittheorems" />.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-voltagedivider">
			<title>Complex Voltage Divider</title>

			<p>For any number of impedances connected in series with a voltage source, the complex voltage divider rule uses the complex form of Ohm's law (defined in <xref ref="ch8-complex-ohms-law" />) to calculate the voltage dropped over any of the impedances.</p>
			
			<p>Given <m>n</m> impedances in series with a source voltage of <m>\vec{V_S}</m>, the voltage drop over the <m>k^\textrm{th}</m> impedance (where <m>k \lt n</m>) is defined in <xref ref="ch9-eq-complex-voltage-divider" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-complex-voltage-divider">\vec{V_k} = \vec{V_S} \left(\frac{\vec{Z_k}}{\vec{Z_1}+\vec{Z_2}+\vec{Z_3}+...+\vec{Z_k}+...+\vec{Z_n}}\right)</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex voltage divider</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v_{out}(t)</m> in the circuit below. The source is equal to <m>v_{AC}(t) = 150~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 60 t + 60 \pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-divider-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, two resistors (55ohm and 105ohm), two inductors (65mH and 210mH), and one 25uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 55ohm at node a. The 55ohm and 65mH connect at node b. The 65mH, 25uF, and 105ohm connect at node c. The 105ohm and 210mH connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, 25uF, and 210mH connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [sV, l^=$v_{AC}(t)$]			(0,5) node[below left] {+};
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=55~$\Omega$]			++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l^=65~mH]				++(right:2.5) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=25~$\mu$F, *-*]			++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=105~$\Omega$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=210~mH]				++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(8.25,5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]		++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						++(right:7.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi60)(25 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j106.10~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of each inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L65}} \amp= j(2\pi 60)(0.065)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j24.5044~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L210}} \amp= j(2\pi 60)(0.210)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j79.1681~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the voltage source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 150\cos{60}+j150\sin{60}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 75 + j129.90~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-divider-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and five impedances (55ohm, 105ohm, j24.5ohm, -j106ohm, and j79.2ohm). The high-potential end of Vac connects to 55ohm at node a. The 55ohm and j24.5ohm connect at node b. The j24.5ohm, -j106ohm, and 105ohm connect at node c. The 105ohm and j79.2ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, -j106ohm, and j79.2ohm connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]				(0,5) node[below left] {+};
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=55~$\Omega$]				++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l^=j24.5~$\Omega$]			++(right:2.5) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=$-$j106~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=105~$\Omega$]				++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j79.2$\Omega$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(8.25,5) to [open, v^=$\vec{V_{out}}$]			++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							++(right:7.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 55<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j24.5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= (55~\Omega) + (j24.5044~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 55 + j24.5044~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The 105<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j79.2<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z''} \amp= (105~\Omega) + (j79.1681~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 105 + j79.1681~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-divider-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and three impedances (55+j24.5ohm, -j106ohm, and 105+j79.2ohm). The high-potential end of Vac connects to 55+j24.5ohm at node a. All three impedances connect at node b. The-low potential end of Vac, -j106ohm, and 105+j79.2ohm connect at node c. Vout drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]					(0,2.5) node[below left] {+};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=55+j24.5~$\Omega$]		++(right:2.75) --++ (right:2.75);
									\draw[]	(2.75,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j106~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=105+j79.2~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_{out}}$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									++(right:5.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j106.1<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the 105+j79.2<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'''} \amp= \frac{(-j106.10~\Omega)(105 + j79.1681)}{(-j106.10~\Omega)+(105 + j79.1681)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100.60 -j80.297~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-divider-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and two impedances (55+j24.5ohm and 101-j80.3ohm). All three circuit elements are connected in series. Vout drops over the 101-j80.3ohm impedance and shares the same polarity as Vac.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]									(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=55+j24.5~$\Omega$]							(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=101$-$j80.3~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_{out}}$]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_{out}}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{out}} \amp= (75 + j129.90~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{100.60 -j80.297~\Omega}{(55 + j24.50~\Omega)+(100.60 -j80.297~\Omega)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 87.9786 + j76.8285~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 116.8026~\textrm{V} \angle 41.1295^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the output to a time-varying signal.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(t) = 116.8~\textrm{V}~\cos{(2\pi60 t+41.1\pi/180)}</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-currentdivider">
			<title>Complex Current Divider</title>

			<p>For any number of impedances connected in parallel with a current source, the complex current divider rule uses the complex form of Ohm's law (defined in <xref ref="ch8-complex-ohms-law" />) to calculate the current through any of the impedances.</p>
			
			<p>Given <m>n</m> impedances in parallel with a source current of <m>\vec{I_S}</m>, the current through the <m>k^\textrm{th}</m> impedance (where <m>k \lt n</m>) is defined in <xref ref="ch9-eq-complex-current-divider" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-complex-current-divider">\vec{I_k} = \vec{I_S}\left(\frac{\vec{Z_{EQ}}}{\vec{Z_k}}\right)</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex current divider</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate each of the currents as shown. The source is equal to <m>i_{AC}(t) = 10~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi 50 t+120\pi/180)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, three resistors (8ohm, 3ohm, and 5ohm), one 470uF capacitor, and one 25mH inductor. The high-potential end of Iac connects to 8ohm and 3ohm at node a. The 3ohm, 470uF, and 5ohm connect at node b. The 5ohm and 25mH connect at node c. The low-potential end of Iac, 8ohm, 470uF, and 25mH connect at node d. Branch current i1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node d. Branch current i2 flows through the 3ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i3 flows through the 470uF capacitor from node b to node d. Branch current i4 flows from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]										(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$i_1(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=3~$\Omega$, i^=$i_2(t)$]				(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=470~$\mu$F, *-*, i^=$i_3(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short,  i^=$i_4(t)$]							(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=25~mH]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the impedances of the inductor and the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j2\pi50(0.025)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j7.85~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi50)(470\times10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j6.77~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the current source to phasor form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 10\cos{120^\circ} + j10\sin{120^\circ}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -5+j8.66~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac and five impedances (8ohm, 3ohm, 5ohm, -j6.77ohm, and j7.85ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to 8ohm and 3ohm at node a. The 3ohm, -j6.77ohm, and 5ohm connect at node b. The 5ohm and j7.85 connect at node c. The low-potential end of Iac, 8ohm, -j6.77ohm, and j7.85 connect at node d. Branch current i1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node d. Branch current i2 flows through the 3ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i3 flows through the -j6.77ohm capacitor from node b to node d. Branch current i4 flows from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=3~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]				(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=$-$j6.77~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short,  i^=$\vec{I_4}$]							(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j7.85~$\Omega$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and j7.85<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Iac and four impedances (8ohm, 3ohm, 5+j7.85ohm, and -j6.77ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to 8ohm and 3ohm at node a. The 3ohm, -j6.77ohm, and 5+j7.85ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of Iac, 8ohm, -j6.77ohm, and 5+j7.85ohm connect at node c. Branch current i1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current i2 flows through the 3ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i3 flows through the -j6.77ohm capacitor from node b to node c. Branch current i4 flows through the 5+j7.85ohm impedance from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j6.77~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short,  i^=$\vec{I_4}$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=5+j7.85~$\Omega$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Refer to <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example" /> for solving a current divider circuit with three meshes. We will need to calculate the equivalent impedance as seen by the <minus />j6.77<nbsp />&#8486; impedance to calculate the branch currents. This impedance will be referred to as <m>\vec{Z'}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(-j6.77)(5+j7.85~\Omega)}{(-j6.77)+(5+j7.85~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.7635 - j8.6680~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Iac and three impedances (8ohm, 3ohm, and 8.76-j8.67ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to the 8ohm and 3ohm at node a. The 3ohm and 8.76-j8.67ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of Iac, 8ohm, and 8.76-j8.67ohm connect at node c. Branch current I1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current I2 flows from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]				(5.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.25,2.5) to [generic, l_=8.76$-$j8.67~$\Omega$]			(5.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 3<nbsp />&#8486;  resistor and 8.76<minus />j8.67<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of current source Iac and two impedances (8ohm and 11.8-j8.67ohm). All three circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of Iac is at node a. Branch current I1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current I2 flows through the 11.8-j8.67ohm impedance from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(5.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.25,2.5) to [generic, l_=11.8$-$j8.67~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]	(5.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of this circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ1}} \amp= \frac{(8~\Omega)(11.7635 - j8.6680~\Omega)}{(8~\Omega)+(11.7635 - j8.6680~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.2841 - j1.1911~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I_1}</m> and <m>\vec{I_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp= (-5+j8.66~\textrm{A})\frac{5.2841 - j1.1911~\Omega}{8~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2.0131 + j6.4647~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.7709~\textrm{A} \angle 107.2968^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= (-5+j8.66~\textrm{A})\frac{5.2841 - j1.1911~\Omega}{11.76-j8.67~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2.9869 + j2.1956~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.7070~\textrm{A} \angle 143.6815^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert both of these values into time-varying signals.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_1(t) \amp= 6.77~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi50t+107\pi/180)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_2(t) \amp= 3.71~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi50t+144\pi/180)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit. Current <m>\vec{I_2}</m> can be used to represent a source supplying the <minus />j6.77<nbsp />&#8486; and 5+j7.85<nbsp />&#8486; impedances.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-divider-example-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of current source I2 and two impedances (-j6.77ohm and 5+j7.85ohm). All three circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of Iac is at node a. Branch current I3 flows through the -j6.77ohm impedance from node a to node b. Branch current I4 flows through the 5+j7.85ohm impedance from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_2}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=-j6.77~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=5+j7.85~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_4}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent impedance of this circuit was previously calculated as <m>\vec{Z'}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I_3}</m> and <m>\vec{I_4}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_3} \amp= (-2.99+j2.20~\textrm{A})\frac{8.7635 - j8.6680~\Omega}{-j6.77~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6.6638 - j1.0549~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.7468~\textrm{A} \angle -171.0044^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_4} \amp= (-2.99+j2.20~\textrm{A})\frac{8.7635 - j8.6680~\Omega}{5+j7.85~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.6769 + j3.2505~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.9077~\textrm{A} \angle 41.4772^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert both of these values into time-varying signals.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_3(t) \amp= 6.75~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi50t-171\pi/180)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_4(t) \amp= 4.91~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi50t+41.5\pi/180)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-kclkvl">
			<title>Complex KCL and KVL Analysis</title>

			<p>Kirchhoff's current and voltage laws still hold in a phasor-transformed circuit. KCL states that the sum of all branch currents entering a node is equal to zero. KVL states that the sum of all voltage drops in a loop is equal to zero.</p>
			
			<p>Together, KCL and KVL can be used to solve for every branch current in a circuit. Those branch currents can be multiplied by impedance to calculate voltage drops over every circuit element. By analyzing nodes and loops in a circuit, a number of equations (using KCL, KVL, and Ohm's law) can be found. If there are <m>n</m> unknown branch currents in the circuit, <m>n</m> linearly independent equations will be needed. A matrix can then be used to solve for the unknowns.</p>
			
			<p>The equations that are derived to generate the matrix defining the circuit will now contain complex coefficients. Not all graphing calculators are capable of solving a matrix with complex coefficients. The TI-89 is capable of solving complex matrices. Otherwise, programs such as Matlab or websites such as <url href="https://www.wolframalpha.com" visual="Wolfram Alpha" /> are capable of doing such calculations.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex KCL / KVL analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate all branch currents in the following circuit. The source values are <m>i_{AC}(t) = 4~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi 100t + 20\pi/180)</m> and <m>v_{AC}(t) = 20~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t + 45\pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-kclkvl-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and six nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, three resistors (50ohm, 20ohm, and 100ohm), two capacitors (20uF and 25uF), and two inductors (25mH and 100mH). The 25mH and 50ohm connect at node a. The 50ohm, high-potential end of Iac, 20ohm, and 20uF connect at node b. The 20ohm, high-potential end of Vac, and 100mH connect at node c. The 20uF, 100mH, and 100ohm connect at node d. The 100ohm and 25uF connect at node e. The 25mH, 25uF, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node f. Branch current i1 flows from node a to node f. Branch current i2 flows through the 20uF capacitor from node b to node d. Branch current i3 flows through the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i4 flows through the 100mH from node c to node d. Branch current i5 flows from node d to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$]								(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=25~mH, i_=$i_1(t)$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) --++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=20~$\mu$F, i^=$i_2(t)$]	++ (right:5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$, i^=$i_3(t)$]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l^=100~mH, i_=$i_4(t)$]						(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=25~$\mu$F, i_=$i_5(t)$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of both capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C20}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 100)(20 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j79.5775~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C25}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 100)(25 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -63.6620~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of both inductors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L25}} \amp= j(2\pi 100)(0.025)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j15.7080~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L100}} \amp= j(2\pi 100)(0.1)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j62.8319~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Phasor transform both sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 4~\cos{20^\circ}+j4~\sin{20^\circ}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.7588 + j1.3681~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 20~\cos{45^\circ}+j20~\sin{45^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.1421 + j14.1421~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-kclkvl-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and six nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, and seven impedances (50ohm, 20ohm, 100ohm, j15.7ohm, j62.8ohm, -j79.6ohm, and -j63.7ohm). The j15.7ohm and 50ohm connect at node a. The 50ohm, high-potential end of Iac, 20ohm, and -j79.6ohm connect at node b. The 20ohm, high-potential end of Vac, and j62.8ohm connect at node c. The -j79.6ohm, j62.8ohm, and 100ohm connect at node d. The 100ohm and -j63.7ohm connect at node e. The j15.7ohm, -j63.7ohm, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node f. Branch current i1 flows from node a to node f. Branch current i2 flows through the -j79.6ohm impedance from node b to node d. Branch current i3 flows through the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i4 flows through the j62.8ohm from node c to node d. Branch current i5 flows from node d to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$]										(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=j15.7~$\Omega$, i_=$\vec{I_1}$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]													(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) --++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=$-$j79.6~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]	++ (right:5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]						(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l^=j62.8~$\Omega$, i_=$\vec{I_4}$]						(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j63.7~$\Omega$, i_=$\vec{I_5}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 50<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j15.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the <minus />j63.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-kclkvl-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, and five impedances (50+j15.7ohm, 20ohm, -j79.6ohm, j62.8ohm, and 100-j63.7ohm). The 50+j15.7ohm, high-potential end of Iac, 20ohm, and -j79.6ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, high-potential end of Vac, and j62.8ohm connect at node b. The -j79.6ohm, j62.8ohm, and 100-j63.7ohm connect at node c. The 50+j15.7ohm, 100-j63.7ohm, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node d. Branch current I1 flows through the 50+j15.7ohm impedance from node a to node d. Branch current I2 flows through the -j79.6ohm impedance from node a to node c. Branch current I3 flows through the 20ohm impedance from node a to node b. Branch current I4 flows through the j62.8ohm impedance from node b to node c. Branch current I5 flows through the 100-j63.7ohm impedance from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l_=50+j15.7~$\Omega$, i_=$\vec{I_1}$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) --++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=$-$j79.6~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]	++ (right:5.5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=j62.8~$\Omega$, i_=$\vec{I_4}$]					(8,2.5);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [generic, l_=100$-$j63.7~$\Omega$, *-, i_=$\vec{I_5}$]	(8,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit has two linearly independent perfect nodes. One of them connects the 50+j15.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, current source, 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, and <minus />j79.6<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The other connects the <minus />j79.6<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, j62.8<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and 100<minus />j63.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. Perform KCL at both of these nodes.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} + \vec{I_2} + \vec{I_3} \amp= \vec{I_{AC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} + \vec{I_4} - \vec{I_5} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit has three linearly independent perfect loops. One of them includes the 50+j15.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, and voltage source. Another one includes the voltage source, the j62.8<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and the 100<minus />j63.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The third includes the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, j62.8<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and <minus />j79.6<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. Perform KVL around each of these loops. The units used in the equations below are A, V, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-(50+j15.71)\vec{I_1} + (20)\vec{I_3} + \vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>-\vec{V_{AC}} + (j62.83)\vec{I_4} + (100-j63.66)\vec{I_5} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>(-j79.58)\vec{I_2} - (j62.83)\vec{I_4} - (20)\vec{I_3} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The five equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, then <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, and <m>\vec{I_4}</m>, and <m>\vec{I_5}</m>. The right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
							1 				\amp 1 			\amp 1 		\amp 0 			\amp 0			\amp (3.76+j1.37)\\
							0 				\amp 1 			\amp 0 		\amp 1 			\amp -1			\amp 0\\
							-(50+j15.71)	\amp 0			\amp 20		\amp 0			\amp 0			\amp -(14.14+j14.14)\\
							0				\amp 0			\amp 0		\amp (j62.83)	\amp (100-j63.66)	\amp (14.14+j14.14)\\
							0				\amp (-j79.58)	\amp -20	\amp -(j62.83)	\amp 0			\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve for each current by calculating the reduced row echelon form of the matrix.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp= 1.15 + j0.13~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.1616~\textrm{A} \angle 6.4752^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= 0.53 + j0.71~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.8857~\textrm{A} \angle 53.3111^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_3} \amp= 2.08 + j0.53~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.1413~\textrm{A} \angle 14.2440^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_4} \amp= -0.84 - j0.24~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.8713~\textrm{A} \angle -164.0878^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_5} \amp= -0.31 + j0.47~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.5635~\textrm{A} \angle 123.2253^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert each branch current to a time-dependent function.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_1(t) \amp= 1.16~\textrm{A}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 6.48\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_2(t) \amp= 0.886~\textrm{A}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 53.3\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_3(t) \amp= 2.14~\textrm{A}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 14.2\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_4(t) \amp= 0.871~\textrm{A}~\cos{(2\pi100t -164\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_5(t) \amp= 0.564~\textrm{A}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 123\pi/180)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>


				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-meshanalysis">
			<title>Complex Mesh Analysis</title>

			<p>Mesh analysis can also be used in phasor-transformed circuits. KCL and KVL are used to solve for mesh currents. The mesh analysis method steps, outlined in <xref ref="ch2-meshanalysismethod" />, are exactly the same in a phasor-transformed circuit.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex mesh analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate all mesh currents as well as <m>\vec{V_X}</m> in the following circuit. The voltage source has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 330~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 250 t - 50\pi/180)</m>. The proportionality constant of the VCCS has a unit of A/V.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-mesh-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and six nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 2*Vx, three resistors (500ohm, 220ohm, and 680ohm) and three capacitors (470nF, 680nF, and 220nF). The high-potential end of Vac, 680nF, and 500ohm connect at node a. The 500ohm and 470nF connect at node b. The 680nF, VCCS, and 220ohm connect at node c. The VCCS flows into node c. The 470nF, 220ohm, and 680ohm connect at node d. The 680ohm and 220nF connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, VCCS, and 220nF connect at node f. The VCCS flows out of node f. Voltage Vx drops over the 680nF capacitor from node a to node c. Mesh current i1 flows through Vac, 680nF, and the VCCS from node a to node c to node f back to node a. Mesh current i2 flows through the VCCS, 220ohm resistor, 680ohm resistor, and 220nF capacitor from node f to node c to node d to node e back to node f. Mesh current I3 flows through the 500ohm resistor, 470nF capacitor, 220ohm resistor, and 680nF capacitor from node a to node b to node d to node c back to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$, *-]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [C, l^=680~nF, v_=$v_X(t)$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=2$v_X(t)$, *-*]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$, -*]						(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=680~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=220~nF]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) --++ (up:2) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5) to [C, l^=470~nF] ++(right:2.5) --++ (down:2);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.5)node{$i_1(t)$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.5)node{$i_2(t)$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (2.5,6)node{$i_3(t)$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C220}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 250)(220 \times 10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j2893.7~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C470}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 250)(470 \times 10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j1354.5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C680}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 250)(680 \times 10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j936.21~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Phasor transform the source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 330~\cos{-50^\circ} + j330~\sin{-50^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 212.12 - j252.79~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-mesh-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and six nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 2*Vx, and six impedances (500ohm, 220ohm, 680ohm, -j1350ohm, -j936ohm, and -j2890ohm). The high-potential end of Vac, -j936ohm, and 500ohm connect at node a. The 500ohm and -j1350ohm connect at node b. The -j936ohm, VCCS, and 220ohm connect at node c. The VCCS flows into node c. The -j1350ohm, 220ohm, and 680ohm connect at node d. The 680ohm and -j2890ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, VCCS, and -j2890ohm connect at node f. The VCCS flows out of node f. Voltage Vx drops over the -j936ohm capacitor from node a to node c. Mesh current i1 flows through Vac, -j936ohm, and the VCCS from node a to node c to node f back to node a. Mesh current i2 flows through the VCCS, 220ohm resistor, 680ohm resistor, and -j2890ohm capacitor from node f to node c to node d to node e back to node f. Mesh current I3 flows through the 500ohm resistor, -j1350ohm capacitor, 220ohm resistor, and -j936ohm capacitor from node a to node b to node d to node c back to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$, *-]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [C, l^=$-$j936~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V_X}$]			(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=2$\vec{V_X}$, *-*]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$, -*]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=680~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j2890~$\Omega$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) --++ (up:2) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j1350~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5) --++ (down:2);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.5)node{$\vec{I_1}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.5)node{$\vec{I_2}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (2.5,6)node{$\vec{I_3}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 500<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the <minus />j1350<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 680<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the <minus />j2890<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-mesh-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 2*Vx, and four impedances (500-j1350ohm, 220ohm, 680-j2890ohm, and -j936ohm). The high-potential end of Vac, -j936ohm, and 500-j1350ohm connect at node a. The -j936ohm, VCCS, and 220ohm connect at node b. The VCCS flows into node b. The 500-j1350ohm, 220ohm, and 680-j2890ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, VCCS, and 680-j2890ohm connect at node d. The VCCS flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over the -j936ohm capacitor from node a to node b. Mesh current i1 flows through Vac, -j936ohm, and the VCCS from node a to node b to node d back to node a. Mesh current i2 flows through the VCCS, 220ohm resistor, 680-j2890ohm impedance from node d to node b to node c back to node d. Mesh current I3 flows through the 500-j1350ohm impedance, 220ohm resistor, and -j936ohm capacitor from node a to node c to node b back to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$, *-]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j936~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V_X}$]			(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=2$\vec{V_X}$, *-*]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$, -*]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [generic, l^=680$-$j2890~$\Omega$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) --++ (up:2) to [generic, l^=500$-$j1350~$\Omega$]	++(right:5) --++ (down:2);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$\vec{I_1}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$\vec{I_2}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (2.5,5)node{$\vec{I_3}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KVL around the mesh containing mesh current <m>\vec{I_3}</m>. All subsequent equations will use units of A, V, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>(500-j1354.51)\vec{I_3} + (220)(\vec{I_3}-\vec{I_2}) + (-j936.21)(\vec{I_3}-\vec{I_1}) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the supermesh containing mesh currents <m>\vec{I_1}</m> and <m>\vec{I_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>(-j936.21)(\vec{I_1}-\vec{I_3}) + (220)(\vec{I_2}-\vec{I_3}) +(680-j2893.73)\vec{I_2} = (212.12-j252.79)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Relate mesh currents <m>\vec{I_1}</m> and <m>\vec{I_2}</m> to branch current <m>2\vec{V_X}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_1} + 2\vec{V_X} - \vec{I_2} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to derive an equation relating the controlling voltage (<m>\vec{V_X}</m>) to known impedances and mesh currents.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{V_X} = (-j936.21)(\vec{I_1}-\vec{I_3})</md>
					</p>

					<p>The five equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, then <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, and <m>\vec{V_X}</m>. The right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
							(j936.21) 		\amp -220			\amp (720-j2290.72)	\amp 0 		\amp 0\\
							(-j936.21)	 	\amp (900-j2893.73)	\amp -(220-j936.21) \amp 0	 	\amp (212.12-j252.79)\\
							1				\amp -1				\amp 0				\amp 2		\amp 0\\
							(-j936.21)		\amp 0				\amp (j936.21)		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}

						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve for each mesh current as well as the controlling voltage by calculating the reduced row echelon form of the matrix. Note that the unit for current has been converted to mA and the unit for voltage has been converted to mV.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp=  1.3052 + j15.5313~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.5860~\textrm{mA} \angle 85.1964^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= 99.3971 + j42.4725~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 108.0912~\textrm{mA} \angle 23.1371^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_3} \amp= 1.3196 + j15.4789~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.5350~\textrm{mA} \angle 85.1273^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_X} \amp= 49.0460 + j13.4706~\textrm{mV}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50.8622~\textrm{mV} \angle 15.3578^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert each variable to a time-dependent function.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_1(t) \amp= 15.6~\textrm{mA}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 85.2\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_2(t) \amp= 108~\textrm{mA}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 23.1\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>i_3(t) \amp= 15.5~\textrm{mA}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 85.1\pi/180)}</mrow>
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= 50.9~\textrm{mV}~\cos{(2\pi100t + 15.4\pi/180)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-superposition">
			<title>Complex Superposition</title>

			<p>Superposition applies to all linear circuits. If a linear circuit has <m>n</m> independent sources, then <m>n</m> subcircuits can be created, each with one of the independent sources activated and all others deactivated. Add the properties of each of the subcircuits together to find the total value of the property.</p>
			
			<p>To deactivate a voltage source, replace the voltage source with a short circuit (corresponding to 0<nbsp />V). To deactivate a current source, replace the current source with an open circuit (corresponding to 0<nbsp />A). Any dependent sources that exist in the circuit cannot be eliminated and must be present in all subcircuits.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex superposition</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>v(t)</m> in the circuit below. The source values are <m>i_{AC}(t) = 2~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi 60 t)</m> and <m>v_{AC}(t) = 50~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 60 t + 60\pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and six nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, four resistors (12ohm, 20ohm, 15ohm, and 16ohm), one 68uF capacitor, and one 55mH inductor. The high-potential end of Iac connects to the 12ohm and 15ohm at node a. The 12ohm and 55mH connect at node b. The 15ohm and 68uF connect at node c. The 68uF, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node d. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node e. The 55mH, 20ohm, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node f. Voltage v drops over the 20ohm resistor from node d to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]										(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=55~mH, -*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l^=68~$\mu$F]								(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]							(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$v_{AC}(t)$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 60)(68 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j39.0086~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi 60)(0.055)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j20.7345~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert each source into phasor form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 2~\cos{0^\circ} + j2~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 50~\cos{60^\circ} + j50~\sin{60^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 25 + j43.3013~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and six nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, and six impedances (12ohm, 20ohm, 15ohm, 16ohm, j20.7ohm, and -j39ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to the 12ohm and 15ohm at node a. The 12ohm and j20.7ohm connect at node b. The 15ohm and -j39ohm connect at node c. The -j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node d. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node e. The j20.7ohm, 20ohm, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node f. Voltage v drops over the 20ohm resistor from node d to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]										(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j20.7, -*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l^=$-$j39~$\Omega$]						(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]							(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j20.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 15<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the <minus />j38<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, current source Iac, and four impedances (12+j20.7ohm, 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to 12+j20.7ohm and 15-j39ohm at node a. The 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node b. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node c. The 12+j20.7ohm, 20ohm, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node d. Voltage V drops over the 20ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=12+j20.7~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=15$-$j39~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit cannot be simplified any further at this time.</p>
					
					<p>Deactivate the current source and calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>, the contribution to the output voltage provided by the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and four impedances (12+j20.7ohm, 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm). The 12+j20.7ohm and 15-j39ohm connect at node a. The 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node b. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node c. The 12+j20.7ohm, 20ohm, and low-potential end of Vac connect at node d. Voltage V1 drops over the 20ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=12+j20.7~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=15$-$j39~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V_1}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12+j20.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in series with the 15<minus />j39<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and three impedances (27-j18.3ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm). The 27-j18.3ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node a. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node b. The 27-j18.3ohm, 20ohm, and low-potential end of Vac connect at node c. Voltage V1 drops over the 20ohm resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=27$-$j18.3~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V_1}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 27<minus />j18.3<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the 20<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(27 - j18.2741~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{(27 - j18.2741~\Omega)+(20~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12.6070 - j2.8745~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and two impedances (12.6-j2.87ohm and 16ohm). The 12.6-j2.87ohm and 16ohm connect at node a. The 16ohm and high-potential end of Vac connect at node b. The 12.6-j2.87ohm and low-potential end of Vac connect at node c. Voltage V1 drops over the 12.6-j2.87ohm impedance from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=12.6$-$j2.87~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_1}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_1} \amp= (25+j43.30~V)\frac{12.61-j2.87~\Omega}{16~\Omega+(12.61-j2.87~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.5664 + j17.9338~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate the voltage source and calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>, the contribution to the output voltage provided by the current source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Iac, and four impedances (12+j20.7ohm, 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to 12+j20.7ohm and 15-j39ohm at node a. The 15-j39ohm, 20ohm, and 16ohm connect at node b. The 12+j20.7ohm, 20ohm, 16ohm, and low-potential end of Iac connect at node c. Voltage V2 drops over the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=12+j20.7~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=15$-$j39~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V_2}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 16<nbsp />&#8486; and 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(16~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{(16~\Omega)+(20~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.8889~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-superposition-example-figure8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Iac, and three impedances (12+j20.7ohm, 15-j39ohm, 8.89ohm). The high-potential end of Iac connects to 12+j20.7ohm and 15-j39ohm at node a. The 15-j39ohm and 8.89ohm connect at node b. The 12+j20.7ohm, 8.89ohm, and low-potential end of Iac connect at node c. Voltage V2 drops over the 8.89ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=12+j20.7~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=15$-$j39~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=8.89~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_2}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(12 + j20.7345~\Omega)((15 - j39.0086~\Omega)+8.8889~\Omega)}{(12 + j20.7345~\Omega)+((15 - j39.0086~\Omega)+8.8889~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 23.9332 + j12.9449~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>	
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the current through the branch including the 8.89<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= (2~\textrm{A})\frac{23.9332 + j12.9449~\Omega}{23.8889 - j39.0086~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0638 + j1.1880~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_2} \amp= (8.89~\Omega)(0.0638 + j1.1880~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.5674 + j10.5599~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Add together the two voltages to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (13.5664 + j17.9338~\textrm{V}) + (0.5674 + j10.5599~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.1337 + j28.4937~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 31.8065~\textrm{V} \angle 63.6172^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert this value into a time-varying signal.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 31.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t+63.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-sourcetransform">
			<title>Complex Source Transformation</title>

			<p>In a phasor-transformed circuit, source transformation can be used to convert an AC current source into an AC voltage source and vice versa. The transformation process changes the source type and the impedance location, but results in an equivalent circuit. The goal of source transformation is usually to reduce a circuit by changing the position of the source and an impedance. This can lead to further reductions using series and parallel combinations of impedances.</p>
			
			<p>Two equivalent circuits will have identical voltage drops over the load (depicted as <m>\vec{V}</m> in <xref ref="equivalent-AC-circuits" />) as well as identical currents flowing into the load (depicted as <m>\vec{I}</m> in <xref ref="equivalent-AC-circuits" />).</p>

			<figure xml:id="equivalent-AC-circuits">
  				<caption>Both of these circuits are equivalent if they have identical voltage drops over the load (<m>\vec{V}</m>) and current into the load (<m>\vec{I}</m>).</caption>
  				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-equivalent-AC-circuits">
    				<shortdescription>Left: A voltage source Vs connected in series with resistor Rs are connected in series with an unknown load circuit. Right: A current source Vs/Rs connected in parallel with resistor Rs are connected in parallel with an unknown load.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_S}$]		(0,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=$\vec{Z_S}$, i^=$\vec{I}$]			(3,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(3,0);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v_=$\vec{V}$]							(2.5,0);
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (4.5,1.25) {load\\circuit};
							\draw[]	(8,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_S/Z_S}$]	(8,0);
							\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [short]											(10,2.5);
							\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [short, i^=$\vec{I}$]							(11,2.5);
							\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [generic, l_=$\vec{Z_S}$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]	(10,0);
							\draw[]	(8,0) to [short]											(11,0);
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (12.5,1.25) {load\\circuit};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A voltage source with a value of <m>\vec{V_S}</m> in series with an impedance with a value of <m>\vec{Z_S}</m> can be transformed into an equivalent circuit with a current source in parallel with that same impedance. Ohm's law is used to calculate the value of the current source, which is equal to <m>\vec{V_S/Z_S}</m>. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="voltage-source-transformed-current" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="voltage-source-transformed-current">
  				<caption>The transformation of a voltage source in series with an impedance (left) to an equivalent circuit consisting of a current source in parallel with an impedance (right).</caption>
  				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-source-transformed-current">
    				<shortdescription>Left: A voltage source Vs is connected in series with impedance Zs. The low-potential end of Vs is labeled "b" and the disconnected end of Zs is labeled "a." Right: A current source Vs/Zs is connected in parallel with impedance Zs. Node "a" is connected to the source and impedance, with current flowing into "a." Node "b" is connected to the source and resistor, with current flowing out of "b."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_S}$] 		(0,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=$\vec{Z_S}$, -o]					(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_S/Z_S}$]	(5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]										(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [generic, l_=$\vec{Z_S}$, *-*]					(7,0);
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]										(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A current source with a value of <m>\vec{I_S}</m> in parallel with an impedance with a value of <m>\vec{Z_S}</m> can be transformed into an equivalent circuit with a voltage source in series with that same impedance. Ohm's law is used to calculate the value of the voltage source, which is equal to <m>\vec{I_S Z_S}</m>. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="current-source-transformed-voltage" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="current-source-transformed-voltage">
  				<caption>The transformation of a current source in parallel with an impedance (left) to an equivalent circuit consisting of a voltage source in series with an impedance (right).</caption>
  				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-current-source-transformed-voltage">
    				<shortdescription>Left: A current source Is is connected in parallel with impedance Zs. Node "a" is connected to the source and impedance, with current flowing into "a." Node "b" is connected to the source and impedance, with current flowing out of "b." Right: A voltage source IsZs is connected in series with impedance Zs. The low-potential end of IsZs is labeled "b" and the disconnected end of Zs is labeled "a." </shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_S}$]	(0,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]									(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(2,2.5) to [generic, l_=$\vec{Z_S}$, *-*]				(2,0);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_SZ_S}$] (5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l^=$\vec{Z_S}$, -o]				(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]									(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>In either case (converting a voltage source to a current source, or vice versa), the position of the impedance changes but the value of the impedance remains unchanged.</p>

			<p>As noted in <xref ref="ch3-sourcetransformation" />, this process is not limited to independent sources. It is possible to conduct source transformation with any kind of power source used in a linear circuit.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex source transformation</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>v(t)</m>. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 24~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 250t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, two resistors (8ohm and 6ohm), 100uF capacitor, and 3mH inductor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 8ohm at node a. The 8ohm, 100uF, and 3mH connect at node b. The 3mH and 6ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, 100uF, and 6ohm connect at node d. Voltage v drops over the 6ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~$\mu$F, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=3~mH]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 250)(100 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j6.3662~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi 250)(0.003)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j4.7124~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a phasor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 24~\cos{0^\circ} + j24~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and four impedances (8ohm, 6ohm, -j6.37ohm, and j4.71ohm). The high-potential end of Vac connects to 8ohm at node a. The 8ohm, -j6.37ohm, and j4.71ohm connect at node b. The j4.71ohm and 6ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, -j6.37ohm, and 6ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops over the 6ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j6.37~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4.71~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the voltage source to a current source. The value of the current source will be 3<nbsp />A.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of a 3A current source and four impedances (8ohm, 6ohm, -j6.37ohm, and j4.71ohm). The high-potential end of the 3A source, 8ohm, -j6.37ohm, and j4.71ohm connect at node a. The j4.71ohm and 6ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 3A source, 8ohm, -j6.37ohm, and 6ohm connect at node c. Voltage V drops over the 6ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=3~A]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j6.37~$\Omega$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4.71~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 8<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the <minus />j6.37<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(8~\Omega)(-j6.37~\Omega)}{(8~\Omega)+(-j6.37~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.1018 - j3.8979~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 3A current source and three impedances (3.1-j3.9ohm, 6ohm, and j4.71ohm). The high-potential end of the 3A source, 3.1-j3.9ohm, and j4.71ohm connect at node a. The j4.71ohm and 6ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 3A source, 3.1-j3.9ohm, and 6ohm connect at node c. Voltage V drops over the 6ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=3~A]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=3.1$-$j3.9~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4.71~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the current source into a voltage source. Use Ohm's law to calculate the value of the voltage source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V'} \amp= (3~\textrm{A})(3.10-j3.90~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9.3054 - j11.6936~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a voltage source V' and three impedances (3.1-j3.9ohm, 6ohm, and j4.71ohm). The high-potential end of V' and the 3.1-j3.9ohm connect at node a. The 3.1-j3.9ohm and j4.71ohm connect at node b. The j4.71ohm and 6ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of V' and 6ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops over the 6ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V'}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=3.1$-$j3.9~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4.71~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 3.1<minus />j3.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in series with the j4.71<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-source-transform-example-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a voltage source V' and two impedances (3.1+j0.815ohm and 6ohm). The high-potential end of V' and the 3.1+j0.815ohm connect at node a. The 3.1+j0.815ohm and 6ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of V' and 6ohm connect at node c. Voltage V drops over the 6ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V'}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=3.1+j0.815~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (9.3054 - j11.6936~\textrm{V})\frac{6~\Omega}{6~\Omega+(3.1018 + j0.8145~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.4011 - j8.1919~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9.8122~\textrm{V} \angle -56.6020^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert this value into a time-varying signal.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 9.81~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi250t-56.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-theveninnorton">
			<title>Complex Th&#233;venin's and Norton's Theorems</title>

			<p>The phasor-transformed version of Th&#233;venin's theorem states that any linear circuit (regardless of the complexity) can be represented by an equivalent circuit that contains a voltage source in series with an impedance. This is depicted in <xref ref="complex-thevenin-equivalent-circuit" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="complex-thevenin-equivalent-circuit">
  				<caption>Th&#233;venin's theorem states that these two circuits are equivalent, given the correct value of <m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m> and <m>\vec{Z_{TH}}</m>.</caption>
  				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-equivalent-circuit">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a box representing an unknown linear circuit has two output terminals labeled a and b. Right: a voltage source of value Vth is wired in series with an impedance of value Zth. Terminal a is connected to the impedance. Terminal b is connected to the low-potential end of Vth.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (0,1.25) {linear\\circuit};
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$\vec{V_{TH}}$]		(5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l^=$\vec{Z_{TH}}$, -o]	(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage (<m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m>) is equal to the open-circuit voltage between the output terminals. The Th&#233;venin equivalent impedance (<m>\vec{Z_{TH}}</m>) is solved using the procedure described in <xref ref="ch3-thevenin" />, using complex (rather than resistive) circuit analysis techniques.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit for the circuit shown below. The source voltage is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 3~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-thevenin-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 0.3*Vx, two resistors (47ohm and 68ohm), and two capacitors (22uF and 47uF). The high-potential end of Vac connects to 47ohm at node c. The 47ohm, 22uF, and 47uF connect at node d. The 47uF, 68ohm, and VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, 22uF, 68ohm, and VCCS connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage Vx drops over the 22uF capacitor from node d to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=22~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=47~$\mu$F]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l^=0.3$v_X(t)$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]									(9,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=68~$\Omega$, *-*]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(9,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of both capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C22}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi100)(22 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j72.3432~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C47}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi100)(47 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j33.8628~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a phasor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 3~\cos{0^\circ} + j3~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-thevenin-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 0.3*Vx and four impedances (47ohm, 68ohm, -j72.3ohm, and -j33.9ohm). The high-potential end of Vac connects to 47ohm at node c. The 47ohm, -j72.3ohm, and -j33.9ohm connect at node d. The -j33.9ohm, 68ohm, and VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, -j72.3ohm, 68ohm, and VCCS connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage Vx drops over the -j72.3ohm impedance from node d to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j72.3~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V_X}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j33.9~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l^=0.3$\vec{V_X}$, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]										(9,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=68~$\Omega$, *-*]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]											(9,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>KCL can be used to derive two equations to solve for <m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m>, which is equal to the voltage dropped over the 68<nbsp />&#8486; resistor. Units of V, A, and &#8486; will be used in all subsequent equations. First, perform KCL at the node connecting the 47<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, <minus />j72.3<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and <minus />j33.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V_X}}{47} \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j72.3} + \frac{\vec{V_X}-\vec{V_{TH}}}{-j33.9}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{3}{47} \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{47} + \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j72.3} + \frac{\vec{V_X}-\vec{V_{TH}}}{-j33.9}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \vec{V_X} \left(\frac{1}{47} + \frac{1}{-j72.3} + \frac{1}{-j33.9}\right) + \vec{V_{TH}} \left(-\frac{1}{-j33.9}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Next, perform KCL at the node connecting the <minus />j33.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, VCCS, and 68<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_X}-\vec{V_{TH}}}{-j33.9} \amp= 0.3\vec{V_X} + \frac{\vec{V_{TH}}}{68}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j33.9} - \frac{\vec{V_{TH}}}{-j33.9} - 0.3\vec{V_X} - \frac{\vec{V_{TH}}}{68}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \vec{V_X} \left(\frac{1}{-j33.9} - 0.3\right) + \vec{V_{TH}}\left(-\frac{1}{-j33.9} - \frac{1}{68}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V_X}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.0213 + j0.0434	\amp -j0.0295 		\amp 0.0638\\
								-0.3 + j0.0295	 	\amp -0.0147 - j0.0295 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.1853 - j0.0945\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 0.2040 + j1.8893
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage can be converted to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{TH}} \amp= 0.2040 + j1.8893~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.9003~\textrm{V} \angle 83.8388^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>v_{th}(t) \amp= 1.9~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi100t + 83.8\pi/180)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because there is a dependent source, the short-circuit current must be calculated to determine the value of the Th&#233;venin equivalent impedance. The 68<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is shorted and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-thevenin-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, VCCS 0.3*Vx, three impedances (47ohm, -j72.3ohm, and -j33.9ohm), and a short. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 47ohm at node c. The 47ohm, -j72.3ohm, and -j33.9ohm connect at node d. The -j33.9ohm, short, and VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, -j72.3ohm, short, and VCCS connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage Vx drops over the -j72.3ohm impedance from node d to node b. Branch current Isc flows through the short from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j72.3~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V_X}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j33.9~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l^=0.3$\vec{V_X}$, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]												(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$\vec{I_{SC}}$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>KCL can be used to derive two equations to solve for <m>\vec{I_{SC}}</m>. Units of V, A, and &#8486; will be used in all subsequent equations. First, perform KCL at the node connecting the 47<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, <minus />j72.3<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and <minus />j33.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V_X}}{47} \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j72.3} + \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j33.9}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{3}{47} \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{47} + \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j72.3} + \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j33.9}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \vec{V_X} \left(\frac{1}{47} + \frac{1}{-j72.3} + \frac{1}{-j33.9}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Next, perform KCL at the node connecting the <minus />j33.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, VCCS, and short.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j33.9} \amp= 0.3\vec{V_X} + \vec{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}}{-j33.9} - 0.3\vec{V_X} - \vec{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \vec{V_X} \left(\frac{1}{-j33.9} - 0.3\right) + \vec{I_{SC}}\left(-1\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V_X}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_{SC}}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.0213 + j0.0434	\amp 0 		\amp 0.0638\\
								-0.3 + j0.0295	 	\amp -1	 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.5823 - j1.1865\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp -0.1397 + j0.3732
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{TH}} \amp= \frac{0.2040 + j1.8893~\textrm{V}}{-0.1397 + j0.3732~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.2616 - j2.1415~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This is equal to a 4.26<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in series with a capacitor. Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the value of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C \amp= \frac{1}{(2\pi100)(2.1415)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 743.2023~\mu\textrm{F}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit is shown below, where <m>v_{TH}(t)</m> is defined above.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-thevenin-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with voltage source Vth(t) connected in series with a 4.26ohm resistor and 743uF capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{TH}(t)$] 	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4.26~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=743~$\mu$F, -o]						(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
			</example>

			<p>Norton's theorem states that any linear circuit, regardless of the level of complexity, can be represented by an equivalent circuit that contains a current source in parallel with an impedance. This is depicted in <xref ref="complex-norton-equivalent-circuit" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="complex-norton-equivalent-circuit">
  				<caption>Norton's theorem states that these two circuits are equivalent, given the correct value of <m>\vec{I_N}</m> and <m>\vec{Z_N}</m>.</caption>
  				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-equivalent-circuit">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a box representing an unknown linear circuit has two output terminals labeled a and b. Right: a current source of value In is wired in parallel with an impedance of value Zn. Terminal a is connected to the impedance and source (the source flows into terminal a). Terminal b is connected to the impedance and the source (the source flows out of terminal b).</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (0,1.25) {linear\\circuit};
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=$\vec{I_N}$]			(5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=$\vec{Z_N}$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]			(8.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(8.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Norton's theorem is simply a source-transformed version of a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit. However, it is possible to derive a Norton equivalent circuit without first deriving a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit. The Norton equivalent current (<m>\vec{I_N}</m>) is equal to the short-circuit current through the output terminals when they are shorted together. The Norton equivalent impedance (<m>\vec{Z_N}</m>) is solved using the procedure described in <xref ref="ch3-norton" />, using complex (rather than resistive) circuit analysis techniques.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Complex Norton equivalent circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit for the circuit shown below. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 30~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi2000t +120\pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, two resistors (220ohm and 150ohm), one 470uF capacitor, and two inductors (20mH and 10mH). The high-potential end of Vac and 470nF connect at node c. The 470nF, 20mH, and 220ohm connect at node d. The 220ohm and 150ohm connect at node a. The 150ohm and 10mH connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, 20mH, and 10mH connect at node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [C, l^=470~nF]								(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=20~mH, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]						(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$, *-]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=10~mH, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short, -o]								(6,5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi2000)(470\times 10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j169.31~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of both inductors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L10}} \amp= j(2\pi2000)(0.01)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j125.66~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L20}} \amp= j(2\pi2000)(0.02)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j251.33~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a phasor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 30~\cos{120^\circ} + j30~\sin{120^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -15 + j25.9808~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and five impedances (220ohm, 150ohm, -j169ohm, j251ohm, and j126ohm). The high-potential end of Vac and -j169ohm connect at node c. The -j169ohm, j251ohm, and 220ohm connect at node d. The 220ohm and 150ohm connect at node a. The 150ohm and j126ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, j251ohm, and j126ohm connect at node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [C, l^=$-$j169~$\Omega$]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=j251~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j126~$\Omega$, -*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short, -o]									(6,5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 150<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and j126<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac and four impedances (220ohm, 150+j126ohm, -j169ohm, and j251ohm). The high-potential end of Vac and -j169ohm connect at node c. The -j169ohm, j251ohm, and 220ohm connect at node d. The 220ohm and 150+j126ohm connect at node a. The low-potential end of Vac, j251ohm, and 150+j126ohm connect at node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j169~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j251~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=150+j126~$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]										(6,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the Norton equivalent current. The 150+j126<nbsp />&#8486; impedance will be shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, three impedances (220ohm, -j169ohm, and j251ohm), and a short. The high-potential end of Vac and -j169ohm connect at node c. The -j169ohm, j251ohm, and 220ohm connect at node d. The 220ohm and short connect at node a. The low-potential end of Vac, j251ohm, and short connect at node b. Branch current In flows through the short from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j169~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j251~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$\vec{I_N}$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the voltage source into a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I'} \amp= \frac{-15 + j25.9808~\textrm{V}}{-j169.31~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.1534 - j0.0886~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source I' and three impedances (220ohm, -j169ohm, and j251ohm). All circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of I' connects to node a. Branch current In flows through the 220ohm impedance from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I'}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j169~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j251~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$\vec{I_N}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{-j169.31~\Omega}+\frac{1}{j251.33~\Omega}+\frac{1}{220~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 186.47 - j79.067~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I_N}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_N} \amp= (-0.1534 - j0.0886~\textrm{A})\frac{186.47 - j79.067~\Omega}{220~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.1619 - j0.0199~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 163.1276~\textrm{mA} \angle -172.9775^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This quantity can be converted to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i_{n}(t) = 163~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi2000t - 173\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

					<p>There are no dependent sources, so the Norton equivalent impedance can be calculated directly. Deactivate the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of four impedances (220ohm, 150+j126ohm, -j169ohm, and j251ohm). The -j169ohm, j251ohm, and 220ohm connect at node c. The 220ohm and 150+j126ohm connect at node a. The -j169ohm, j251ohm, and 150+j126ohm connect at node b. The Norton equivalent impedance is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j169~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j251~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=150+j126~$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]								(6,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j169<nbsp />&#8486; impedance and j251<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(-j169.31~\Omega)(j251.33~\Omega)}{(-j169.31~\Omega)+(j251.33~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j518.86~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of three impedances (220ohm, 150+j126ohm, and -j519ohm). The -j519ohm and 220ohm connect at node c. The 220ohm and 150+j126ohm connect at node a. The -j519ohm, and 150+j126ohm connect at node b. The Norton equivalent impedance is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l_=$-$j519~$\Omega$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=150+j126~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o]							(3.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
									\node[align=left, anchor=west] at (3.5,1.25) {$\leftarrow\vec{Z_N}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j519<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in series with the 220<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of two impedances (220-j519ohm and 150+j126ohm). Both impedances are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The Norton equivalent impedance is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l_=220$-$j519~$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=150+j126~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]								(3.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
									\node[align=left, anchor=west] at (3.5,1.25) {$\leftarrow\vec{Z_N}$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two impedances are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_N} \amp= \frac{(220 - j518.86~\Omega)(150 + j125.66~\Omega)}{(220 - j518.86~\Omega)+(150 + j125.66~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 192.34 + j68.763~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This is equal to a 192<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in series with an inductor. Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the value of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L \amp= \frac{68.76}{2\pi2000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.472~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Norton equivalent circuit is shown below, where <m>i_{N}(t)</m> is defined above.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-norton-example-figure9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source In(t) connected in parallel with the series combination of a 192ohm resistor and 5.47mH inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_N(t)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short, -o]								(3.5,5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=192~$\Omega$, *-]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=5.47~mH, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

	</section>

<section xml:id="ch9-transferfuncs">
		<title>Transfer Functions</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>A <term>transfer function</term> is defined as the frequency response of a system, described in <xref ref="ch9-eq-transfer-function" />, where <m>j = \sqrt{-1}</m> and <m>\omega</m> is the angular frequency.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-transfer-function">H(j\omega) = \frac{v_{out}(j\omega)}{v_{in}(j\omega)}</md>
			</p>

			<p>The transfer function provides a lot of useful information about the response of a circuit across different frequencies of operation. The <term>magnitude response</term> of the circuit is calculated by taking the absolute value of the transfer function, and is defined in <xref ref="ch9-eq-transfer-function-magnitude" />, where <m>\Re H(j\omega)</m> is equal to the real part of the transfer function and <m>\Im H(j\omega)</m> is equal to the imaginary part of the transfer function. (Note that this equation describes using the Pythagorean theorem on the real and imaginary parts of the transfer function, similar to what we saw in <xref ref="ch8-pythagorean-theorem" />.)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-transfer-function-magnitude">|H(j\omega)| = \sqrt{(\Re H(j\omega))^2 + (\Im H(j\omega))^2}</md>
			</p>

			<p>The magnitude response of the transfer function dictates how the magnitude (amplitude) of the output will vary based on the frequency of the input signal.</p>

			<p>The <term>phase response</term> of the circuit is derived by calculating the angle of the transfer function, and is defined in <xref ref="ch9-eq-transfer-function-phase" />. (Note that this is similar to what we saw in <xref ref="ch8-calculating-phi" />.)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-transfer-function-phase">\angle H(j\omega) = \atantwo{(\Im H(j\omega),\Re H(j\omega))}</md>
			</p>

			<p>The phase response of the transfer function dictates how the phase (angle) of the output will vary based on the frequency of the input signal.</p>

			<p>When the magnitude and phase responses of a transfer function are plotted, it is called a <term>Bode plot</term>.</p>

			<p>The transfer function of a circuit is derived by using a phasor-transformed circuit to calculate the output voltage divided by the input voltage as a function of <m>j\omega</m>. A transfer function is in <term>standard form</term> when the highest-order term of <m>j\omega</m> in the denominator has a coefficient of one. For a first-order circuit, the highest-order <m>j\omega</m> term in the denominator will be <m>j\omega</m>. For a second-order circuit, the highest-order <m>j\omega</m> term in the denominator will be <m>-\omega^2</m>.</p>

			<example xml:id="ch9-lpf-transferfunction-example">
				<title>First-order RC circuit transfer function</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the transfer function, magnitude response, and phase response of the circuit shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-function-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with voltage source Vin, resistor R, and capacitor C, all connected in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]												(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$\frac{1}{j\omega C}$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage divider rule can be used to derive the transfer function of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \left( \frac{\frac{1}{j\omega C}}{R + \frac{1}{j\omega C}} \right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{j\omega RC + 1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{1}{RC}}{j\omega + \frac{1}{RC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the Pythagorean theorem in both the numerator and denominator to derive the magnitude response of the transfer function.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>|H(j\omega)|	\amp= \left| \frac{\frac{1}{RC}}{j\omega + \frac{1}{RC}} \right|</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{|\frac{1}{RC}|}{|j\omega + \frac{1}{RC}|}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{1}{RC}}{\sqrt{\omega^2 + (\frac{1}{RC})^2}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the arctan function to derive the phase response of the transfer function.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\angle H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{\angle (\frac{1}{RC})}{\angle (j\omega + \frac{1}{RC})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0^\circ}{\atantwo{(\omega, \frac{1}{RC})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\atantwo{\left(\omega, \frac{1}{RC}\right)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Transfer function in symbolic form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-function-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes and two meshes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and L connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin connects to R2 and L at node c. Voltage Vout is measured over L from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=L, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This transfer function can be derived using a voltage divider. First, calculate the equivalent impedance of <m>R_2</m> combined in parallel with <m>L</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{j\omega LR_2}{R_2+j\omega L}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply the voltage divider rule and reduce until the transfer function is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_{out}(\omega) \amp= v_{in}(\omega)\left[\frac{\frac{j\omega LR_2}{R_2+j\omega L}}{R_1+\frac{j\omega LR_2}{R_2+j\omega L}}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{\frac{j\omega LR_2}{R_2+j\omega L}}{R_1+\frac{j\omega LR_2}{R_2+j\omega L}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega LR_2}{j\omega LR_1 + R_1R_2+j\omega LR_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega LR_2}{j\omega L(R_1+R_2) + R_1R_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega \frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}}{j\omega + \frac{R_1R_2}{L(R_1+R_2)}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Op-amp transfer function in symbolic form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example" />. Use KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(\omega)}{R_1} \amp= -\frac{v_{out}(\omega)}{R_2} - \frac{v_{out}(\omega)}{\frac{1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -v_{out}(\omega)\left(\frac{1}{R_2}+j\omega C\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= -\frac{1}{R_1}\left(\frac{1}{\frac{1}{R_2}+j\omega C}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{R_2}{R_1}\left(\frac{1}{1+j\omega CR_2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{R_2}{R_1} \left(\frac{\frac{1}{R_2C}}{j\omega + \frac{1}{R_2C}}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Op-amp transfer function in symbolic form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the transfer function of the circuit shown in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example" />. Use KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(\omega)}{R_1+\frac{1}{j\omega C}} \amp= -\frac{v_{out}(\omega)}{R_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= -\frac{R_2}{R_1+\frac{1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{j\omega CR_2}{j\omega CR_1+1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{R_2}{R_1} \left(\frac{j\omega}{j\omega + \frac{1}{R_1C}}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Transfer function in numeric form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown below. The circuit parameters are <m>L = 20~\textrm{mH}</m>, <m>R = 800~\Omega</m>, and <m>C = 200~\textrm{nF}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-function-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, inductor L, resistor R, and capacitor C, all connected in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the resistor and capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [L, l^=$L$]									(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=$R$]									(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=$C$]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.5,4) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]					(3.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule and reduce until the transfer function is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_{out}(\omega) \amp= v_{in}(\omega)\left[\frac{R+\frac{1}{j\omega C}}{j\omega L+R+\frac{1}{j\omega C}}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{j\omega CR + 1}{-\omega^2LC + j\omega CR + 1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega\frac{R}{L}+\frac{1}{LC}}{-\omega^2+j\omega\frac{R}{L}+\frac{1}{LC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega40000+2.5\times10^8}{-\omega^2+j\omega40000+2.5\times10^8}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The bode plot for this circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-function-example3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Top: magnitude showing an LPF response. Bottom: phase response showing decrease in phase from 0 degrees to -90 degrees.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$\omega$~(rad/s), xmin=1000, xmax=1000000]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$|H(j\omega)|$, ymode=log, xmode=log]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1000:1000000, samples=100]	{sqrt(40000^2*x^2+(2.5E8)^2)/sqrt((2.5E8-x^2)^2+(40000^2*x^2))};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$\angle H(j\omega)$~($^\circ$), xmode=log, ytick={-90,-45,0}]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1000:15811, samples=100]	{atan(x*40000/2.5E8)-atan(x*40000/(2.5E8-x^2))};
											\addplot[thick, domain=15820:1000000, samples=100]	{atan(x*40000/2.5E8)-atan(x*40000/(2.5E8-x^2))-180};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-transferdiffeq">
			<title>Relating Transfer Functions and Differential Equations</title>

			<p>After close observation, it can be seen that a transfer function contains exactly the same information as the differential equation that describes a circuit. This will be demonstrated using the circuit we have previously analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-lpf-transferfunction-example" />. (For extra convincing, try it with other circuits we've analyzed in this textbook.)</p>
			
			<p>Consider the circuit analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-lpf-transferfunction-example" />. KCL can be used to derive the differential equation, which is</p>
			
			<p>
				<md>\frac{1}{RC}v_{in}(t) = \frac{d}{dt}v_{out}(t) + \frac{1}{RC}v_{out}(t)</md>.
			</p>

			<p>Replace every <m>d/dt</m> term with <m>j\omega</m> (we learned that time differentiation is equal to multiplication by <m>j\omega</m> in <xref ref="ch8-phasorcalculus" />). Replace every <m>t</m> term with <m>\omega</m>. Then, solve for <m>v_{out}/v_{in}</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<md>\frac{v_{out}(\omega)}{v_{in}(\omega)} = \frac{\frac{1}{RC}}{j\omega + \frac{1}{RC}}</md>
			</p>

			<proof>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{RC}v_{in}(t) \amp= j\omega v_{out}(t) + \frac{1}{RC}v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{RC}v_{in}(\omega) \amp= v_{out}(\omega) \left( j\omega + \frac{1}{RC} \right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v_{out}(\omega)}{v_{in}(\omega)} \amp= \frac{\frac{1}{RC}}{j\omega + \frac{1}{RC}}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Note that this is exactly the same as the transfer function derived in <xref ref="ch9-lpf-transferfunction-example" />!</p>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-funcpolezero">
			<title>Understanding Transfer Function Poles and Zeros</title>

			<p>Consider the numerator and denominator of the transfer function to consist of polynomials expressed with a variable of <m>j\omega</m>. <term>Poles</term> are the roots of the denominator of the transfer function; <term>zeros</term> are the roots of the numerator of the transfer function.</p>

			<p>A transfer function can be re-written as shown in <xref ref="ch9-eq-poles-zeros-function" />, where each of the <m>z</m> values is a zero, and each of the <m>p</m> values is a pole.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-eq-poles-zeros-function">H(j\omega) = \frac{(j\omega-z_1)(j\omega-z_2)...(j\omega-z_n)}{(j\omega-p_1)(j\omega-p_2)...(j\omega-p_n)}</md>
			</p>

			<p>Identifying the poles and zeros of a transfer function can provide information about the stability of a system. Stability relates to what happens after a system is disturbed from a condition of equilibrium. If, after a disturbance, a system tends to return to equilibrium, that system is said to be stable. If, after a disturbance, the system remains in the new state without changing, that system is said to be marginally stable. If, after a disturbance, the system diverges away from equilibrium, that system is said to be unstable.</p>
			
			<p>Resistive circuits (such as the ones described in this textbook) will be stable due to the damping property of resistors. This causes all exponential terms in output voltage equations to have negative exponents, causing them to decay over time. All poles in these circuits have negative real values. The greater the magnitude of the real part of the pole, the faster the exponential term will decay.</p>
			
			<p>Homogeneous circuits will have a number of poles equal to the order of the system, and these correspond to the general solution of the circuit. (In other words, a homogeneous second-order circuit will have two poles.) Non-homogeneous circuits may have additional poles. Poles relating to the general solution of the differential equation describe the transient response of the circuit. If the two poles have real and distinct negative values, then the system will have an overdamped transient response. If the two poles have real and repeated negative values, then the system will have a critically damped transient response. If the two poles are complex conjugate pairs, then the system will have an underdamped transient response.</p>
			
			<example>
				<title>Relating poles to the transient response (overdamped)</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>A second-order circuit has a transfer function as described below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega+6}{-\omega^2+j\omega3+2}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit has one zero at <minus />6 (i.e. the numerator will be equal to zero when <m>j\omega = -6</m>).</p>
					
					<p>The circuit has two poles at <minus />2 and <minus />1 (i.e. the denominator will be equal to zero when <m>j\omega=-2</m> or when <m>j\omega=-1</m>).</p>
					
					<p>The transfer function can be rewritten.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{(j\omega+6)}{(j\omega+2)(j\omega+1)}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the poles are real and distinct, the transient response of this circuit will be overdamped.</p>

					<p>The transient step response of the circuit is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-poles-zeros-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Overdamped transient step response.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(s), xmin=-2, xmax=10, ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:10, samples=100] {3+2*exp(-2*x)-5*exp(-x)};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Relating poles to the transient response (underdamped)</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>A second-order circuit has a transfer function as described below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{10000}{-\omega^2+j\omega50+10000}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit has no zeros. The circuit has two complex-conjugate poles: one at <minus />25+j96.8 and the other at <minus />25<minus />j96.8. The transfer function can be rewritten.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{10000}{(j\omega+25+j\sqrt{9375})(j\omega+25-j\sqrt{9375})}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the poles are complex conjugate pairs, the transient response of this circuit will be underdamped.</p>
					
					<p>The transient step response of the circuit is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-poles-zeros-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Underdamped transient step response.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-20, xmax=250, ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-20,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:250, samples=200] {1+exp(-0.025*x)*(-cos(5.548*x)+0.258*sin(5.548*x))};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Relating poles to the transient response (critically damped)</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>A second-order circuit has a transfer function as described below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{1\times10^6}{-\omega^2+j\omega2000+1\times10^6}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit has no zeros. The circuit has two repeated poles at <minus />1000. The transfer function can be rewritten.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{1\times10^6}{(j\omega+1000)(j\omega+1000)}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the poles are real and repeated, the transient response of this circuit will be critically damped.</p>
					
					<p>The transient step response of the circuit is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-poles-zeros-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Critically damped transient step response.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-2, xmax=10, ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:10, samples=100] {1-exp(-x)-x*exp(-x))};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>


			<example>
				<title></title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Consider the circuit analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-bpf-transient-analysis" />. The transfer function of the circuit is defined below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega416.6667}{-\omega^2 + j\omega750 + 52083.33}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit has one zero at 0. The circuit has two poles, one at <minus />673 and the other at <minus />77.4.</p>
					
					<p>The transfer function can be rewritten.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{(j\omega416.6667-0)}{(j\omega+673)(j\omega +77.4)}</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<p>Poles and zeros can be plotted on the complex plane. Each pole is depicted with an <m>\times</m> symbol, and each zero is depicted with a <m>\circ</m> symbol. Based on the location of the poles in the complex plane, the stability of the system can be quickly determined. Pole-zero diagrams for stable second-order systems are depicted in <xref ref="stable-pole-zeros" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="stable-pole-zeros">
  				<caption>Distinct poles lead to an underdamped response (left). Repeated poles lead to a critically damped response (middle). Complex conjugate poles lead to an underdamped response (right). All three of these pole-zero diagrams represent a stable system.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-stable-pole-zeros">
					<shortdescription>Left: real distinct poles. Middle: real repeated poles. Right: complex-conjugate poles.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\fill[gray!20] (-2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (-0.5,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-1.5,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-1,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(5.5,0)}]
							\fill[gray!20] (-2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (-1.5,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-1.55,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-1,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(5.5,0)}]
							\fill[gray!20] (-2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (-1,1.5) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-1,-1.5) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (0,0) {$\circ$};
							\end{scope}
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Unstable systems have increasing exponential functions in the output response. In the real physical world, this output response is not tenable. Not only is there only a finite amount of energy (which means that no output, whether it be voltage, current, or a non-electrical property, can increase infinitely), but any physical system will eventually break once the output response reaches a certain value.</p>
			
			<p>An unstable second-order system with distinct or repeated real roots is depicted in <xref ref="unstable-poles-real-roots" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="unstable-poles-real-roots">
  				<caption>Poles with positive real parts (whether they are distinct, as shown, or repeated, not shown) lead to an unstable response (left). The system output consists of increasing exponentials (right).</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-unstable-poles-real-roots">
					<shortdescription>Left: poles are positive, real and unique. Right: exponential growth in the transient response.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\fill[gray!20] (2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (0.5,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (1.5,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (1,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-1.75)}]
							\begin{axis}[width=10cm, height=5cm, yticklabels={,,}, xticklabels={,,}]
								\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,-1) (0,-1)};
								\addplot[thick, domain=0:2, samples=500] {-2*exp(x)+exp(2*x)};
							\end{axis}
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>An unstable system with complex conjugate roots (with positive real parts) has an output response of an increasing amplitude sinusoid. This is depicted in <xref ref="unstable-poles-cplx-roots" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="unstable-poles-cplx-roots">
  				<caption>Poles that are complex conjugates with positive real parts lead to an unstable response (left). The system output consists of an increasing amplitude sinusoid (right).</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-unstable-poles-cplx-roots">
					<shortdescription>Left: poles are complex-conjugate pairs with positive real parts. Right: sinusoidal exponential growth in the transient response.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\fill[gray!20] (2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (1,1) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (1,-1) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (1.5,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-1.75)}]
							\begin{axis}[width=10cm, height=5cm, yticklabels={,,}, xticklabels={,,}]
								\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
								\addplot[thick, domain=0:2, samples=500] {exp(x)*(sin(deg(20*x)))};
							\end{axis}
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Marginally stable systems have constant amplitude functions in the output response. A second-order system with one or more poles of zero is depicted in <xref ref="marginal-poles-real-roots" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="marginal-poles-real-roots">
  				<caption>Poles equal to zero lead to a marginally stable response (left). The system output consists of a constant value (right).</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-marginal-poles-real-roots">
					<shortdescription>Left: poles are zero. Right: constant value for the transient response.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\fill[gray!20] (2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (0,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (0.05,0) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (-0.5,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-1.75)}]
							\begin{axis}[width=10cm, height=5cm, yticklabels={,,}, xticklabels={,,}]
								\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (1,0)};
							\end{axis}
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A marginally stable system with complex conjugate pair poles (with no real part) has a constant amplitude sinusoid as a response. This would be the case for an ideal LC circuit with no parasitic resistance. This type of output response is depicted in <xref ref="marginal-poles-cplx-roots" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="marginal-poles-cplx-roots">
  				<caption>Poles that are complex conjugates with zero real parts lead to a marginally stable response (left). The system output consists of a constant amplitude sinusoid (right).</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-marginal-poles-cplx-roots">
					<shortdescription>Left: poles are complex conjugate pairs with zero real part. Right: constant sinusoidal signal in the transient response.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\fill[gray!20] (2,-2) rectangle (0,2);
							\draw[step=0.5, help lines, color=gray!50] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
							\draw[] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {real};
							\draw[] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above] {imaginary};
							\node[align=center] at (0,1.5) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (0,-1.5) {$\times$};
							\node[align=center] at (0,0) {$\circ$};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(4,-1.75)}]
							\begin{axis}[width=10cm, height=5cm, yticklabels={,,}, xticklabels={,,}]
								\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
								\addplot[thick, domain=0:2, samples=500] {sin(deg(20*x))};
							\end{axis}
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>
	
  		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch9-passivefilters">
		<title>Passive Filters</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>A <term>filter</term> is a circuit that selectively blocks certain frequencies (or a range of frequencies) from passing through to the output. Filters can be passive (made of passive components only) or active (made with a combination of passive and active components, mostly commonly op-amps). Active filters will be discussed in <xref ref="ch9-activefilters" />. </p> 
			
			<p>Filters are defined by their frequency response. The four types of frequency responses are low-pass, high-pass, band-pass, and band-stop.</p>

			<p>The <term>cutoff frequency</term> (<m>\omega_c</m>) of a filter defines the frequency at which input signals become modified by the circuit and either start to become blocked from or start to become passed to the output.</p>

			<p>Filters are additionally defined by the circuit order. <term>First-order filters</term> consist of first-order circuits (only one energy storage device is included in the design). <term>Second-order filters</term> consist of second-order circuits (two energy storage devices are included in the design).</p>
			
			<p>The cutoff frequency of a first-order filter is defined by <xref ref="ch9-cutoff-frequency-first-order" />, where <m>\tau</m> was defined in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" />. The unit of the cutoff frequency is rad/s.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-cutoff-frequency-first-order">\omega_c = \frac{1}{\tau}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of cutoff frequency</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\omega_c</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: rad/s (radians per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>The cutoff frequency of a second-order filter is defined as the frequency where the magnitude of the transfer function of the filter is equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m>, as described in <xref ref="ch9-cutoff-frequency-second-order" />. For band-pass and band-stop filters, there will be two cutoff frequencies, denoted as <m>\omega_{c1}</m> and <m>\omega_{c2}</m>.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-cutoff-frequency-second-order">H(\omega_c) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</md>
			</p>

			<p>A second-order filter is also characterized by a quantity known as the <term>quality factor</term>, <m>Q</m> (sometimes known as q-factor), which defines the <q>narrowness</q> of the filter response at the resonant frequency. A high quality factor means that the filter has a very narrow response (and vice versa). The quality factor of a second-order circuit is defined in <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" />, where <m>\omega_0</m> is the resonant frequency of the filter, and where <m>a_1</m> is one of the coefficients defined in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode" /> for a second-order differential equation <alert>in standard form</alert>.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-quality-factor">Q = \frac{\omega_0}{a_1}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of quality factor</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>Q</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: none</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>Because the properties of a filter are dictated by the transfer function (in standard form) of the circuit, analysis of a filter will typically require deriving the corresponding transfer function. The process of deriving these equations will not be worked through in as much detail as it was in <xref ref="ch9-transferfuncs" />.</p>
			
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-lpfs">
			<title>Low-Pass Filters (LPF)</title>

			<p>A <term>low-pass filter</term> blocks high frequency signals (signals with a frequency above the cutoff frequency) from passing through to the output. (Only low frequencies can pass through, hence the name <q>low-pass filter.</q>) A common use of low-pass filters is to remove high frequency noise from a circuit's output.</p>

			<p>As the frequency of the input source changes, the magnitude of the output voltage will change. This can be characterized by calculating <m>v_{out}/v_{in}</m> at varying frequencies and looking at the response. An ideal low-pass filter has what's called a <term><q>brick wall</q> response</term>: all frequencies above the cutoff frequency are completely eliminated from the output, and all frequencies below the cutoff frequency are able to pass through with no change in magnitude.</p>
			
			<p>The output response of a low-pass filter is shown in <xref ref="lpf-magnitude-response" />. (Note the use of the log/log scale. In addition, note that the x-axis is normalized to the cutoff frequency.) The ideal brick wall response is depicted with a dashed line. The real response expected from a low-pass filter is depicted with a thick black line.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="lpf-magnitude-response">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between an ideal (<q>brick wall</q>) low-pass filter and a real low-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-lpf-magnitude-response">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Dashed line has magnitude of 1 until w/wc=1, then goes to zero. Thick line is 1 until around w/wc=1, then gradually decays.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, ymax=2, ymin=0.001, legend pos=south west, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{1/(sqrt(x^2+1))};
								\addlegendentry{real LPF}
								\addplot[thick, dashed] coordinates {(0.01,1) (1,1) (1,0.001)};
								\addlegendentry{ideal LPF}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A second-order low-pass filter will cause a much faster decay of the output signal compared to a first-order low-pass filter. A comparison of waveforms for a first and second-order low-pass filter (with identical cutoff frequencies) is shown in <xref ref="lpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="lpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between a first-order low-pass filter and a second-order low-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-lpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Black line is 1 until around w/wc=1, then gradually decays. Gray line is 1 until around w/wc=1, then more quickly decays.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, legend pos=south west, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{1/(sqrt(x^2+1))};
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{1/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+x^2))};
								\legend{first-order filter, second-order filter}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example>
				<title>First-order low-pass filter circuits</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>One example of a first-order passive low-pass filter is shown below. The capacitor, having a low impedance at high frequencies leads to a decreasing voltage drop over the output as the input frequency increases.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-first-order-lpf-rc">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, and capacitor C, all wired in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[] (0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$] 									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>It is also possible to use a resistor-inductor combination to create a first-order low-pass filter. This circuit, shown below, acts as a low-pass filter because the inductor acts as an open at high frequencies, blocking current through the output resistor when the input frequency becomes too high.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-first-order-lpf-rl">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, and inductor L, all wired in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the resistor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$] 	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Low-pass filter analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Analyze the following low-pass filter. Calculate the cutoff frequency and quality factor of the filter. The circuit parameters are <m>R=500~\Omega</m>, <m>L=1~\textrm{H}</m>, and <m>C=100~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-lpf-second-order-example-figure">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, inductor L, resistor R, and capacitor C. Vin and L connect at node a. L, R, and C connect at node b. Vin, R, and C connect at node c. Vout is measured over the capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_{out}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The filter is a second-order low-pass filter. This is because the circuit contains two separate energy-storage elements.</p>
					
					<p>The second-order differential equation will provide information about the properties of the circuit. Perform KCL at the node connecting the inductor, resistor, and capacitor. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{L}\int(v_{in}(t) - v_{out}(t)) \,dt \amp= \frac{v_{out}(t)}{R} + C\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{L}(v_{in}(t) - v_{out}(t))  \amp= \frac{1}{R}\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + C\frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{L}v_{in}(t) \amp= C\frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{R}\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{L}v_{out}(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{LC}v_{in}(t) \amp= \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{RC}\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The transfer function of the circuit is therefore equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{\frac{1}{LC}}{-\omega^2 + \frac{1}{RC}j\omega + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{10000}{-\omega^2 + 20j\omega + 10000}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{10000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor of the filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{100}{20}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Set <m>|H(j\omega)|</m> equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m> and solve for <m>\omega</m> to calculate the cutoff frequency of the circuit.</p>
			
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>|H(j\omega_c)| = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp= \frac{a_0}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>2a_0^2 \amp= (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2\omega_c^2 + (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Let <m>x = \omega_c^2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>2a_0^2 \amp= a_1^2x + (a_0-x)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2x + a_0^2 - 2a_0x + x^2</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= x^2 + (a_1^2 - 2a_0)x - a_0^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= x^2 - 19600x - (10000)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= 154~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Bode plot for this filter is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-lpf-example1-bode">
							<shortdescription>Top: magnitude response showing LPF behavior. Bottom: phase response with the phase decreasing from 0 degrees to -180 degrees.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$\omega$~(rad/s), xmin=10, xmax=1000]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$|H(j\omega)|$, ymode=log, xmode=log]
											\addplot[thick, domain=10:1000, samples=400]	{10000/sqrt((10000-x^2)^2+(20^2))};

										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$\angle H(j\omega)$~($^\circ$), xmode=log, ytick={0,-90,-180}]
											\addplot[thick, domain=10:100, samples=100]	{-atan(400/(10000-x^2))};
											\addplot[thick, domain=100.001:1000, samples=100]	{-atan(400/(10000-x^2))-180};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-hpfs">
			<title>High-Pass Filters (HPF)</title>

			<p>A <term>high-pass filter</term> blocks low frequency signals (signals with a frequency below the cutoff frequency) from passing through to the output. (Only high frequencies can pass through, hence the name <q>high-pass filter.</q>)</p>

			<p>As the frequency of the input source changes, the magnitude of the output voltage will change. This can be characterized by calculating <m>v_{out}/v_{in}</m> at varying frequencies and looking at the response. An ideal high-pass filter has an ideal brick wall response where all frequencies below the cutoff frequency are completely eliminated from the output, and all frequencies above the cutoff frequency are able to pass through with no change in magnitude.</p>
			
			<p>The output response of a high-pass filter is shown in <xref ref="hpf-magnitude-response" />. (Note the use of the log/log scale. In addition, note that the x-axis is normalized to the cutoff frequency.) The ideal brick wall response is depicted with a dashed line. The real response expected from a high-pass filter is depicted with a thick black line.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="hpf-magnitude-response">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between an ideal (<q>brick wall</q>) high-pass filter and a real high-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-hpf-magnitude-response">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Dashed line has magnitude of zero until w/wc=1, then goes to one. Thick line gradually rises until around w/wc=1, then is equal to one.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, ymax=2, ymin=0.001, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{x/(sqrt(x^2+1))};
								\addlegendentry{real HPF}
								\addplot[thick, dashed] coordinates {(1,0.001) (1,1) (100,1)};
								\addlegendentry{ideal HPF}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A second-order high-pass filter will cause a much faster rise of the output signal compared to a first-order high-pass filter. A comparison of waveforms for a first and second-order high-pass filter (with identical cutoff frequencies) is shown in <xref ref="hpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="hpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between a first-order high-pass filter and a second-order high-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-hpf-magnitude-response-first-and-second">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Black line rises until around w/wc=1, then is 1. Gray line rises faster until around w/wc=1, then is 1.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{x/(sqrt(x^2+1))};
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{x^2/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+x^2))};
								\legend{first-order filter, second-order filter}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example>
				<title>First-order high-pass filters</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>One example of a first-order passive high-pass filter is shown below. The capacitor, having a high impedance at low frequencies, blocks current from passing through the load resistor at low frequencies. The output voltage becomes larger as the frequency increases and the capacitor transitions from an open to a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-first-order-hpf-rc">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, and capacitor C, all wired in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the resistor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>It is also possible to use a resistor-inductor combination to create a first-order high-pass filter. This circuit, shown below, acts as a high-pass filter because the inductor acts as a short at low frequencies, creating leading to a small voltage drop over the load.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-first-order-hpf-rl">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, and inductor L, all wired in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>High-pass filter analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Analyze the following high-pass filter. Calculate the cutoff frequency and quality factor of the filter. The circuit parameters are <m>C = 200~\textrm{nF}</m>, <m>R_1 = 500~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 400~\Omega</m>, and <m>L = 10~\textrm{mH}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-hpf-analysis-example">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, capacitor C, inductor L, and resistors R1 and R2. Vin and C connect at node a. C, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and L connect at node c. Vin, R1, and L connect at node d. Voltage Vout is measured over L.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The second-order differential equation describing the output voltage is shown below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{d^2v_{in}(t)}{dt^2} = \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{CR_1} + \frac{R_2}{L}\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_2}{R_1LC} + \frac{1}{LC}\right]v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The transfer function of the circuit is therefore equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + \left[\frac{1}{CR_1} + \frac{R_2}{L}\right]j\omega + \left[\frac{R_2}{R_1LC} + \frac{1}{LC}\right]}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>

					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + 50000j\omega + 900000000}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{900000000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{30000}{50000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.6</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Set <m>|H(j\omega)|</m> equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m> and solve for <m>\omega</m> to calculate the cutoff frequency of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>|H(j\omega_c)| = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp= \frac{\omega_c^2}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>2(\omega_c^2)^2 \amp= (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2\omega_c^2 + (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Let <m>x = \omega_c^2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>2x^2 \amp= a_1^2x + (a_0-x)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2x + a_0^2 - 2a_0x + x^2</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= x^2 + (2a_0 - a_1^2)x - a_0^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= x^2 -700000000x - (900000000)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= 36300~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-bpf-and-bsf">
			<title>Band-Pass and Band-Stop Filters</title>

			<p>Before going into the details of each individual filter in <xref ref="ch9-bpfs" /> and <xref ref="ch9-bsfs" />, it is useful to introduce, describe, and derive aspects that are common to both of these two filter types.</p>
		
			<p>Band-pass filters and band-stop filters are defined by their ability to pass or stop a given range of frequencies from the output, respectively. The width of the pass-band or stop-band is defined by the two cutoff frequencies (<m>\omega_{c1}</m> and <m>\omega_{c2}</m>), which are the two frequencies where the magnitude of the transfer function is equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m>. The bandwidth (<m>\beta</m>) of a band-pass filter or band-stop filter is therefore defined by <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-wc1-wc2" />. (Note that while bandwidth shares a symbol with the oscillation frequency of a second-order circuit, the two quantities are not the same!)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-bandwidth-wc1-wc2">\beta = \omega_{c2}-\omega_{c1}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of bandwidth</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\beta</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: rad/s (radians per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>The bandwidth for an RLC band-pass filter and RLC band-stop filter can be calculated using their respective transfer functions. This equation is defined in <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-a1" />, and derived in <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-bpf" /> for an RLC band-pass filter and in <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-bsf" /> for an RLC band-stop filter.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-bandwidth-a1">\beta = a_1</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch9-bandwidth-bpf">
				<p>The transfer function of an RLC band-pass filter will take the form shown below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{a_1 j\omega}{-\omega^2 + a_1 j\omega + a_0}</md>
				</p>

				<p>The magnitude of this transfer function is shown below.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>|H(j\omega)| = \frac{(a_1 \omega)}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}}</md>
				</p>

				<p>Set the magnitude of the transfer function equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m> and solve for the two values of <m>\omega</m> that are positive. These will describe the two cutoff frequencies <m>\omega_{c1}</m> and <m>\omega_{c2}</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp= \frac{a_1 \omega}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{2} \amp= \frac{(a_1 \omega)^2}{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}</mrow>
						<mrow>2(a_1 \omega )^2 \amp= (a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2</mrow>
						<mrow>(a_0 - \omega^2)^2 \amp= (a_1 \omega)^2</mrow>
						<mrow>0 \amp= a_0^2 - 2a_0\omega^2 + \omega^4 - a_1^2 \omega^2 </mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \omega^4 - (2a_0 + a_1^2) \omega^2 + a_0^2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The two positive real roots are shown below. These will not be derived in this book.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\omega_{c1} \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} - a_1\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\omega_{c2} \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} + a_1\right)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Subtract <m>\omega_{c1}</m> from <m>\omega_{c2}</m> to calculate the bandwidth.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\beta \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} + a_1\right) - \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} - a_1\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}a_1 + \frac{1}{2}a_1</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<proof xml:id="ch9-bandwidth-bsf">
				<p>The transfer function of an RLC band-stop filter will take the form shown below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + a_0}{-\omega^2 + a_1 j\omega + a_0}</md>
				</p>

				<p>The magnitude of this transfer function is shown below.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>|H(j\omega)| = \frac{(a_0 - \omega^2)}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}}</md>
				</p>

				<p>Set the magnitude of the transfer function equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m> and solve for the two values of <m>\omega</m> that are positive. These will describe the two cutoff frequencies <m>\omega_{c1}</m> and <m>\omega_{c2}</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp= \frac{(a_0 - \omega^2)}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{2} \amp= \frac{(a_0 - \omega^2)^2}{(a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2}</mrow>
						<mrow>2(a_0 - \omega^2)^2 \amp= (a_0-\omega^2)^2 + (a_1 \omega)^2</mrow>
						<mrow>(a_0 - \omega^2)^2 \amp= (a_1 \omega)^2</mrow>
						<mrow>0 \amp= a_0^2 - 2a_0\omega^2 + \omega^4 - a_1^2 \omega^2 </mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \omega^4 - (2a_0 + a_1^2) \omega^2 + a_0^2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The two positive real roots are shown below. These will not be derived in this book.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\omega_{c1} \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} - a_1\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\omega_{c2} \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} + a_1\right)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Subtract <m>\omega_{c1}</m> from <m>\omega_{c2}</m> to calculate the bandwidth.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\beta \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} + a_1\right) - \frac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt{a_1^2+4a_0} - a_1\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}a_1 + \frac{1}{2}a_1</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Band-pass and band-stop filters, as mentioned, have two cutoff frequencies straddling a <term>center frequency</term>. The center frequency of a band-pass or band-stop filter is equal to the resonant frequency of the circuit, as defined in <xref ref="ch9-center-frequency-bsf-bpf" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-center-frequency-bsf-bpf">\omega_c = \omega_0</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of center frequency</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\omega_c</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: rad/s (radians per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-bpfs">
			<title>Band-Pass Filters (BPF)</title>

			<p>A <term>band-pass filter</term> only allows a range of frequencies to pass through to the output. Anything below a certain frequency, or above a certain frequency, is blocked from reaching the output. The range of frequencies that are allowed to pass through to the output is known as the <term>pass-band</term> of the circuit. The width of the pass-band is defined by the bandwidth of the circuit. <alert>Band-pass filters cannot be first-order circuits; they must be second-order or higher.</alert></p>
			
			<p>As the frequency of the input source changes, the magnitude of the output voltage will change. This can be characterized by calculating <m>H(j\omega)</m> at varying frequencies and looking at the response. An ideal band-pass filter has an ideal brick wall response where all frequencies outside the pass-band are completely eliminated from the output, and all frequencies inside the pass-band are able to pass through with no change in magnitude.</p>
			
			<p>The output response of a band-pass filter is shown in <xref ref="bpf-magnitude-response" />. (Note the use of the log/log scale. In addition, note that the x-axis is normalized to the cutoff frequency.) The ideal brick wall response is depicted with a dashed line. The real response expected from a band-pass filter is depicted with a thick black line.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="bpf-magnitude-response">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between an ideal (<q>brick wall</q>) band-pass filter and a real band-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-bpf-magnitude-response">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Dashed line has magnitude of 0 until wc1, magnitude of 1 until wc2, then magnitude of 0. Thick line gradually rises to w/wc=1, then gradually decays.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, ymax=2, ymin=0.001, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{x/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+x^2))};
								\addlegendentry{real BPF}
								\addplot [thick, dashed]	coordinates {(0.618, 0.001) (0.618, 1) (1.618, 1) (1.618, 0.001)};
								\addlegendentry{ideal BPF}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Band-pass filters are characterized by their quality factor. A filter with a high quality factor will have a very narrow pass-band (low bandwidth), and vice versa. A graph showing the output response of band-pass filters with different quality factors is shown in <xref ref="bpf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="bpf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between a low q-factor band-pass filter and a high q-factor band-pass filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-bpf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Black line gradually rises to w/wc=1, then gradually decays. Gray line quickly rises to w/wc=1, then quickly decays.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:100, samples=100]	{x/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+x^2))};
								\addlegendentry{low q-factor}
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=0.01:0.9, samples=50]	{(x/100)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/100)^2))};
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=0.9:1.1, samples=200]	{(x/100)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/100)^2))};
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=1.1:100, samples=50]	{(x/100)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/100)^2))};
								\addlegendentry{high q-factor}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example xml:id="ch9-bpf-transient-analysis">
				<title>Band-pass filter analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Analyze the following band-pass filter. Calculate the center frequency and the quality factor of the circuit. The circuit parameters are <m>C_1 = 40~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, <m>C_2 = 20~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, <m>R_1 = 200~\Omega</m>, and <m>R_2 = 120~\Omega</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-bpf-analysis-example">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, capacitors C1 and C2, and resistors R1 and R2. Vin and C1 connect at node a. C1, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and C2 connect at node c. Vin, R1, and C2 connect at node d. Voltage Vout is measured over C2.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C_1$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_2$, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The second-order differential equation describing the output voltage is shown below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{1}{R_2C_2}\frac{dv_{in}(t)}{dt} = \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{C_1R_1} + \frac{1}{C_2R_2} + \frac{1}{C_1R_2}\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_2}\right]v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The transfer function of the circuit is therefore equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{\frac{1}{R_2C_2} j\omega}{-\omega^2 + \left[\frac{1}{C_1R_1} + \frac{1}{C_2R_2} + \frac{1}{C_1R_2}\right]j\omega + \left[\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_2}\right]}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>

					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{416.6667 j\omega}{-\omega^2 + 750 j\omega + 52083.33}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-center-frequency-bsf-bpf" /> to calculate the center frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= \sqrt{52083.33}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 228~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{228}{750}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.304</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The bode plot for this circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-function-bpf-bode">
							<shortdescription>Top: magnitude response showing BPF behavior. Bottom: phase response with the phase decreasing from 90 degrees to -90 degrees.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$\omega$~(rad/s), xmin=1, xmax=100000]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$|H(j\omega)|$, ymode=log, xmode=log]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1:100000, samples=100]	{(416.6667*x)/sqrt((52083-x^2)^2+(750^2*x^2))};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$\angle H(j\omega)$~($^\circ$), xmode=log, ytick={-90,0,90}]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1:228.2, samples=100]	{90-atan(x*750/(52083-x^2))};
											\addplot[thick, domain=228.3:100000, samples=100]	{90-atan(x*750/(52083-x^2))-180};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-bsfs">
			<title>Band-Stop Filters (BSF)</title>

			<p>A <term>band-stop filter</term> only allows a range of frequencies to pass through to the output. Anything between a range of frequencies known as the <term>stop-band</term> is blocked from reaching the output. All other frequencies are allowed through. The width of the stop-band is defined by the bandwidth of the circuit. <alert>Band-stop filters cannot be first-order circuits; they must be second-order or higher.</alert></p>
			
			<p>As the frequency of the input source changes, the magnitude of the output voltage will change. This can be characterized by calculating <m>v_{out}/v_{in}</m> at varying frequencies and looking at the response. An ideal band-stop filter has an ideal brick wall response where all frequencies inside the stop-band are completely eliminated from the output, and all frequencies outside the stop-band are able to pass through with no change in magnitude.</p>
			
			<p>The output response of a band-stop filter is shown in <xref ref="bsf-magnitude-response" />. (Note the use of the log/log scale. In addition, note that the x-axis is normalized to the cutoff frequency.) The ideal brick wall response is depicted with a dashed line. The real response expected from a band-stop filter is depicted with a thick black line.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="bsf-magnitude-response">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between an ideal (<q>brick wall</q>) band-stop filter and a real band-stop filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-bsf-magnitude-response">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Dashed line has magnitude of 1 until wc1, goes to zero until wc2, then goes to 1 again. Thick line is 1 until around wc1, decays to w/wc=1, rises to wc2, then stays 1.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, ymax=2, ymin=0.0001, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:0.9999, samples=100, forget plot]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/0.1)^2))};
								\addplot [thick, domain=1.001:100, samples=100]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/0.1)^2))};
								\addlegendentry{real BSF}
								\addplot [thick, dashed]	coordinates {(0.01, 1) (0.099,1) (0.099, 0.0001) (10.11, 0.0001) (10.11, 1) (100, 1)};
								\addlegendentry{ideal BSF}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Band-stop filters are characterized by their quality factor. A filter with a high quality factor will have a very narrow stop-band (low bandwidth), and vice versa. A graph showing the output response of band-stop filters with different quality factors is shown in <xref ref="bsf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="bsf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq">
  				<caption>Output response comparison between a low q-factor band-stop filter and a high q-factor band-stop filter.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-bsf-magnitude-response-highq-and-lowq">
					<shortdescription>Graph of vout/vin on y-axis and w/wc on x-axis. Graph is log-log. Black line gradually decays to w/wc=1, then gradually rises. Gray line quickly decays to w/wc=1, then quickly rises.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{loglogaxis}[xlabel=$\omega/\omega_c$, ylabel=$v_{out}/v_{in}$, xmin=0.01, xmax=100, legend pos=south east, clip=false, height=5cm, width=\linewidth]
								\addplot [thick, domain=0.01:0.9999, samples=100, forget plot]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/0.1)^2))};
								\addplot [thick, domain=1.001:100, samples=100]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/0.1)^2))};
								\addlegendentry{low q-factor}
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=0.01:0.99999, samples=100]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/5)^2))};
								\addplot [thick, gray, domain=1.0001:100, samples=100]	{abs(1-x^2)/(sqrt((1-x^2)^2+(x/5)^2))};
								\addlegendentry{high q-factor}
							\end{loglogaxis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example>
				<title>Band-stop filter analysis</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Analyze the following band-stop filter. Calculate the center frequency, the bandwidth, and the quality factor of the circuit. The circuit parameters are <m>R = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 200~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>C = 50~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-bsf-analysis-example">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C, all connected in series. Voltage Vout is measured over the inductor and capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=$R$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [L, l_=$L$]								(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=$C$]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.25,4) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(t)$]					(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The second-order differential equation describing the output voltage is shown below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{d^2v_{in}(t)}{dt^2} +\frac{1}{LC}v_{in}(t)= \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R}{L}\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{LC}\right]v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The transfer function of the circuit is therefore equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + \frac{1}{LC}}{-\omega^2 + \frac{R}{L}j\omega + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>

					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + 100000}{-\omega^2 + 50j\omega + 100000}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-center-frequency-bsf-bpf" /> to calculate the center frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= \sqrt{100000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 316~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{316~\textrm{rad/s}}{50~\textrm{rad/s}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.32</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-a1" /> to calculate the bandwidth of the filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\beta = 50~\textrm{rad/s}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Bode plot for this filter is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-bsf-example1-bode">
							<shortdescription>Top: magnitude response showing BSF behavior. Bottom: phase response with the phase mostly equal to 0, with a discontinuity at the resonant frequency.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$\omega$~(rad/s), xmin=1, xmax=100000]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$|H(j\omega)|$, ymode=log, xmode=log]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1:316.2, samples=100]	{(1E5-x^2)/sqrt((1E5-x^2)^2+(50^2*x^2))};
											\addplot[thick, domain=316.3:100000, samples=100]	{abs(1E5-x^2)/sqrt((1E5-x^2)^2+(50^2*x^2))};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$\angle H(j\omega)$~($^\circ$), xmode=log, ytick={-90,0,90}]
											\addplot[thick, domain=1:100000, samples=200]	{-atan(x*50/(1E5-x^2))};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-determinefilter">
			<title>Determining Filter Type</title>

			<p>To determine the type of filter a circuit corresponds to, first analyze the circuit given DC steady-state conditions (<m>\omega=0</m>). In this case, all capacitors can be replaced with opens and all inductors can be replaced with shorts. Determine the value of the output voltage (which will either be zero or non-zero).</p>
			
			<p>Second, analyze the circuit given a frequency approaching infinity (<m>\omega\rightarrow\infty</m>). In this case, all capacitors can be replaced with shorts and all inductors can be replaced with opens. Determine the value of the output voltage (which will either be zero or non-zero).</p>
			
			<p>Then, use <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" /> to determine the filter type based on these two calculations.</p>

			<table xml:id="ch9-filtertype-table">
				<title>Determination of filter type based on very-low and very-high frequency analysis of the output voltage.</title>
  				<tabular halign="left" width="100%">
					<col width="33%" />
					<col width="33%" />
					<col width="33%" />
    				<row header="yes" bottom="medium">
      					<cell><m>v_{out}(0)</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>v_{out}(\infty)</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>Filter Type</p></cell>
	    			</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>band-pass filter</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>non-zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>high-pass filter</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>non-zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>low-pass filter</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>non-zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>non-zero</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>band-stop filter</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<example>
				<title>Determining filter type</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Determine the type of filter shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Series RLC circuit consisting of voltage source Vin, inductor L, resistor R, and capacitor C. Voltage Vout is measured over the capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit for <m>\omega=0</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of Voltage source Vin(0) wired in series with R. Vout is measured over R and Vin.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the output voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(0) = v_{in}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit for <m>\omega\rightarrow\infty</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by a short and the inductor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example1-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of Voltage source Vin(0) wired in series with R. Vout is measured over a short.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [open]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the output voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(\infty) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Consult <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />. The circuit is a second-order, passive, low-pass filter.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Determining filter type</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Determine the type of filter shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors (R, RC, and RL), capacitor C, and inductor L. Vin and R connect at node a. R, RC, and RL connect at node b. RC and C connect at node c. RL and L connect at node d. Vin, C, and L connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]										(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_C$, *-]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, -*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R_L$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit for <m>\omega=0</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors (R, RC, and RL), an open, and a short. Vin and R connect at node a. R, RC, and RL connect at node b. RC and the open connect at node c. RL and the short connect at node d. Vin, the open, and the short connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,3.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,3.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(2.5,3.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3.5) to [short]									(5,3.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3.5) to [R, l_=$R_C$, *-]							(2.5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,1.5) to [open]										(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,3.5) to [R, l_=$R_L$]								(5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(5,1.5) to [short]										(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,3.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]					(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the output voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(0) = v_{in}\frac{R_L}{R+R_L}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit for <m>\omega\rightarrow\infty</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by a short and the inductor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filter-type-example2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors (R, RC, and RL), a short, and an open. Vin and R connect at node a. R, RC, and RL connect at node b. RC and the short connect at node c. RL and the open connect at node d. Vin, the short, and the open connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,3.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,3.5) to [R, l^=$R$]										(2.5,3.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3.5) to [short]										(5,3.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3.5) to [R, l_=$R_C$, *-]								(2.5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,1.5) to [short, -*]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,3.5) to [R, l_=$R_L$]									(5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(5,1.5) to [open]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,3.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the output voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(\infty) = v_{in}\frac{R_C}{R+R_C}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Consult <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />. The circuit is a second-order, passive, band-stop filter.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch9-activefilters">
		<title>Active Filters</title>

		<introduction>
		
			<p>It is possible to design filters that use an op-amp; these filters are known as active filters. Active filters are capable of generating output amplification (from the op-amp gain). While there are many different types of active filters, only a few will be discussed in this book.</p>

			<p>All properties of active filters, such as cutoff frequency, bandwidth, quality factor, and so on, are defined as discussed in <xref ref="ch9-passivefilters" />.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-firstorder">
			<title>First-Order Active Filters</title>

			<p>First-order active filters are first-order (one energy storage element) filters that use an op-amp.</p>
			
			<example xml:id="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example">
				<title>Active first-order low-pass filter</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>An active first-order low-pass filter is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-lpf-diagram1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, C, and R2 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2, C, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node d. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]		(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]			($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [C, l^=$C$, *-*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The first-order differential equation that describes the behavior of this low-pass filter can be derived by performing KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-\frac{v_{in}(t)}{R_1C} = \frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{R_2C}v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The cutoff frequency of this filter is defined below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_c = \frac{1}{R_2C}</md>
					</p>

					<p>To determine the filter type, first analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m> to calculate <m>v_{out}(0)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-lpf-diagram2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and R2 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node d. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]		(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]			($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]	++(up:1) to 	[R, l^=$R_2$] ++ (right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v_{out}(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(0) = -\frac{R_2}{R_1}v_{in}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega\rightarrow\infty</m> to calculate <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-lpf-diagram3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and a short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, the short, and R2 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2, the short, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node d. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]		(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]			($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The output voltage is zero due to the virtual node between the non-inverting and inverting inputs, and the fact that the shorted capacitor turns this into a voltage follower circuit (refer to <xref ref="ch4-voltagefollower" />).</p>
					
					<p>Consult <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />. The circuit is a first-order, active, low-pass filter.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example xml:id="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example">
				<title>Active first-order high-pass filter</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>An active first-order high-pass filter is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-hpf-diagram1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, op-amp, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and C connect at node b. C, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [C, l^=$C$]				(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]			($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The first-order differential equation that describes the behavior of this high-pass filter can be derived by performing KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-\frac{R_2}{R_1}\frac{dv_{in}(t)}{dt} = \frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{R_1C}v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The cutoff frequency of this filter is defined below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_c = \frac{1}{R_1C}</md>
					</p>

					<p>To determine the filter type, first analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m> to calculate <m>v_{out}(0)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-hpf-diagram2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, op-amp, resistors R1 and R2, and an open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and the open connect at node b. The open, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [open]				(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]			($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The output is zero because the input is not connected to the output.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega\rightarrow\infty</m> to calculate <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-active-firstorder-hpf-diagram3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, op-amp, resistors R1 and R2, and a short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and the short connect at node b. The short, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [short]				(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]			($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_{out}(\infty) = -\frac{R_2}{R_1}v_{in}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Consult <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />. The circuit is a first-order, active, high-pass filter.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-cascadedfilters">
			<title>Cascaded Active Filters</title>

			<p>As mentioned in <xref ref="ch4-cascaded" />, op-amps can be cascaded together. While <xref ref="ch4-cascaded" /> made reference to the product of each individual circuit gain, cascaded filters can be analyzed by calculating the product of each filter's transfer function (this topic will be introduced in <xref ref="ch9-transferfuncs" />). In this manner, band-pass filters, band-stop filters, and second-order (or higher) high-pass and low-pass filters can be designed.</p>

			<p>Finally, higher-order active filters can be created by cascading together multiple low-pass filters or multiple high-pass filters (for example). Cascading together identical filters creates a sharper slope at the cutoff frequency. An inverting amplifier can be staged at the output to create an overall circuit gain.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Cascaded active band-pass filter</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>A low-pass filter whose input is fed into a high-pass filter acts as a band-pass filter. A cascaded active band-pass filter is depicted below. (Note that this is the circuit from <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example" /> fed into the circuit from <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example" />, whose output is then fed into an inverting op-amp.)</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-cascaded-active-bpf-example-diagram">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of three op-amp stages. The left stage connects to Vin and is a LPF. The middle stage is a HPF. The right stage is an inverting op-amp.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_L$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp.-)-(2,3)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [C, l^=$C_L$, *-*]		($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_L$]			($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	(4.5,0) node[op amp, anchor=-] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp2.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(1.5,0)$) to [C, l^=$C_H$]			(opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=$R_H$]					($(opamp2.-)-(1.5,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_H$]			($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)+(2,0)$) node[op amp, anchor=-]	 (opamp3) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,3)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,-1.49)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,-1.49)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [R, l^=$R_I$]					(opamp3.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp3.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp3.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp3.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_F$]			($(opamp3.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This filter has three stages. The first stage is a low-pass filter (analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example" />), the second stage is a high-pass filter (analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example" />), and the third stage is an inverting amplifier to provide overall amplification (gain).</p>
					
					<p>The low-pass filter stage sets the upper cutoff frequency <m>\omega_{c2}</m>. Based on the analysis in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example" />, we can find that</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_{c2} = \frac{1}{R_LC_L}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>The high-pass filter stage sets the lower cutoff frequency <m>\omega_{c1}</m>. Based on the analysis in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example" />, we can find that</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_{c1} = \frac{1}{R_HC_H}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>The final stage has a gain given by <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> and sets the overall gain of the band-pass filter circuit. In this manner, each of the circuit properties can be tuned in each individual stage.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Active band-stop filter</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>A low-pass filter (analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-lpf-example" />) and high-pass filter (analyzed in <xref ref="ch9-active-first-order-hpf-example" />) can also be used as inputs to a summing amplifier, which leads to an active band-stop filter. This circuit is depicted below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-active-bsf-example-diagram">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of a summing op-amp with two inputs of an op-amp LPF and an op-amp HPF.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_L$]				(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [C, l^=$C_L$, *-*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_L$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=$R_I$]						($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	(0,-4) node[op amp] (opamp2) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(3.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp2.-)-(3.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(3.5,0)$) to [short, *-]				($(opamp.-)-(3.5,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(3.5,0)$) to [short]					($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(1.5,0)$) to [C, l^=$C_H$]				(opamp2.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(3.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_H$]				($(opamp2.-)-(1.5,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_H$]				($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [R, l^=$R_I$]						($(opamp2.out)+(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	(4, -2) node[op amp, anchor=-]	 (opamp3) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$) to [short]					($(opamp3.-)-(0.8,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.out)+(2,0)$) to [short, -*]				($(opamp3.-)-(0.8,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp3.-)-(0.8,0)$) to [short]					(opamp3.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_F$, *-]			($(opamp3.out)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp3.out)+(0,2.5)$) to [short, -*]				(opamp3.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)-(3.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,-3.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp3.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp3.out)+(0.5,-3.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The low-pass filter stage sets the lower cutoff frequency of this circuit. The high-pass filter stage sets the higher cutoff frequency of this circuit. The inverting op-amp sets the overall filter gain.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch9-otheractive">
			<title>Other Active Filter Topologies</title>

			<p>While there are numerous other active (and passive) filter designs that will not be explored in this book, one other active filter topology will be described. This is called the Sallen-Key topology. A low-pass Sallen-Key filter is depicted in <xref ref="unity-gain-sallen-key-filter" />. (Swapping all resistors for capacitors and vice versa will result in a high-pass Sallen-Key filter. A band-pass Sallen-Key filter can also be realized.)</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="unity-gain-sallen-key-filter">
  				<caption>A unity gain Sallen-Key low-pass filter circuit.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-unity-gain-sallen-key-filter">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, capacitors C1 and C2, and a short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and C1 connect at node b. R2, C2, and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. The short connects to the inverting input of the op-amp at node d. C1, the short, and the output of the op-amp connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vin and C2 connect at node f. Voltage Vout drops from node e to node f.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				(opamp.+);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$);
							\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]								($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
							\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
							\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [C, l_=$C_2$, *-*]						($(opamp.+)-(0,2)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$]	($(opamp.+)-(4,2)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)+(-2,2.75)$) to [C, l^=$C_1$]			($(opamp.out)+(0.5,2.25)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2,0)$) to [short, *-]					($(opamp.+)+(-2,2.75)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,2.25)$) to [short, -*]			($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$);
							\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(t)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.5)$);
							\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4,2)$) to [short, -o]					($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.5)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The voltage follower configuration of the circuit gives it a gain of one (known as unity gain), and ensures that the voltage at the non-inverting input of the op-amp is equal to <m>v_{out}(t)</m>. If a filter gain is desired, then the voltage follower configuration can be altered to include divided feedback instead of shorting the inverting input to the output.</p>
			
			<p>The Sallen-Key filter shown in <xref ref="unity-gain-sallen-key-filter" /> has a differential equation described in <xref ref="ch9-sallen-key-diffeq" /> and is derived in <xref ref="ch9-sallen-key-derivation" />. The derivation uses KCL at the nodes connecting the two resistors to capacitor <m>C_1</m> and also at the non-inverting input of the op-amp. (The term <m>v_a(t)</m> in the derivation denotes the node connecting the two resistors to capacitor <m>C_1</m>.)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch9-sallen-key-diffeq">\frac{1}{R_1R_2C_1C_2}v_{in}(t) = \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{R_1C_1}+\frac{1}{R_2C_1}\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{R_1R_2C_1C_2}\right]v_{out}(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch9-sallen-key-derivation">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(t)-v_a(t)}{R_1} \amp= C_1\frac{d}{dt}\left[v_a(t)-v_{out}(t)\right] + C_2\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(t)}{R_1} \amp= \left[\frac{1}{R_1}+C_1\frac{d}{dt}\right]v_a(t) + \left[C_2\frac{d}{dt}-C_1\frac{d}{dt}\right]v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v_a(t)-v_{out}(t)}{R_2} \amp= C_2\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>v_a(t) \amp= R_2C_2\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}+v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(t)}{R_1} \amp= \left[\frac{1}{R_1}+C_1\frac{d}{dt}\right]\left[R_2C_2\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}+v_{out}(t)\right] + \left[C_2\frac{d}{dt}-C_1\frac{d}{dt}\right]v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2C_2}{R_1}\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}+\frac{1}{R_1}v_{out}(t)+C_1R_2C_2\frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + C_2\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= R_2C_1C_2\frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2}+\left[\frac{R_2C_2}{R_1}+C_2\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{R_1}\right]v_{out}(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{R_1R_2C_1C_2}v_{in}(t) \amp= \frac{d^2v_{out}(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{R_1C_1}+\frac{1}{R_2C_1}\right]\frac{dv_{out}(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{R_1R_2C_1C_2}\right]v_{out}(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>
	
  		</subsection>

	</section>

	

	<references xml:id="phasor-references">

		<biblio type="raw" xml:id="erikcheever">Cheever, Erik. Bode Plots Overview. Retrieved 3 March 2021. <url href="https://lpsa.swarthmore.edu/Bode/Bode.html">https://lpsa.swarthmore.edu/Bode/Bode.html</url></biblio>
			
		<biblio type="raw" xml:id="mitme">Massachusetts Institute of Technology Department of Mechanical Engineering. Retrieved 2 September 2021. <url href="https://web.mit.edu/2.14/www/Handouts/PoleZero.pdf">https://web.mit.edu/2.14/www/Handouts/PoleZero.pdf</url></biblio>

		<biblio type="raw" xml:id="natsemi">National Semiconductor Corporation, 2010. Application Note 779: A Basic Introduction to Filters - Active, Passive, and Switched Capacitor. <url href="https://www.ti.com/lit/an/snoa224a/snoa224a.pdf">https://www.ti.com/lit/an/snoa224a/snoa224a.pdf</url></biblio>

		<biblio type="raw" xml:id="tisallenkey">Texas Instruments, 2002. Application Report: Analysis of the Sallen-Key Architecture. <url href="https://www.ti.com/lit/an/sloa024b/sloa024b.pdf">https://www.ti.com/lit/an/sloa024b/sloa024b.pdf</url></biblio>
	
	</references>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Complex Voltage and Current Divider</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example1-diagram" />. The source is equal to <m>v_{AC}(t) = 50~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t + 60\pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-voltage-divider-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 50ohm resistor, and 35uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor and capacitor connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vac connects to the capacitor at node c. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=50~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=35~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 41.7~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t + 26.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi60)(35\times10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j75.7881~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the source to phasor form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 50\cos(60^\circ)+j50\sin(60^\circ)~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 25 + j43.3013~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 50ohm resistor, and -j75.8ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor and -j75.8ohm impedance connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vac connects to the -j75.8ohm impedance at node c. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=50~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j75.8~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (25+j43.30~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{-j75.79~\Omega}{50~\Omega-j75.79~\Omega}\right)</mrow><mrow>\amp= 37.3227 + j18.6782~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 41.7356~\textrm{V} \angle 26.5858^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 41.7~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t + 26.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example2-diagram" />. The source is equal to <m>v_{AC}(t) = 160~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi80t + 150\pi/180)</m></p>

					<figure xml:id="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-voltage-divider-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 53mH inductor, 120ohm resistor, and 26uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the inductor at node a. The inductor and resistor connect at node b. The resistor and capacitor connect at node c. The capacitor and low-potential end of Vac connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [L, l^=53~mH]								(3,5);
									\draw[]	(3,5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$]						(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [C, l_=26~$\mu$F]						(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3.5,5) to [open, v^=$v(t)$]						(3.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 175~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi80t+140\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi80)(26\times10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j76.5168~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi80)(0.053)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j26.6407~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the source to phasor form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 160\cos(150^\circ)+j160\sin(150^\circ)~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -138.56 + j80~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, j26.6ohm impedance, 120ohm resistor, and -j76.5ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the j26.6ohm impedance at node a. The j26.6ohm impedance and resistor connect at node b. The resistor and -j76.5ohm impedance connect at node c. The -j76.5ohm impedance and low-potential end of Vac connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [L, l^=j26.6~$\Omega$]							(3,5);
									\draw[]	(3,5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$]							(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j76.5~$\Omega$]					(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3.5,5) to [open, v^=$\vec{V}$]							(3.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (-138.56+j80~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{120-j75.8~\Omega}{120-j75.8~\Omega+j26.4~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -134.25+j112.26~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 175.2265~\textrm{V} \angle 140.0462^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 175~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi80t+140\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example3-diagram" />. The source frequency is 150<nbsp />Hz.</p>

					<figure xml:id="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-voltage-divider-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a (2A angle -120 deg) current source, -j7ohm impedance, 6ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source, -j7ohm, and 6ohm impedances connect at node a. The 6ohm and j5ohm impedances connect at node b. The low-potential end of the source, -j7ohm, and j5ohm impedances connect at node c. Current I flows through the j5ohm impedance from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=20~A~$\angle-$120$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]													(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$]											(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I}$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 22.1~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi150t+168\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of a (2A angle -120 deg) current source, -j7ohm impedance, and 6+j5 ohm impedance. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source is connected to node a. Current I flows through the 6+j5ohm impedance from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=20~A~$\angle-$120$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=6+j5~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(-j7~\Omega)(6+j5~\Omega)}{(-j7~\Omega)+(6+j5~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.35 - j4.55~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the Cartesian form of the source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 20\cos(-120^\circ)+j20\sin(-120^\circ)~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -10 - j17.3205~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= (-10 - j17.3205~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{7.35 - j4.55~\Omega}{6+j5~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -21.6865 + j4.4378~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 22.1359~\textrm{A} \angle 168.4349^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 22.1~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi150t+168\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example4-diagram" />. The source voltage is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 7~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-voltage-divider-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 28ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, 52mH inductor, and 48uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 28ohm resistor at node a. The 28ohm resistor and 52mH inductor connect at node b. The inductor, capacitor, and 30ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, capacitor, and 30ohm resistor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=28~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=52~mH]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=48~$\mu$F, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 3.23~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi100t-63.9\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi100)(48\times10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j33.1573~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi100)(0.052)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j32.6726~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the source to phasor form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 7\cos(0^\circ)+j7\sin(0^\circ)~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 28ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, j32.7ohm impedance, and -j33.2ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 28ohm resistor at node a. The 28ohm resistor and j32.7ohm impedance connect at node b. The j32.7ohm impedance, -j33.2ohm impedance, and 30ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, -j33.2ohm impedance, and 30ohm resistor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=28~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j32.7~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j33.2~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 28<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j32.7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the <minus />j33.2<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(30~\Omega)(-j33.1573~\Omega)}{(30~\Omega)+(-j33.1573~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 16.4960 - j14.9252~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 28+j32.7ohm impedance, and 16.5-j14.9ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 28+j32.7ohm impedance at node a. The two impedances connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vac connects to the 16.5-j14.9ohm impedance at node c. Voltage V drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=28+j32.7~$\Omega$]						(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=16.5$-$j14.9~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= 7~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{16.50-j14.93~\Omega}{16.50-j14.93~\Omega+28+j32.67~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.43 - j2.92~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.2506~\textrm{V} \angle -63.8828^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 3.23~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi100t-63.9\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example5-diagram" />. The source value is <m>i_{AC}(t) = 4.5~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi6000t + 30\pi/180)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="cplx-current-voltage-divider-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-voltage-current-divider-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-cplx-current-voltage-divider-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and two nodes consisting of current source Iac, 40ohm resistor, 330nF capacitor, and 1mH inductor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source is connected to node a. Branch current i(t) flows through the resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=40~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=330~nF, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=1~mH]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 3.92~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi6000t + 59.4\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi6000)(330\times10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j80.3813~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi6000)(0.001)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j37.6991~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the source to phasor form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 4.5\cos(30^\circ)+j4.5\sin(30^\circ)~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.8971 + j2.25~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-voltage-current-div-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and two nodes consisting of current source Iac, 40ohm resistor, -j80.4ohm impedance, and j37.7ohm impedance. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source is connected to node a. Branch current I flows through the resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=40~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j80.4~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j37.7~$\Omega$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{40~\Omega}+\frac{1}{j37.6991~\Omega}+\frac{1}{-j80.3813~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30.3623 + j17.1062~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Apply the current divider rule.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= (3.90+j2.25~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{30.3623 + j17.1062}{40~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.9959 + j3.3745~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.9206~\textrm{A} \angle 59.3971^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 3.92~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi6000t + 59.4\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Complex KCL and KVL</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise1-diagram" />. The source has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 24~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 60t)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 40ohm resistor, 80ohm resistor, 330uF capacitor, and 10mH inductor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 40ohm resistor at node a. The 40ohm resistor, capacitor, and inductor connect at node b. The inductor and 80ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, capacitor, and 80ohm resistor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 80ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=330~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=80~$\Omega$, i^=$i(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 4.64~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t - 73.2\pi/180)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 58~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi60t -73.2\pi/180)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi60)(330\times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j8.0381~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L}\amp= j(2\pi60)(0.01)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j3.7699~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 24~\cos{0^\circ} + j24~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 40ohm resistor, 80ohm resistor, -j8.04ohm impedance, and j3.77ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 40ohm resistor at node a. The 40ohm resistor, -j8.04ohm impedance, and j3.77ohm impedance connect at node b. The j3.77ohm impedance and 80ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, -j8.04ohm impedance, and 80ohm resistor connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d. Branch current I flows through the 80ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j8.04~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j3.77~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=80~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 40<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, <minus />j8.04<nbsp />&#8486; impedance, and j3.77<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. Then, solve for <m>\vec{V}</m>. The units used below are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}}-\vec{V}}{40} \amp= \frac{\vec{V}}{-j8.04} + \frac{\vec{V}}{80+j3.77}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{24}{40} \amp= \frac{\vec{V}}{40} + \frac{\vec{V}}{-j8.04} + \frac{\vec{V}}{80+j3.77}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \vec{V}\left(\frac{1}{40} + \frac{1}{-j8.04} + \frac{1}{80+j3.77}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= \frac{24}{40}\left(\frac{1}{\frac{1}{40} + \frac{1}{-j8.04} + \frac{1}{80+j3.77}}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.3435 - j4.4392~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.6380~\textrm{V} \angle -73.1622^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{I}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= \frac{1.3435 - j4.4392~\textrm{V}}{80~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0168 - j0.0555~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 16.7933 - j55.4899~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 57.9753~\textrm{mA} \angle -73.1622^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert both quantities to time-varying form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 4.64~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t - 73.2\pi/180)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 58~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi60t -73.2\pi/180)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V}</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise2-diagram" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of (80V angle 120deg) voltage source, 8ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, -j6ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 8ohm resistor at node a. The three resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor, -j6ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance connect at node c. The low-potential end of the source, 10ohm resistor, -j6ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=80~V~$\angle$~120$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j6~$\Omega$, *-*]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]													(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>-31.2 + j28.7~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the <minus />j6<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(j5~\Omega)(-j6~\Omega)}{(j5~\Omega)+(-j6~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j30~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of (80V angle 120deg) voltage source, 8ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, and j30ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 8ohm resistor at node a. The three resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor and j30ohm impedance connect at node c. The low-potential end of the source, 10ohm resistor, and j30ohm impedance connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=80~V~$\angle$~120$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=j30~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Define each branch current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Branch current I1 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current I2 flows through the 10ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current I3 flows through the 5ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=80~V~$\angle$~120$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=j30~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>There are three unknown branch currents. Therefore, three equations will need to be derived.</p>
					
					<p>There are two perfect nodes in this circuit. However, using both will generate linearly dependent equations. Perform KCL at the node located where the 8<nbsp />&#8486;, 10<nbsp />&#8486;, and 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistors connect. All units used in subsequent equations in this problem are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_1}-\vec{I_2}-\vec{I_3} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Any two of the three circuit loops can be used with KCL to derive the other two equations. The first KVL equation below analyzes the left loop of the circuit, and the second KVL equation below analyzes the right loop of the circuit. </p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>8\vec{I_1} + 10\vec{I_2} \amp= 80 \angle 120^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>-10\vec{I_2} + (5+j30)\vec{I_3} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp -1 	\amp -1	 		\amp 0	\\
								8	\amp 10		\amp 0		\amp 80 \angle 120^\circ	\\
								0	\amp -10		\amp 5+j30	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The branch current that needs to be known to solve for <m>\vec{V}</m> is <m>\vec{I_3}</m>. This value is</p>

					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_3} = 0.9551 + j1.0414~\textrm{A}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (0.9551 + j1.0414~\textrm{A})(j30~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -31.2 + j28.7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise3-diagram" />. The source frequency is 20<nbsp />kHz.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of (15A angle 0deg) current source, 6ohm resistor, 4ohm resistor, 8ohm resistor, j9ohm impedance, -j3ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source, 6ohm, and j9ohm connect at node a. The j9ohm, -j3ohm, and 8ohm connect at node b. The -j3ohm and 4ohm connect at node c. The 8ohm and j5ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of the source, 6ohm, 4ohm, and j5ohm connect at node e. Voltage V drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=15~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]												(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l^=j9~$\Omega$]									(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=$-$j3~$\Omega$, *-]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~$\Omega$, -*]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]										(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 14.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 20000t + 4.18\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The <minus />j3<nbsp />&#8486; and 4<nbsp />&#8486; impedances are connected in series.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of (15A angle 0deg) current source, 6ohm resistor, 8ohm resistor, j9ohm impedance, 4-j3ohm impedance, and j5ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source, 6ohm, and j9ohm connect at node a. The j9ohm, 4-j3ohm, and 8ohm connect at node b. The 8ohm and j5ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of the source, 6ohm, 4-j3ohm, and j5ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=15~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j9~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=4$-$j3~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Define branch currents.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Branch current I1 flows through the 6ohm resistor from node at to node d. Branch current I2 flows through the j9ohm impedance from node a to node c. Branch current I3 flows through the 4-j3ohm impedance from node b to node d. Branch current I4 flows through the 8ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=15~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j9~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=4$-$j3~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_4}$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at two perfect nodes. All units used in subsequent equations in this problem are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} + \vec{I_2} \amp= 15 \angle 0^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2}-\vec{I_3}-\vec{I_4} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around two loops. The equations below use the center loop and right loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-6\vec{I_1} +j9\vec{I_2} + (4-j3)\vec{I_3} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>-(4-j3)\vec{I_3} + (8+j5)\vec{I_4} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_4}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0		\amp 15 \angle 0^\circ\\
								0 	\amp 1	\amp -1		\amp -1		\amp 0\\
								-6	\amp j9	\amp (4-j3)	\amp 0		\amp 0\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp -(4-j3)	\amp (8+j5)	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The branch current that needs to be known to solve for <m>\vec{V}</m> is <m>\vec{I_4}</m>. This value is</p>

					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_4} = 0.2130 - j2.9179~\textrm{A}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (0.2130 - j2.9179~\textrm{A})(j5~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.5897 + j1.0652~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.6286~\textrm{V} \angle 4.1758^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the signal to a time-varying function.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 14.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 20000t + 4.18\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise4-diagram" />. The source frequency is 30<nbsp />Hz.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of a (100V angle 0deg) voltage source, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, -j5ohm impedance, -j10ohm impedance, and j10ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source, 5ohm, and -j5ohm connect at node a. The 5ohm, 10ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node b. The -j5ohm, 10ohm, and j10ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of the source, -j10ohm, and j10ohm connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j10~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j5~$\Omega$]										(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]												(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]												(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 146~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi30t + 14.0 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Define branch currents.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Branch current I1 flows through the -j5ohm impedance from node a to node c. Branch current I2 flows through the 5ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current I3 flows through the -j10ohm impedance from node b to node d. Branch current I4 flows through the 10ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current I5 flows through the j10ohm impedance from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_4}$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j10~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, i_=$\vec{I_5}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j5~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]						(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]												(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]												(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at two perfect nodes. All units used in subsequent equations in this problem are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2}-\vec{I_3}-\vec{I_4} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1}+\vec{I_4}-\vec{I_5} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around three perfect loops.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>5\vec{I_2} -j10\vec{I_3} \amp= 100 \angle 0^\circ</mrow>
							<mrow>j10\vec{I_3} + 10\vec{I_4} + j10\vec{I_5} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>-j5\vec{I_1} - 10\vec{I_4} - 5\vec{I_2} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The five equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_4}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_5}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0	\amp 1		\amp -1		\amp -1		\amp 0		\amp 0\\
								1	\amp 0		\amp 0		\amp 1		\amp -1		\amp 0\\
								0	\amp 5		\amp -j10	\amp 0		\amp 0		\amp 100 \angle 0^\circ\\
								0	\amp 0		\amp j10	\amp 10		\amp j10	\amp 0\\
								-j5	\amp -5		\amp 0		\amp -10	\amp 0		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The branch current that needs to be known to solve for <m>\vec{V}</m> is <m>\vec{I_5}</m>. This value is</p>

					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_5} = 3.53-j14.12~\textrm{A}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (3.53-j14.12~\textrm{A})(j10~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 141.18+j35.29~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 145.52~\textrm{V} \angle 14.04^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 146~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi30t + 14.0 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-diagram" />. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 5~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 200 t)</m>. The proportionality constant of the VCCS has a unit of A/V.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-cplx-kcl-kvl-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 2ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, 7ohm resistor, 40uF capacitor, 100uF capacitor, and 0.5*v(t) VCCS. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 2ohm at node a. The 2ohm, 5ohm, and 40uF connect at node b. The 40uF, VCCS, 7ohm, and 100uF connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The low-potential end of Vac, 5ohm, VCCS, 7ohm, and 100uF connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=40~$\mu$F]							(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.5$v(t)$, *-*]					(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [short]									(10.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=7~$\Omega$, *-*]						(8,0);
									\draw[]	(10.5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~$\mu$F]						(10.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>3.12~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi200t - 8 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C40}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 200)(40 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j19.8944~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C100}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 200)(100 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j7.9577~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the source to phasor form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 5~\cos{0^\circ} +j5~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 2ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, 7ohm resistor, -j19.9ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, and 0.5*V VCCS. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 2ohm at node a. The 2ohm, 5ohm, and -j19.9ohm connect at node b. The -j19.9ohm, VCCS, 7ohm, and -j7.96ohm connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The low-potential end of Vac, 5ohm, VCCS, 7ohm, and -j7.96ohm connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j19.9~$\Omega$]						(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.5$\vec{V}$, *-*]						(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [short]										(10.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=7~$\Omega$, *-*]							(8,0);
									\draw[]	(10.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$]						(10.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(10.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 7<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the <minus />j7.96<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(7~\Omega)(j7.9577~\Omega)}{(7~\Omega)+(j7.9577~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.9464 - j3.4714~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 2ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, -j19.9ohm impedance, 3.94-j3.47ohm impedance, and 0.5*V VCCS. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 2ohm at node a. The 2ohm, 5ohm, and -j19.9ohm connect at node b. The -j19.9ohm, VCCS, and 3.94-j3.47ohm connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The low-potential end of Vac, 5ohm, VCCS, and 3.94-j3.47ohm connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j19.9~$\Omega$]						(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.5$\vec{V}$, *-*]						(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [short]										(8.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(8.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=3.94$-$j3.47~$\Omega$]			(8.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(8.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Define branch currents.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-kcl-kvl-exercise5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Branch current I1 flows through the 2ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current I2 flows through the 5ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current I3 flows through the -j19.9ohm impedance from node b to node c. Branch current I4 flows through the 3.94-j3.47ohm impedance from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$, i_=$\vec{I_2}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j19.9~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]				(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.5$\vec{V}$, *-*]								(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [short]												(8.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(8.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=3.94$-$j3.47~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_4}$]	(8.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(8.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at two perfect nodes. All units used in subsequent equations in this problem are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1}-\vec{I_2}-\vec{I_3} \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_3} - 0.5\vec{V} - \vec{I_4} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around two perfect loops.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>2\vec{I_1} +5\vec{I_2} \amp= \vec{V_{AC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-5\vec{I_2} - j19.89\vec{I_3} + (3.94-j3.47)\vec{I_4} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive a dependent source equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{V} = 5\vec{I_2}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The five equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_3}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_4}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1	\amp -1		\amp -1			\amp 0			\amp 0		\amp 0	\\
								0	\amp 0		\amp 1			\amp -1			\amp -0.5	\amp 0	\\
								2	\amp 5		\amp 0			\amp 0			\amp 0		\amp 5	\\
								0	\amp -5		\amp -j19.89	\amp 3.9-j3.74	\amp 0		\amp 0	\\
								0	\amp 5		\amp 0			\amp 0			\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve the matrix for <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= 3.0910 - j0.4346~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.1214~\textrm{V} \angle -8.0037^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 3.12~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi200t - 8 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>


				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Complex Mesh Analysis</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Use mesh analysis to calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise1-diagram" />. The source frequency is 50<nbsp />Hz.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of (105V angle 0deg) voltage source, 12ohm resistor, 24ohm resistor, 9ohm resistor, and j12ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The three resistors connect at node b. The 9ohm and j12ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of the source, 24ohm, and j12ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=105~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=9~$\Omega$]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j12~$\Omega$]										(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 50.5~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50t + 17.9 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Mesh current <m>\vec{I_A}</m> will flow clockwise around the left mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_B}</m> will flow clockwise around the right mesh. Derive the mesh equations.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>105 \amp= 12\vec{I_A} + 24(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 24(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_A}) + (9+j12)\vec{I_B}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_A}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_B}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								36	\amp -24	\amp 105	\\
								-24	\amp 33+j12	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 4.75-j1.29\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 2.75-j1.94\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (24~\Omega)(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (24~\Omega)((4.75-j1.29~\textrm{A})-(2.75-j1.94~\textrm{A}))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 48.01+j15.52~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50.46~\textrm{V} \angle 17.91^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 50.5~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50t + 17.9 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2-diagram" />. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 30~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t + 45\pi/180)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 30ohm resistor, 15ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, 250uF capacitor, 160uF capacitor, and 8mH inductor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 15ohm and 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and 250uF connect at node b. The 15ohm and 8mH connect at node c. The 250uF, 8mH, and 20ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, 160uF, and 20ohm connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 160uF from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$](0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=160~$\mu$F, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=8~mH]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]								(0,4);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l^=250~$\mu$F]						(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]								(5,4);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 1.83~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi100t + 103 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of both capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C160}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 100)(160 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j9.9472~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C250}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 100)(250 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j6.3662~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi 100)(0.008)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j5.0265~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 30~\cos{45^\circ} + j30~\sin{45^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 21.2132 + j21.2132~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 30ohm resistor, 15ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, -j6.37ohm impedance, -j9.95ohm impedance, and j5.03ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 15ohm and 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and -j6.37ohm connect at node b. The 15ohm and j5.03ohm connect at node c. The -j6.37ohm, j5.03ohm, and 20ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, -j9.95ohm, and 20ohm connect at node e. Branch current I flows through the -j9.95ohm from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j9.95~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j5.03~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]										(0,4);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l^=$-$j6.37~$\Omega$]						(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]										(5,4);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and <minus />j6.37<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>
						
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 15ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, 30-j6.37ohm impedance, -j9.95ohm impedance, and j5.03ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to the 15ohm and 30-j6.37ohm at node a. The 15ohm and j5.03ohm connect at node b. The 30-j6.37ohm, j5.03ohm, and 20ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac, -j9.95ohm, and 20ohm connect at node d. Branch current I flows through the -j9.95ohm from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j9.95~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j5.03~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]										(0,4);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [generic, l^=30$-$j6.37~$\Omega$]					(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]										(5,4);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh current <m>\vec{I_A}</m> will flow clockwise around the upper mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_B}</m> will flow clockwise around the lower-left mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_C}</m> will flow clockwise around the lower-right mesh. Derive the mesh equations.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= (30-j6.37)\vec{I_A} + j5.03(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_C}) + 15(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</mrow>
							<mrow>21.21+j21.21 \amp= 15(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_A}) -j9.95(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_C})</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -j9.95(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_B}) + j5.03(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_A}) + 20\vec{I_C}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_A}</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_B}</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_C}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								45-j1.34	\amp -15		\amp -j5.03		\amp 	0\\
								4-15		\amp 15-j9.95	\amp j9.95		\amp 	21.21+j21.21\\
								4-j5.03	\amp j9.95			\amp 20-j4.92	\amp 	0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve the matrix for <m>\vec{I_B}</m> and <m>\vec{I_C}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_B} \amp= 0.31+j1.83~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_C} \amp= 0.73+j0.05~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the branch current <m>\vec{I}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= (\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_C})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.31+j1.83~\textrm{A})-(0.73+j0.05~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.42+j1.79~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.83~\textrm{A} \angle 103.18^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 1.83~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi100t + 103 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3-diagram" />. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 12~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi200t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 130ohm resistor, 80ohm resistor, 56ohm resistor, 210ohm resistor, 5uF capacitor, and 95mH inductor. The high-potential end of Vac and 130ohm connect at node a. The 130ohm, 5uF, and 56ohm connect at node b. The 5uF, 210ohm, and 80ohm connect at node c. The 56ohm, 210ohm, and 95mH connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, 80ohm, and 95mH connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=130~$\Omega$]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [C, l_=5~$\mu$F, *-]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=80~$\Omega$, -*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]									(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=210~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=56~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=95~mH, v^=$v(t)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 5.05~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi200t + 32.4 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi200)(5 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j31.8310~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi200)(0.095)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j119.38~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source to a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 12~\cos{0^\circ} + j12~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 130ohm resistor, 80ohm resistor, 56ohm resistor, 210ohm resistor, -j31.8ohm impedance, and j119ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac and 130ohm connect at node a. The 130ohm, -j31.8ohm, and 56ohm connect at node b. The -j31.8ohm, 210ohm, and 80ohm connect at node c. The 56ohm, 210ohm, and j119ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, 80ohm, and j119ohm connect at node e. Voltage V drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=130~$\Omega$]							(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [C, l_=$-$j31.8~$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=80~$\Omega$, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]										(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=210~$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=56~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j119~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh current <m>\vec{I_A}</m> will flow clockwise around the left mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_B}</m> will flow clockwise around the upper-right mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_C}</m> will flow clockwise around the lower-right mesh. Derive the mesh equations.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>12 \amp= 130\vec{I_A} - j159.15(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B}) + 80(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_C})</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -j159.15(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_A}) + 56\vec{I_B} + 210(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_C})</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 80(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_A}) + 210(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_B}) + j119.38\vec{I_C}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_A}</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_B}</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_C}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								210-j159.15	\amp j159.15		\amp -80		\amp 12	\\
								j159.15		\amp 266-j159.15	\amp -210		\amp 0	\\
								-80			\amp -210		\amp 290+j119.38	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve the matrix for <m>\vec{I_C}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{I_C} = 22.68-j35.74~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (j119.38~\Omega)(\vec{I_C})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (j0.11938~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.68-j35.74~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.27+j2.71~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.05~\textrm{V} \angle 32.40^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 5.05~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi200t + 32.4 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4-diagram" />. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 10~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi5000t - 50\pi/180)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 20ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor 60ohm resistor, 1.4uF capacitor, and 2*v(t) VCVS. The high-potential end of Vac and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, 50ohm, and 1.4uF connect at node b. The 1.4uF, 20ohm, and 60ohm connect at node c. The 20ohm and high-potential end of VCVS connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, 50ohm, and 60ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [C, l^=1.4~$\mu$F]						(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l_=2$v(t)$, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]									(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=60~$\Omega$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 8.09~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi5000t -35.6 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi5000)(1.4 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j22.7364~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 10~\cos{-50^\circ} + j10~\sin{-50^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.4279 - j7.6604~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 20ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor 60ohm resistor, -j22.7ohm impedance, and 2*V VCVS. The high-potential end of Vac and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, 50ohm, and -j22.7ohm connect at node b. The -j22.7ohm, 20ohm, and 60ohm connect at node c. The 20ohm and high-potential end of VCVS connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, 50ohm, and 60ohm connect at node e. Voltage V drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]							(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [C, l^=$-$j22.7~$\Omega$]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l_=2$\vec{V}$, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]										(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=60~$\Omega$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh current <m>\vec{I_A}</m> will flow clockwise around the left mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_B}</m> will flow clockwise around the center mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_C}</m> will flow clockwise around the right mesh. Derive the mesh equations.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 20\vec{I_A} + 50(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 50(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_A}) -j22.7364\vec{I_B} + 20(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_C}) + 2\vec{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -2\vec{V} + 20(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_B}) + 60\vec{I_C}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation for the dependent source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{V} = 50(\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</md>
					</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_A}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_B}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_C}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								70			\amp -50			\amp 0			\amp 0		\amp 6.4279 - j7.6604		\\
								-50			\amp 70-j22.7364	\amp -20		\amp 2		\amp 0		\\
								0			\amp -20			\amp 80			\amp -2		\amp 0		\\
								-50			\amp 50				\amp 0			\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve the matrix for <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= 6.5722 - j4.7139~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.0880~\textrm{V} \angle -35.6497^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 8.09~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi5000t -35.6 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>


				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5-diagram" />. The source voltage is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 15~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 600t + 25\pi/180)</m>. The proportionality constant of the VCCS has a unit of A/V.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, 60ohm resistor, 5.3uF capacitor, 6.6uF capacitor, and 0.3*v(t) VCCS. The high-potential end of Vac, 30ohm, and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm and 5.3uF connect at node b. The 30ohm, 60ohm, and VCCS connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The 5.3uF, 60ohm, and 6.6uF connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, VCCS, and 6.6uF connect at node e. The VCCS flows into node e. Voltage v(t) drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.3$v(t)$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=6.6~$\mu$F]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]									(0,4.5);
									\draw[]	(0,4.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$, v_=$v(t)$]				(2.5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4.5) to [C, l^=5.3~$\mu$F]							(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]									(5,4.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 15.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi600t + 122 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C6.6}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 600)(6.6 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j40.1906~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C5.3}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 600)(5.3 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j50.0487~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 15~\cos{25^\circ} + j15~\sin{25^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.5946 + j6.3393~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-mesh-analysis-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, 60ohm resistor, -j50ohm impedance, -j40.2ohm impedance, and 0.3*V VCCS. The high-potential end of Vac, 30ohm, and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm and -j50ohm connect at node b. The 30ohm, 60ohm, and VCCS connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The -j50ohm, 60ohm, and -j40.2ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vac, VCCS, and -j40.2ohm connect at node e. The VCCS flows into node e. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.3$\vec{V}$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j40.2~$\Omega$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]									(0,4.5);
									\draw[]	(0,4.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]			(2.5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4.5) to [C, l^=$-$j50~$\Omega$]					(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]									(5,4.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh current <m>\vec{I_A}</m> will flow clockwise around the upper mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_B}</m> will flow clockwise around the lower-left mesh. Mesh current <m>\vec{I_C}</m> will flow clockwise around the lower-right mesh. The two bottom meshes comprise a supermesh. Derive the mesh and supermesh KVL equations.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= (20-j50.05)\vec{I_A} + 60 (\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_C}) + 30 (\vec{I_A}-\vec{I_B})</mrow>
							<mrow>13.59+j6.34 \amp= 30(\vec{I_B}-\vec{I_A}) + 60(\vec{I_C}-\vec{I_A}) -j40.19\vec{I_C}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Relate the supermesh mesh currents to the branch current.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \vec{I_B} - 0.3\vec{V} - \vec{I_C}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation for the dependent source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\vec{V} = 20\vec{I_A}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_A}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_B}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_C}</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								110-j50.05	\amp -30		\amp -60		\amp 0		\amp 0			\\
								-90			\amp 30			\amp 60-j40.19	\amp 0		\amp 13.59+j6.34	\\
								0			\amp 1			\amp -1			\amp -0.3	\amp 0			\\
								-20			\amp 0			\amp 0			\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve the matrix for <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= -8.43+j13.42~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.84~\textrm{V} \angle 122.13^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 15.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi600t + 122 \pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Complex Superposition</title>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise2-diagram" />. The frequency of operation is 40<nbsp />Hz.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-superposition-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and three nodes consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 5ohm resistor, -j20/9ohm impedance, j5ohm impedance, 20ohm resistor, and (100V angle -90deg) voltage source. The high-potential end of the current source, 5ohm, -j20/9ohm, j5ohm, and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm and high-potential end of the voltage source connect at node b. The low-potential end of both sources, 5ohm, -j20/9ohm, j5ohm connect at node c. Voltage V drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(8.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j20/9~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(8.25,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, *-*]								(8.25,0);
									\draw[]	(8.25,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]									(11.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(11.25,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~$-$90$^\circ$]	(11.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(11.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 31.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 40t - 71.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit can be simplified before starting the superposition process. The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance as well as with the <minus />20/9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{5~\Omega}+\frac{1}{-j20/9~\Omega}+\frac{1}{j5~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.9512 - j2.4390~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance, 20ohm resistor, and (100V angle -90deg) voltage source. The high-potential end of the current source and both impedances connect at node a. The 20ohm resistor and high-potential end of the voltage source connect at node b. The low-potential end of both sources and 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance connect at node c. Voltage V drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.95$-$j2.44~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~$-$90$^\circ$]		(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(6.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 10<nbsp />A source. Deactivate the 100<nbsp />V source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance, and 20ohm resistor. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source connects to node a. Voltage V1 drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.95$-$j2.44~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_1}$, *-*]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [short]												(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Z_{EQ1} \amp= \frac{(1.9512 - j2.4390~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{(1.9512 - j2.4390~\Omega)+(20~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2-j2~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_1} \amp= (10~\textrm{A})(2-j2~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20-j20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 100<nbsp />V source. Deactivate the 10<nbsp />A source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance, 20ohm resistor, and (100V angle -90deg) voltage source. All three circuit elements connect in series. Voltage V2 drops over the 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.95$-$j2.44~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_2}$]		(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(6.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.75,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~$-$90$^\circ$]	(6.75,0);
									\draw[]	(3,0) to [short]													(6.75,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_2} \amp= (-j100~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{1.9512 - j2.4390~\Omega}{21.95-j2.44~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -10-j10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= \vec{V_1}+\vec{V_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (20-j20~\textrm{V}) + (-10-j10~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10-j30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 31.6228~\textrm{V} \angle -71.5651^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 31.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 40t - 71.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V}</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise1-diagram" />.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-superposition-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a (5V angle 15deg) voltage source, 20ohm resistor, j15ohm impedance, -j40ohm impedance, and (30V angle 0deg) voltage source. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 20ohm connect at node a. The three impedances connect at node b. The -j40ohm and high-potential end of the 30V source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources and j15ohm impedance connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=5~V~$\angle$~15$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j15~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=30~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>-5.16 + j12.0~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 5<nbsp />V source. Deactivate the 30<nbsp />V source.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a (5V angle 15deg) voltage source, 20ohm resistor, j15ohm impedance, and -j40ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 20ohm connect at node a. The three impedances connect at node b. The -j40ohm, low-potential end of the 5V source, and j15ohm impedance connect at node dc. Voltage V1 drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=5~V~$\angle$~15$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j15~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_1}$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]													(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The j15<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the <minus />j40<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(j15~\Omega)(-j40~\Omega)}{(j15~\Omega)+(-j40~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j24~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of (5V angle 15deg) voltage source, 20ohm resistor, and j24ohm impedance. All circuit elements connect in series. Voltage V1 drops over the j24ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=5~V~$\angle$~15$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j24~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_1}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>.</p>
						
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_1} \amp= (5~\textrm{V} \angle 15^\circ)\left(\frac{j24~\Omega}{j24~\Omega+20~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.2138 + j3.1390~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 30<nbsp />V source. Deactivate the 5<nbsp />V source.</p>


					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a 20ohm resistor, j15ohm impedance, -j40ohm impedance, and (30V angle 0deg) voltage source. The three impedances connect at node a. The -j40ohm and high-potential end of the 30V source connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 20ohm, and j15ohm impedance connect at node c. Voltage V2 drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j15~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_2}$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=30~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The j15<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z''} \amp= \frac{(j15~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{(j15~\Omega)+(20~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.2+j9.6~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of (30V angle 0deg) voltage source, -j40ohm impedance, and 7.2+j15ohm impedance. All circuit elements are connected in series. Voltage V2 drops over the 7.2+j15ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=7.2+j15~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V_2}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=30~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_2} \amp= (30~\textrm{V} \angle 0^\circ)\left(\frac{7.2+j9.6~\Omega}{-32.8+j9.6~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -7.3770 + j8.8525~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= \vec{V_1}+\vec{V_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (2.21+j3.14~\textrm{V}) + (-7.38+j8.85~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -5.16 + j12.0~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise3-diagram" />. The frequency of operation is 100<nbsp />Hz.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-superposition-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of (100-j50V) source, 20ohm resistor, j5ohm impedance, 12ohm resistor, j16ohm impedance, -j10ohm impedance, and (30+j20A) source. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, j5ohm, and 12ohm connect at node b. The 12ohm and j16ohm connect at node c. The j16ohm, -j10ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, j5ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node e. Voltage V drops form node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100$-$j50~V]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=j16~$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.25) to [open, v^=$\vec{V}$]						(7.5,2.25);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]									(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=30+j20~A]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 434~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t - 134\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and j16<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of (100-j50V) source, 20ohm resistor, j5ohm impedance, 12+j16ohm impedance, -j10ohm impedance, and (30+j20A) source. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, j5ohm, and 12+j16ohm connect at node b. The 12+j16ohm, -j10ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, j5ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops form node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100$-$j50~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=12+j16~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=30+j20~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 100<minus />j50<nbsp />V voltage source. Deactivate the current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of (100-j50V) source, 20ohm resistor, j5ohm impedance, 12+j16ohm impedance, and -j10ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, j5ohm, and 12+j16ohm connect at node b. The 12+j16ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of the voltage source, j5ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node d. Voltage V1 drops form node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100$-$j50~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=12+j16~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V_1}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_1}</m>. (Refer to <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> for information on solving a two-mesh voltage divider circuit.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_1} \amp= (100-j50~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{j5~\Omega//(12+j6~\Omega)}{j5~\Omega//(12+j6~\Omega)+20~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{12+j16~\Omega}{12+j6~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (100-j50~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{1.13+j3.96~\Omega}{21.13+j3.96~\Omega}\right)\left(1.33+j0.67\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (100-j50~\textrm{V})(0.09+j0.17)\left(1.33+j0.67\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.29+j28.57~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>, the voltage contributed by the 30+j20<nbsp />A current source. Deactivate the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of 20ohm resistor, j5ohm impedance, 12+j16ohm impedance, -j10ohm impedance, and (30+j20A) source. The 20ohm, j5ohm, and 12+j16ohm connect at node b. The 12+j16ohm, -j10ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of the current source, 20ohm, j5ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node d. Voltage V2 drops form node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j5~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=12+j16~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V_2}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=30+j20~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V'} \amp= \frac{(20~\Omega)(j5~\Omega)}{(20~\Omega)+(j5~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.1765 + j4.7059~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise3-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of 1.18+j4.71ohm impedance, 12+j16ohm impedance, -j10ohm impedance, and (30+j20A) source. The 1.18+4.71ohm and 12+j16ohm connect at node a. The 12+j16ohm, -j10ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node b. The low-potential end of the current source, 1.18+j4.71ohm, and -j10ohm connect at node c. Voltage V2 drops form node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=1.18+j4.71~$\Omega$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l^=12+j16~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V_2}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j10~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=30+j20~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{((1.1765 + j4.7059~\Omega)+(12+j16~\Omega))(-j10~\Omega)}{((1.1765 + j4.7059~\Omega)+(12+j16~\Omega))+(-j10~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.5714 - j13.7143~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the current through the branch containing the 12+j16<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= (30+j20~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{4.5714 - j13.7143}{13.18+j20.71~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2-j21.14~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V_2}</m>. Note that the polarity opposes the current source, which is why it is multiplied by negative one.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_2} \amp= -(12+j16~\Omega)(-2-j21.14~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -314.29+j285.71~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= \vec{V_1}+\vec{V_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (14.29+j28.57~\textrm{V}) + (-314.29+j285.71~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -300 + 314.29~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 434.4830~\textrm{V} \angle 133.6678^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 434~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 100t - 134\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise4-diagram" />. The current source has a value of <m>i_{AC}(t) = 5~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi2000t)</m> and the voltage source has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 20~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi2000t + 90\pi/180)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-superposition-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, 20uF capacitor, 10uF capacitor, 310uH inductor, and 12ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Iac, 10uF, and 20uF connect at node a. The 10uF, 310uH, and 12ohm connect at node b. The 20uF, 12ohm, and high-potential end of Vac connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources and 310uH connect at node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 310uH from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=10~$\mu$F]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=310~$\mu$H, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=20~$\mu$F]								(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]									(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]									(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 2.23~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi 2000t + 64.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of both capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C20}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 2000)(20 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j3.9789~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C10}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 2000)(10 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j7.9577~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi2000)(330 \times 10^{-6})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j3.8956~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert both sources to phasors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 20~\cos{90^\circ} + j20~\sin{90^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 5~\cos{0^\circ} + j5~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, voltage source Vac, -j3.98ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, j3.9ohm impedance, and 12ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Iac, -j7.96ohm, and -j3.98ohm connect at node a. The -j7.96ohm, j3.9ohm, and 12ohm connect at node b. The -j3.98ohm, 12ohm, and high-potential end of Vac connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources and j3.9ohm connect at node d. Branch current I flows through the j3.9ohm from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j3.98~$\Omega$]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]										(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]										(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the current contributed by the voltage source. Deactivate the current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, -j3.98ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, j3.9ohm impedance, and 12ohm resistor. The -j7.96ohm and -j3.98ohm connect at node a. The -j7.96ohm, j3.9ohm, and 12ohm connect at node b. The -j3.98ohm, 12ohm, and high-potential end of Vac connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vac and j3.9ohm connect at node d. Branch current I1 flows through the j3.9ohm from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, *-, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j3.98~$\Omega$]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -]									(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]									(5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j7.96<nbsp />&#8486; and <minus />j3.98<nbsp />&#8486; impedances are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, -j11.9ohm impedance, 12ohm resistor, and j3.9ohm impedance. The j3.9ohm, -j11.9ohm, and 12ohm connect at node a. The -j11.9ohm, 12ohm, and high-potential end of Vac connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vac and j3.9ohm connect at node c. Branch current I1 flows through the j3.9ohm from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$, *-*]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=$-$j11.9~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and <minus />j11.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(12~\Omega)(-j11.9366~\Omega)}{(12~\Omega)+(-j11.9366~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.9682 - j6~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with voltage source Vac, 5.97-j6ohm impedance, and j3.9ohm impedance, all connected in series. Branch current I1 flows through the circuit in the direction of high source potential to low source potential.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=5.97$-$j6~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{I_1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp= \frac{j20~\textrm{V}}{(5.9682 - j6~\Omega)+(j3.8956~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1.0509 + j2.9805~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the current contributed by the current source. Deactivate the voltage source. A new branch current called <m>\vec{I_X}</m> is introduced to assist in the analysis of this circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Iac, -j3.98ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, j3.9ohm impedance, and 12ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Iac, -j7.96ohm, and -j3.98ohm connect at node a. The -j7.96ohm, j3.9ohm, and 12ohm connect at node b. The -j3.98ohm, 12ohm, low-potential end of Iac, and j3.9ohm connect at node c. Branch current I2 flows through the j3.9ohm from node b to node c. Branch current Ix flows through the -j7.96ohm impedance from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_X}$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j3.98~$\Omega$]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-]										(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-]										(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the j3.8<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. This will remove <m>\vec{I_2}</m> from the circuit. Once we calculate <m>\vec{I_X}</m>, we will come back to solve for <m>\vec{I_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z''} \amp= \frac{(12~\Omega)(j3.8956~\Omega)}{(12~\Omega)+(j3.8956~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.1441 + j3.5242~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source Iac connected in parallel with a -j3.98ohm impedance connected in parallel with the series combination of -j7.96ohm impedance and 1.14+j3.52ohm impedance. Branch current Ix flows through the branch with the two impedances.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_X}$, *-]	(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.14+j3.52~$\Omega$, -*]			(3,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [C, l^=$-$j3.98~$\Omega$]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j7.96<nbsp />&#8486; and 1.14+j3.52<nbsp />&#8486; impedances are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source Iac, 1.14-j4.43ohm impedance, and -j3.98ohm impedance, all connected in parallel. Branch current Ix flows through the branch with the 1.14-j4.43ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]														(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.14$-$j4.43~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I_X}$, *-*]		(3,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j3.98~$\Omega$]									(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(5.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(1.1441 - j4.4336~\Omega)(-j3.9789~\Omega)}{(1.1441 - j4.4336~\Omega)+(-j3.9789~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.2513 - j2.1311~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I_X}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_X} \amp= (5~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{0.2513 - j2.1311~\Omega}{1.1441 - j4.4336~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.3219 - 0.3158~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equivalent circuit consisting of the 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and j3.9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise4-figure8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of current source Ix, j3.9ohm impedance, and 12ohm resistor, all connected in parallel. Branch current I2 flows through the branch with the j3.9ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{X}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j3.9~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ2}} \amp= \frac{(j3.8956~\Omega)(12~\Omega)}{(j3.8956~\Omega)+(12~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.1441 + j3.5242~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I_2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= (2.3219 - 0.3158~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{1.1441 + j3.5242~\Omega}{j3.8956~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.0078 - j0.9676~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= \vec{I_1}+\vec{I_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-1.05+j2.98~\textrm{A}) + (2.01-j0.97~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.9569 + j2.0130~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.2288~\textrm{A} \angle 64.5747^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 2.23~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi 2000t + 64.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise5-diagram" />. The frequency of operation is 20<nbsp />Hz.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-superposition-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and six nodes consisting of (5V angle 0deg) voltage source, 200ohm resistor, j80ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, (10V angle 60deg) voltage source, 120ohm resistor, and (600mA angle 45deg) current source. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 200ohm connect at node a. The 200ohm, j80ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm, 100ohm, and j60ohm connect at node c. The 100ohm and high-potential end of the 10V source connect at node d. The j60ohm, 120ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node e. The low-potential end of all three sources, j80ohm, and 120ohm connect at node f. Branch current I flows through the j80ohm from node b to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=5~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]											(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=10~V~$\angle$~60$^\circ$, -*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l^=j60~$\Omega$]											(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$, *-*]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [short]														(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=600~mA~$\angle$~45$^\circ$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 152~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi 20t - 8.72\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I_1}</m>, the current contributed by the 5<nbsp />V source. Deactivate both other sources.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of (5V angle 0deg) voltage source, 200ohm resistor, j80ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, and 120ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 200ohm connect at node a. The 200ohm, j80ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm, 100ohm, and j60ohm connect at node c. The j60ohm and 120ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of the voltage source, j80ohm, and 120ohm connect at node f. Branch current I1 flows through the j80ohm from node b to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=5~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_1}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=j60~$\Omega$]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance as seen by the j80<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= ((((j60~\Omega)+(120~\Omega)) // 100~\Omega) + 150~\Omega) // j80~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (((120+j60~\Omega) // 100~\Omega) + 150~\Omega) // j80~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= ((57.6923 + j11.5385~\Omega) + (150~\Omega)) // j80~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (207.69 + j11.538~\Omega) // j80~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 25.8026 + j68.6277~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the voltage dropped over this equivalent impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V'} \amp= (5~\textrm{V})\frac{25.8026 + j68.6277~\Omega}{(200~\Omega)+(25.8026 + j68.6277~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.9458 + j1.2322~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{I_1}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp= \frac{0.9458 + j1.2322~\textrm{V}}{j80~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0154 - j0.0118~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.4021 - j11.8231~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I_2}</m>, the current contributed by the 10<nbsp />V source. Deactivate the other sources.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of 200ohm resistor, j80ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, (10V angle 60deg) voltage source, and 120ohm resistor. The 200ohm, j80ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm, 100ohm, and j60ohm connect at node c. The 100ohm and high-potential end of the 10V source connect at node d. The j60ohm and 120ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 200ohm, j80ohm, and 120ohm connect at node f. Branch current I2 flows through the j80ohm from node b to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]														(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]											(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=10~V~$\angle$~60$^\circ$, -*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l^=j60~$\Omega$]											(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$]											(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 120<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and j60<nbsp />&#8486; impedance are connected in series.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of 200ohm resistor, j80ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, (10V angle 60deg) voltage source, and 120+j60ohm impedance. The 200ohm, j80ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm, 100ohm, and 120+j60ohm connect at node c. The 100ohm and high-potential end of the 10V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 200ohm, j80ohm, and 120+j60ohm connect at node f. Branch current I2 flows through the j80ohm from node b to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]														(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]											(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=10~V~$\angle$~60$^\circ$, -*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]														(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [generic, l_=120+j60~$\Omega$]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit is equivalent to above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~V~$\angle$~60$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=120+j60~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_2}$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]													(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance as seen by the j80<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z''} \amp= \frac{(j80~\Omega)(200~\Omega)}{(j80~\Omega)+(200~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 27.5862 + j68.9655~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of (10V angle 60deg) voltage source, 100ohm resistor, 120+j60ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, and 27.6+j69ohm impedance. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 100ohm connect at node a. The 100ohm, 120+j60ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm and 27.6+j69ohm connect at node c. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 120+j60ohm, and 27.6+j69ohm connect at node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~V~$\angle$~60$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=120+j60~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=27.6+j69~$\Omega$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the voltage dropped over the j80<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. (Refer to <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> for information on solving a two-mesh voltage divider circuit.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V''} \amp= (10~\textrm{V} \angle 60^\circ)\left(\frac{(120+j60~\Omega) // (150~\Omega + (27.5862 + j68.9655~\Omega))}{100~\Omega + ((120+j60~\Omega) // (150~\Omega + (27.5862 + j68.9655~\Omega)))}\right)\left(\frac{(27.5862 + j68.9655~\Omega)}{150~\Omega + (27.5862 + j68.9655~\Omega)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.8951 + j1.5124~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{I_2}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= \frac{-0.8951 + j1.5124~\textrm{V}}{j80~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0189 + j0.0112~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18.9046 + j11.1888~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I_3}</m> the current contributed by the current source. Deactivate the other two sources.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise5-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and four nodes consisting of 200ohm resistor, j80ohm impedance, 150ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, 12ohm resistor, and (600mA angle 45deg) current source. The 200ohm, j80ohm, and 150ohm connect at node b. The 150ohm, 100ohm, and j60ohm connect at node c. The j60ohm, 120ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node e. The low-potential end of the current source, 200ohm, j80ohm, and 120ohm connect at node f. Branch current I3 flows through the j80ohm from node b to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]														(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j80~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I_3}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=150~$\Omega$]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=j60~$\Omega$]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=120~$\Omega$, *-*]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]													(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l^=600~mA~$\angle$~45$^\circ$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a four-mesh circuit and will use a current divider approach similar to what was explained in <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example" />.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_3} \amp= (600~\textrm{mA} \angle 45^\circ)\left(\frac{((((200~\Omega // j80~\Omega) + 150~\Omega) // 100~\Omega) + j60~\Omega) // 120~\Omega}{((((200~\Omega // j80~\Omega) + 150~\Omega) // 100~\Omega) + j60~\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{((200~\Omega // j80~\Omega) + 150~\Omega) // 100~\Omega}{((200~\Omega // j80~\Omega) + 150~\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{200~\Omega // j80~\Omega}{j80~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 115.75 - j22.385~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\vec{I}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= \vec{I_1}+\vec{I_2} +\vec{I_3}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (15.40-j11.82~\textrm{mA}) + (18.90+j11.19~\textrm{mA}) + (115.75-j22.38~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 150.05-j23.019~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 151.8077~\textrm{mA} \angle -8.7216^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 152~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi 20t - 8.72\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Complex Source Transformation</title>
		
			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>v(t)</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise2-diagram" /> (from the complex superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 31.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 40t - 71.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit can be simplified before starting the source transformation process. The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance as well as with the <minus />20/9<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{5~\Omega}+\frac{1}{-j20/9~\Omega}+\frac{1}{j5~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.9512 - j2.4390~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance, 20ohm resistor, and (100V angle -90deg) voltage source. The high-potential end of the current source, 1.95-j2.44ohm, and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm and high-potential end of the voltage source connect at node b. The low-potential end of both sources connect to the 1.95-j2.44ohm at node c. Voltage V drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.95$-$j2.44~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]										(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=100~V~$\angle$~$-$90$^\circ$]		(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(6.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_S} \amp= \frac{-j100~\textrm{V}}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and two nodes consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 1.95-j2.44ohm impedance, 20ohm resistor, and -j5A current source. All four circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of both sources connect to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(8,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=1.95$-$j2.44~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]								(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$-$j5~A]					(8,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the 1.95<minus />j2.44<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(20~\Omega)(1.95-j2.44~\Omega)}{(20~\Omega)+(1.95-j2.44~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2-j2~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise1-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of (10A angle 0deg) current source, 2-j2ohm impedance, and -j5A current source. All three circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of both sources connect to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~A~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=2$-$j2~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$-$j5~A]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two current sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{TOTAL}} \amp= (10~\textrm{A} \angle 0^\circ) + (j5~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (10~\textrm{A}) + (j5~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10+j5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise1-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of 10+j5A current source and 2-j2ohm impedance. Both circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source connects to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10+j5~A]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=2$-$j2~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (10-j5~\textrm{A})(2-j2~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10-j30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 31.62~\textrm{V} \angle -71.57^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 31.6~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi40t - 71.6\pi/180)</md>
					</p>

					<p>This answer should be (and is) identical to the answer from <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise2" />.</p>
						
				</solution>
			</exercise>


			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-superposition-exercise1-diagram" /> (from the complex superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>-5.16 + j12.0~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Convert both sources to current sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{S1}} \amp= frac{5\cos(15^\circ)+j5\sin(15^\circ)~\textrm{V}}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.24+j0.06~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{S2}} \amp= \frac{30~\textrm{V}}{-j40~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j0.75~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and two nodes consisting of 0.24+j0.06A current source, 20ohm resistor, j15ohm impedance, -j40ohm impedance, and j0.75A current source. All circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of both sources connect to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=0.24+j0.06~A]	(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j15~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j40~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j0.75~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The three impedances are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{20~\Omega}+\frac{1}{j15~\Omega}+\frac{1}{-j40~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 11.8033 + j9.8361~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Combine the two current sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{TOTAL}} \amp= (0.24+j0.06~\textrm{A}) + (j0.75~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.2415 + j0.8147~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of 0.24+j0.81A current source and 11.8+j9.84ohm impedance. All circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source connects to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=0.24+j0.81~A]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=11.8+j9.84~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(3,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(3,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(3,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (0.24+j0.81~\textrm{A})(11.80+j9.84~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -5.16+j11.99~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This answer should be (and is) identical to the answer from <xref ref="ch9-complex-superposition-exercise1" />.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>\vec{I}</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-source-transformation-exercise3-diagram" />.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-source-transformation-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of (20V angle 0deg) voltage source, 6ohm resistor, -j5ohm impedance, j4ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm impedance, and (30V angle 0deg) voltage source. The high-potential end of the 20V source and 6ohm connect at node a. The 6ohm, -j5ohm, and j4ohm connect at node b. The j4ohm, 11ohm, and -j7ohm connect at node c. The -j7ohm and high-potential end of the 30V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, -j5ohm, and 11ohm connect at node e. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=20~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j5~$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4~$\Omega$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j7~$\Omega$]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=30~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>288-j60.4~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Convert both voltage sources into current sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_1} \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{6~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.33~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{I_2} \amp= \frac{30~\textrm{V}}{-j7~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j4.28~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five meshes and three nodes consisting of 3.33A source, 6ohm resistor, -j5ohm impedance, j4ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm impedance, and j4.28A source. The high-potential end of the 3.33A source, 6ohm, -j5ohm, and j4ohm connect at node a. The j4ohm, 11ohm, -j7ohm, and high-potential end of the j4.28A source connect at node b. The low-potential end of both sources, 6ohm, -j5ohm, 11ohm, and -j7ohm connect at node c. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=3.33~A]		(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-*]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j5~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j4.28~A]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the <minus />j5<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(6~\Omega)(-j5~\Omega)}{(6~\Omega)+(-j5~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.4590 - j2.9508~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five meshes and three nodes consisting of 3.33A source, 2.46-j2.95ohm impedance, j4ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm impedance, and j4.28A source. The high-potential end of the 3.33A source, 2.46-j2.95ohm, and j4ohm connect at node a. The j4ohm, 11ohm, -j7ohm, and high-potential end of the j4.28A source connect at node b. The low-potential end of both sources, 2.46-j2.95ohm, 11ohm, and -j7ohm connect at node c. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=3.33~A]		(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]									(0.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=2.46$-$j2.95~$\Omega$, *-*]	(0.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4~$\Omega$]						(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]		(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [short]										(8,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j4.28~A]		(8,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]										(8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the 3.33<nbsp />A source to a voltage source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_S} \amp= (3.33~\textrm{A})(2.46-j2.95~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.20-j9.84~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of 8.20-j9.84V source, 2.46-j2.95ohm impedance, j4ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm resistor, and j4.28A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to the 2.46-j2.95ohm at node a. The 2.46-j2.95ohm and j4ohm connect at node b. The j4ohm, 11ohm, -j7ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, 11ohm, and -j7ohm connect at node d. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=8.20$-$j9.84~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=2.46$-$j2.95~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=j4~$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j4.28~A]			(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2.46<minus />j2.95<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in series with the j4<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of 8.20-j9.84V source, 2.46+j1.05ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm resistor, and j4.28A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to the 2.46+j1.05ohm at node a. The 2.46+j1.05ohm, 11ohm, -j7ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, 11ohm, and -j7ohm connect at node d. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=8.20$-$j9.84~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=2.46+j1.05~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j4.28~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source back to a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_S} \amp= \frac{8.20-j9.84~\textrm{V}}{2.46+j1.05~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.38-j4.59~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and two nodes consisting of a 1.38-j4.59A source, 2.46+j1.05ohm impedance, 11ohm resistor, -j7ohm impedance, and j4.28A source. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of both sources connect to node a. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.38$-$j4.59~A]	(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=2.46+j1.05~$\Omega$, *-*]			(0.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$\vec{I}$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j4.28~A]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the current sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{TOTAL}} \amp= (1.38-j4.59~\textrm{A})+(j4.28~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.38-j0.30~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The 2.46+j1.05<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the <minus />j7<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z''} \amp= \frac{(-j7~\Omega)(2.46+j1.05~\Omega)}{(-j7~\Omega)+(2.46+j1.05~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.91+j0.03~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise3-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of a 1.38-j0.30A source, 2.91+j0.03ohm impedance, and 11ohm resistor. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source connects to node a. Branch current I flows through the 11ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.38$-$j0.30~A]		(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0.5,2.5) to [generic, l_=2.91+j0.03~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [R, l_=11~$\Omega$, i^=$\vec{I}$]					(3,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]											(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]												(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(2.91+j0.03~\Omega)(11~\Omega)}{(2.91+j0.03~\Omega)+(11~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.2989 + j0.0208~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{I}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I} \amp= (1.38-j0.30~\textrm{A})\left(\frac{j0.0208~\Omega}{11~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.29-j0.06~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 288-j60.4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-source-transformation-exercise4-diagram" />. The current source has a value of <m>i_{AC}(t) = 3~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi50000t)</m>. The voltage source has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 15~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50000t)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-source-transformation-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, 25uH inductor, 500nF capacitor, 12ohm resistor, 9ohm resistor, and voltage source Vac. The high-potential end of Iac, 25uH, and 500nF connect at node a. The 500nF, 12ohm, and 9ohm connect at node b. The 9ohm connects to the high-potential end of Vac at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, 25uH, and 12ohm connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops form node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=25~$\mu$H, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=500~nF, v_=$v(t)$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=9~$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 29.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50000t + 3.86\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi50000)(500 \times 10^{-9})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j6.3662~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi50000)(25 \times 10^{-6})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j7.8540~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert both sources to phasors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 3~\cos{0^\circ} +j3~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 15~\cos{0^\circ} +j15~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, j7.85ohm impedance, -j6.67ohm impedance, 12ohm resistor, 9ohm resistor, and voltage source Vac. The high-potential end of Iac, j7.85ohm, and -j6.67ohm connect at node a. The -j6.67ohm, 12ohm, and 9ohm connect at node b. The 9ohm connects to the high-potential end of Vac at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, j7.85ohm, and 12ohm connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops form node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j7.85~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j6.67~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=9~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the current source into a voltage source. Convert the voltage source into a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_S} \amp= \frac{15~\textrm{V}}{9~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.67~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_S} \amp= (3~\textrm{A})(j7.8540~\Omega)~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j23.56~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of j23.56V source, j7.85ohm impedance, -j6.67ohm impedance, 12ohm resistor, 9ohm resistor, and 1.67A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to j7.85ohm at node a. The j7.85ohm and -j6.67ohm connect at node b. The -j6.67ohm, 12ohm, 9ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, 12ohm, and 9ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j23.56~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=j7.85~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j6.67~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=9~$\Omega$, *-*]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.67~A]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the 9<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of j23.56V source, j7.85ohm impedance, -j6.67ohm impedance, 5.14ohm resistor, and 1.67A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to j7.85ohm at node a. The j7.85ohm and -j6.67ohm connect at node b. The -j6.67ohm, 5.14ohm, and high-potential end of the current source connect at node c. The low-potential end of both sources, and 5.14ohm connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j23.56~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=j7.85~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j6.67~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.14~$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.67~A]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the current source to a voltage source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{S2}} \amp= (1.67~\textrm{A})(5.14~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.57~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and five nodes consisting of j23.56V source, j7.85ohm impedance, -j6.67ohm impedance, 5.14ohm resistor, and 8.47V source. The high-potential end of the j23.56V source connects to j7.85ohm at node a. The j7.85ohm and -j6.67ohm connect at node b. The -j6.67ohm and 5.14ohm connect at node c. The 5.14ohm connects to the high-potential end of the 8.57V at node d. The low-potential end of both sources connect at node e. Voltage V drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=j23.56~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=j7.85~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j6.67~$\Omega$, v_=$\vec{V}$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=5.14~$\Omega$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=8.57~V]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two voltage sources.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{TOTAL}} \amp= (j23.56~\textrm{V}) - (8.57~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8.5714 + j23.5619~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise4-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with -8.57+j23.56V source, j7.85ohm impedance, -j6.67ohm impedance, and 5.14ohm resistance, all connected in series. Voltage V drops over the -j6.67ohm impedance.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$-$8.57+j23.56~V]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=j7.85~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j6.67~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=5.14~$\Omega$]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (-8.57+j23.56~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{-j6.37~\Omega}{(-j6.37~\Omega)+(5.14+j7.85~\Omega)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 29.7467 + 2.0049~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 29.8141~\textrm{V} \angle 3.8558^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 29.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50000t + 3.86\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-source-transformation-exercise5-diagram" />. The source voltage is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 3~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi100t)</m>. The proportionality constant of the VCCS has a unit of A/V.</p>

					<figure xml:id="complex-source-transformation-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 2ohm resistor, 1.6mF capacitor, 0.3mF capacitor, 2*v(t) VCCS, and 5ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 2ohm at node a. The 2ohm, 1.6mF, and 0.3mF connect at node b. The 0.3mF, VCCS, and 5ohm resistor connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The low-potential end of Vac, 1.6mF, VCCS, and 5ohm connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=1.6~mF, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=0.3~mF]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l^=2~$v(t)$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 576~\textrm{mV}~\cos(2\pi100t - 53.9\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of both capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C1.6}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi100)(0.0016)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j0.9947~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C0.3}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi100)(0.0003)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j5.3052~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert the independent source to a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 3~\cos{0^\circ} +j3~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 2ohm resistor, -j0.995ohm impedance, -j5.31ohm impedance, 2*V VCCS, and 5ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 2ohm at node a. The 2ohm, -j0.995ohm, and -j5.31ohm connect at node b. The -j5.31ohm, VCCS, and 5ohm resistor connect at node c. The VCCS flows out of node c. The low-potential end of Vac, -j0.995ohm, VCCS, and 5ohm connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Voltage V drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j0.995~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l^=2~$\vec{V}$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source into a current source. Convert the VCCS into a VCVS.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_S} \amp= \frac{3~\textrm{V}}{2~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_S} \amp= (-2\vec{V})(5)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -10\vec{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of 1.5A source, 2ohm resistor, -j0.995ohm impedance, -j5.31ohm impedance, 5ohm resistor, and 10*V VCVS. The high-potential end of the current source, 2ohm, -j0.995ohm, and -j5.31ohm connect at node a. The -j5.31ohm and 5ohm connect at node b. The 5ohm and low-potential end of the VCVS connect at node c. The low-potential end of the current source, 2ohm, -j0.995ohm, and high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node d. Voltage V drops from node a to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.5~A]			(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j0.995~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cV, l^=10~$\vec{V}$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in parallel with the <minus />j0.995<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the <minus />5.31<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(2~\Omega)(-j0.995~\Omega)}{(2~\Omega)+(-j0.995~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.3966 - j0.7975~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 1.5A source, 0.4-j0.8ohm impedance, 5-j5.31ohm impedance, and 10*V VCVS. The high-potential end of the current source and both impedances connect at node a. The 5-j5.31ohm connects to the low-potential end of the VCVS at node b. The low-potential end of the current source, 0.4-j0.8ohm impedance, and high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node c. Voltage V drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.5~A]				(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l_=0.4-j0.8~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=5-j5.31~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cV, l^=10~$\vec{V}$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]												(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]											(0,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the dependent source to a VCCS. The unit of the proportionality constant is A/V.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_S} \amp= \frac{10\vec{V}}{5-j5.31}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.94+j1.00)\vec{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and two nodes consisting of a 1.5A source, 0.4-j0.8ohm impedance, 5-j5.31ohm impedance, and (0.94-j1)*V VCCS. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the 1.5A source connects to node a. The VCCS flows out of node a and into node b. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.5~A]				(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l_=0.4-j0.8~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=5-j5.31~$\Omega$, *-*]					(3,0);
									\draw[]	(6,2.5) to [cI, l_=(0.94+j1)$\vec{V}$]							(6,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]												(6,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]											(6,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two impedances are connected in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(0.3966 - j0.7975~\Omega)(5 - j5.3052~\Omega)}{(0.3966 - j0.7975~\Omega)+(5 - j5.3052~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.3774 - j0.7020~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Combine the two sources. (Note that the source is a current source, which may appear slightly unclear from the circuit diagram.)</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-source-transformation-exercise5-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of a (1.5-(0.94+j1)*V) source and 0.377-j0.70-2ohm impedance. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The high-potential end of the source connects to node a. Voltage V drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=1.5$-$(0.94+j1)$\vec{V}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(3.25,2.5) to [generic, l_=0.377$-$j0.702~$\Omega$, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]													(3.25,2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>\vec{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= (0.38-j0.70)(1.5 - (0.94 + j1) \vec{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.57-j1.05) - (1.06-j0.28)\vec{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>(2.06-j0.28)\vec{V} \amp= (0.57-j1.05)</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V} \amp= \frac{(0.57-j1.05)}{(2.06-j0.28)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.34-j0.47~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 339.57-j465.34~\textrm{mV}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 576.06~\textrm{mV} \angle -53.88^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Convert to time-varying from.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 576~\textrm{mV}~\cos(2\pi100t - 53.9\pi/180)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Complex Th&#233;venin and Norton Circuits</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-thevenin-exercise1-diagram" />. The source value is <m>v_{AC}(t) = 10~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi50t + 30\pi/180)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-thevenin-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 5ohm resistor, 25mH inductor, and 750uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vac and 5ohm connect at node c. The 5ohm, 25mH, and 750uF connect at node d. The 750uF floats at node a. The low-potential end of Vac and 25mH connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=25~mH, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=750~$\mu$F, -o]						(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise1-answer">
							<shortdescription>Thevenin equivalent circuit consisting of (8.44V angle 62.5deg) source in series with a 3.56ohm resistor and 1.61mF capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=8.44~V~$\angle$~62.5$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3.56~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=1.61~mF, -o]					(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi50)(750 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j4.2441~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi50)(0.025)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j7.8540~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 10~\cos{30^\circ} + j10~\sin{30^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.6603 + j5~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 5ohm resistor, j7.85ohm impedance, and -j4.24ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac and 5ohm connect at node c. The 5ohm, j7.85ohm, and -j4.24ohm connect at node d. The -j4.24ohm floats at node a. The low-potential end of Vac and j7.85ohm connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=8.66+j5~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=j7.85~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j4.24~$\Omega$, -o]				(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{TH}} \amp= (8.66+j5~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{j7.85~\Omega}{5+j7.85~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.90+j7.48~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.44~\textrm{V} \angle 62.48^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate the source and calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{TH}} \amp= (-j4.24~\Omega)+(5~\Omega)//(j7.85~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-j4.24~\Omega)+(3.56+j2.27~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.56-j1.98~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the value of the capacitance based on the Th&#233;venin equivalent reactance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C \amp= -\frac{1}{2\pi 50 (-1.98)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.61~\textrm{mF}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-thevenin-exercise2-diagram" />. The current source has a value of <m>i_{AC}(t) = 60~\textrm{mA}~\cos(2\pi 1500t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-thevenin-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, 50ohm resistor, 10mH inductor, 2uF capacitor, and 100ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Iac, 50ohm, and 2uF connect at node c. The 50ohm and 10mH connect at node d. The 2uF and 100ohm connect at node a. The low-potential end of Iac, 10mH, and 100ohm connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$i_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]									(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=10~mH, -*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=2~$\mu$F]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]								(6,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise2-answer">
							<shortdescription>Thevenin equivalent circuit consisting of (4.12V angle 46.7deg) source connected in series with a 38ohm resistor and 1.81mH inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=4.12~V~$\angle$~46.7$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=38~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=1.81~mH, -o]							(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi1500)(2 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j53.0516~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_L} \amp= j(2\pi1500)(0.01)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j94.2478~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_{AC}} \amp= 60~\cos{0^\circ} + j60~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 60~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Iac, 50ohm resistor, j94.2ohm impedance, -j53ohm impedance, and 100ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Iac, 50ohm, and -j53ohm connect at node c. The 50ohm and j94.2ohm connect at node d. The -j53ohm and 100ohm connect at node a. The low-potential end of Iac, j94.2ohm, and 100ohm connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{I_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]										(5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-]						(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [L, l_=j94.2~$\Omega$, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [C, l_=$-$j53~$\Omega$]						(5,2);
									\draw[]	(5,2) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2) to [short, -o]									(6,2) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{(50+j94.2~\Omega)(100-j53~\Omega)}{(50+j94.2~\Omega)+(100-j53~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 73.5207 + j24.9562~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the current flow through the branch including the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{I_X} \amp= 60~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{73.5207 + j24.9562~\Omega}{100-j53.05}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 28.2248 + j29.9474~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{TH}} \amp= (28.2248 + j29.9474~\textrm{mA})(0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.8225 + j2.9947~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.1152~\textrm{V} \angle 46.6962^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate the source and calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{TH}} \amp= (100~\Omega)//(50~\Omega+j94.25~\Omega-j53.05~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 38.0092 + j17.0252~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the value of the inductance based on the Th&#233;venin equivalent reactance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L \amp= \frac{17.03}{2 \pi 50}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.8064~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-thevenin-exercise3-diagram" />. The voltage source has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 50~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 2000t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-thevenin-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 10ohm resistor, 600uH inductor, 10uF capacitor, 6ohm resistor, and 400uH inductor. The high-potential end of Vac and 10ohm connect at node c. The 10ohm and 600uH connect at node d. The 600uH, 10uF, and 6ohm connect at node a. The 6ohm and 400uH connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, 10uF, and 400uH connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]							(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [L, l^=600~$\mu$H]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [C, l_=10~$\mu$F, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [short, -o]									(8.5,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-]						(7.5,2);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2) to [L, l_=400~$\mu$H, -*]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(8.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise3-answer">
							<shortdescription>Thevenin equivalent circuit consisting of a (24.7V angle -35.4deg) source connected in series with a 6.18ohm resistor and 13.5uH inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=24.7~V~$\angle$~$-$35.4$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6.18~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=13.5~$\mu$H, -o]			(5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]							(5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_C} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi2000)(10 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j7.9577~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the impedance of the inductors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L600}} \amp= j(2\pi 2000)(600\times10^{-6})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j7.5398~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{L400}} \amp= j(2\pi 2000)(400\times10^{-6})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= j5.0265~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 50~\cos{0^\circ} + j50~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 10ohm resistor, j7.54ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, 6ohm resistor, and j5.03ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac and 10ohm connect at node c. The 10ohm and j7.54ohm connect at node d. The j7.54ohm, -j7.96ohm, and 6ohm connect at node a. The 6ohm and j5.03ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vac, -j7.96ohm, and j5.03ohm connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]							(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [L, l^=j7.54~$\Omega$]						(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [C, l_=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [short, -o]									(8.5,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, *-]						(7.5,2);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2) to [L, l_=j5.03~$\Omega$, -*]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(8.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j7.54<nbsp />&#8486; impedance. The 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is connected in series with the j5.03<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 10+j7.54ohm impedance, -j7.96ohm impedance, and 6+j5.03ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac and 10+j7.54ohm connect at node c. All three impedances connect at node a. The low-potential end of Vac, -j7.96ohm, and 6+j5.03ohm connect at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=10+j7.54~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j7.96~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o]									(6,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, l_=6+j5.03~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <minus />j7.96<nbsp />&#8486; impedance is connected in parallel with the 6+j5.03<nbsp />&#8486; impedance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z'} \amp= \frac{(-j7.9577~\Omega)(6+j5.0265~\Omega)}{(-j7.9577~\Omega)+(6+j5.0265~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.5207 - j3.7951~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-thevenin-exercise3-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 10+j7.54ohm impedance, and 8.52-j3.8ohm impedance. The high-potential end of Vac connects to 10+j7.54ohm at node c. Both impedances connect at node a. The low-potential end of Vac connects to 8.52-j3.8ohm at node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, l^=10+j7.54~$\Omega$]					(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [generic, l_=8.52$-$j3.8~$\Omega$, *-*]			(3,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [short, -o]										(4,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(4,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>\vec{V_{TH}}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{TH}} \amp= 50~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{8.52-j3.80~\Omega}{(10+j7.54~\Omega)+(8.52-j3.80~\Omega)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20.11-j14.31~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24.68~\textrm{V} \angle -35.44^\circ</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Deactivate the source and calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent impedance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{TH}} \amp= (10+j7.54~\Omega)//(8.52-j3.80~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.18+j0.17~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-inductor" /> to calculate the value of the inductance based on the Th&#233;venin equivalent reactance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L \amp= \frac{0.17}{2 \pi 2000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.54~\mu\textrm{H}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-norton-exercise4-diagram" />. The proportionality constant of the VCCS has a unit of A/V. The source voltage is equal to <m>v_{AC}(t) = 3~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi60t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-norton-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 5ohm resistor, 500uF capacitor, 200uF capacitor, 0.3*v(t) VCCS, and 8ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Vac and 5ohm connect at node c. The 5ohm, 500uF, and 200uF connect at node d. The 200uF, VCCS, and 8ohm connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, 500uF, VCCS, and 8ohm connect at node b. The VCCS flows into node b. Voltage v(t) drops form node d to node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=500~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=200~$\mu$F]							(5.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.3~$v(t)$, *-*]					(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [short, -o]								(9.25,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(8.25,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*]					(8.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(9.25,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise4-answer">
							<shortdescription>Norton equivalent circuit consisting of (561mA angle 113deg) source connected in parallel with the series combination of a 4.07ohm resistor and 1.2mF capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=561~mA~$\angle$~113$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short, -o]										(3.5,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=4.07~$\Omega$, *-]						(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=1.2~mF, -*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]										(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C200}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 60)(200 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j13.2629~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C500}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 60)(500 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j5.3052~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 3~\cos{0^\circ} + j3~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 5ohm resistor, -j5.31ohm impedance, -j13.3ohm impedance, 0.3*V VCCS, and 8ohm resistor. The high-potential end of Vac and 5ohm connect at node c. The 5ohm, -j5.31ohm, and -j13.3ohm connect at node d. The -j13.3ohm, VCCS, and 8ohm connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, -j5.31ohm, VCCS, and 8ohm connect at node b. The VCCS flows into node b. Voltage V drops form node d to node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j13.3~$\Omega$]						(5.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.3~$\vec{V}$, *-*]				(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [short, -o]								(9.25,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(8.25,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*]					(8.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(9.25,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the two capacitors. All subsequent units are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V}}{5} = \frac{\vec{V}}{-j5.3052} + \frac{\vec{V}-\vec{V_{OC}}}{-j13.2629}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 8<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the VCCS and capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V}-\vec{V_{OC}}}{-j13.2629} = 0.3\vec{V} + \frac{\vec{V_{OC}}}{8}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V_{OC}}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.2 + j0.2639	\amp -j0.0754 			\amp 0.6\\
								0.3 - j0.0754 	\amp 0.1250 + j0.0754 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.7837 - j0.9414\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 0.2456 + j2.5840
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The open-circuit voltage is equal to 0.2456+j2.5840<nbsp />V.</p>

					<p>Short terminals a and b to calculate the Norton equivalent current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>ircuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vac, 5ohm resistor, -j5.31ohm impedance, -j13.3ohm impedance, 0.3*V VCCS, and a short. The high-potential end of Vac and 5ohm connect at node c. The 5ohm, -j5.31ohm, and -j13.3ohm connect at node d. The -j13.3ohm, VCCS, and short connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. The low-potential end of Vac, -j5.31ohm, VCCS, and short connect at node b. The VCCS flows into node b. Branch current In flows through the short from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=3~V]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$-$j13.4~$\Omega$]						(5.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [cI, l_=0.3~$\vec{V}$, *-*]					(5.75,0);
									\draw[]	(8.25,2.5) to [short, i_=$\vec{I_N}$]						(8.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(8.25,0);
									\draw[]	(5.75,2.5) to [short]										++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the two capacitors. All subsequent units are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V}}{5} = \frac{\vec{V}}{-j5.3052} + \frac{\vec{V}}{-j13.2629}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the VCCS and capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V}}{-j13.2629} = 0.3\vec{V} + \vec{I_N}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_N}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.2 + j0.2639	\amp 0 	\amp 0.6\\
								0.3 - j0.0754 	\amp 1 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 1.0945 - j1.4441\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp -0.2195 + j0.5158
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Norton equivalent current is equal to <minus />0.2195+j0.5158<nbsp />A.</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Z_N \amp= \frac{0.2456 + j2.5840~\textrm{V}}{-0.2195 + j0.5158~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.0703 - j2.2082</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the value of the capacitance based on the Th&#233;venin equivalent reactance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C \amp= -\frac{1}{2\pi 60 (2.2082)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.2~\textrm{mF}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="complex-norton-exercise5-diagram" />. The source voltage has a value of <m>v_{AC}(t) = 10~\textrm{V}~\cos(2\pi 300 t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="complex-norton-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-complex-thev-norton-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of (10V angle 0deg) voltage source, 6ohm resistor, 100uF capacitor, 20uF capacitor, 10ohm resistor, and 0.8*v(t) VCVS. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to 6ohm at node c. The 6ohm, 100uF, and 20uF connect at node d. The 20uF and 10ohm connect at node a. The 10ohm connects to the high-potential end of the VCVS at node e. The low-potential end of both sources and 100uF connect at node b. Voltage v(t) drops from node d to node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=10~V~$\angle$~0$^\circ$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l_=100~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l^=20~$\mu$F]					(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-]				(5,2);
									\draw[]	(5,2) to [cV, l_=0.8~$v(t)$, -*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [short, -o]							(6,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]							(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise5-answer">
							<shortdescription>Norton equivalent circuit consisting of a (525mA angle -28.4deg) source connected in parallel with the series combination of a 9.78ohm resistor and 188uF capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=525~mA~$\angle$~$-$28.4$^\circ$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short, -o]								(3.5,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=9.78~$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=188~$\mu$F, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]								(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the impedance of the capacitors.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C20}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 300)(20 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j26.5258~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{Z_{C100}} \amp= \frac{1}{j(2\pi 300)(100 \times 10^{-6})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -j5.3052~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Transform the source into a phasor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{V_{AC}} \amp= 10~\cos{0^\circ} + j10~\sin{0^\circ}~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of (10V angle 0deg) voltage source, 6ohm resistor, -j5.31ohm impedance, -j26.5ohm impedance, 10ohm resistor, and 0.8*V VCVS. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to 6ohm at node c. The 6ohm, -j5.31ohm, and -j26.5ohm connect at node d. The -j26.5ohm and 10ohm connect at node a. The 10ohm connects to the high-potential end of the VCVS at node e. The low-potential end of both sources and -j5.31ohm connect at node b. Voltage V drops from node d to node b. The Norton equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l_=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l^=$-$j26.5~$\Omega$]					(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-]						(5,2);
									\draw[]	(5,2) to [cV, l_=0.8~$\vec{V}$, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [short, -o]									(6,4) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]									(6,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the two capacitors. All subsequent units are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V}}{6} = \frac{\vec{V}}{-j5.3052} + \frac{\vec{V}-\vec{V_{OC}}}{-j26.5258}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the 20&#181;F capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V}-\vec{V_{OC}}}{-j26.5258} = \frac{\vec{V_{OC}-0.8\textrm{V}}}{10}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V_{OC}}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.1667 + j0.2262	\amp -j0.0377 			\amp 1.6667\\
								-0.08 - j0.0377 	\amp 0.1 + 0.j0377	 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 4.2283 - j4.8767\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 3.8098 - j3.7436
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The open-circuit voltage is equal to 3.8098<minus />j3.7436<nbsp />V.</p>

					<p>Short terminals a and b to calculate the Norton equivalent current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-complex-norton-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of (10V angle 0deg) voltage source, 6ohm resistor, -j5.31ohm impedance, -j26.5ohm impedance, 10ohm resistor, 0.8*V VCVS, and a short. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to 6ohm at node c. The 6ohm, -j5.31ohm, and -j26.5ohm connect at node d. The -j26.5ohm, 10ohm, and short connect at node a. The 10ohm connects to the high-potential end of the VCVS at node e. The low-potential end of both sources, short, and -j5.31ohm connect at node b. Branch current In flows through the short from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$\vec{V_{AC}}$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l_=$-$j5.31~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$\vec{V}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [C, l^=$-$j26.5~$\Omega$]					(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-]						(5,2);
									\draw[]	(5,2) to [cV, l_=0.8~$\vec{V}$, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [short]										++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [short, i_=$\vec{I_N}$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the two capacitors. All subsequent units are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V_{AC}} - \vec{V}}{6} = \frac{\vec{V}}{-j5.3052} + \frac{\vec{V}}{-j26.5258}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to the 20&#181;F capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{\vec{V}}{-j26.5258} = \frac{-0.8\textrm{V}}{10} + \vec{I_N}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{V}</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>\vec{I_N}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.1667 + j0.2262	\amp 0		\amp 1.6667\\
								-0.08 - j0.0377 	\amp 1	 	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 3.5188 - j4.7756\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp 0.4615 - j0.2494
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Norton equivalent current is equal to 0.4615<minus />j0.2494<nbsp />A.</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Z_N \amp= \frac{3.8098 - j3.7436~\textrm{V}}{0.4615 - j0.2494~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9.7815 - j2.8258</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch8-eq-impedance-capacitor" /> to calculate the value of the capacitance based on the Th&#233;venin equivalent reactance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C \amp= -\frac{1}{2\pi 300 (2.8258)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 187.74~\mu\textrm{F}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Transfer Functions</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="transfer-functions-exercise1-diagram" />. Then, determine the filter type.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="transfer-functions-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and C connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R2, and C connect at node c. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{\frac{1}{CR_1}}{j\omega + \frac{R_1+R_2}{CR_1R_2}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is a passive, first-order, low-pass filter.</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of <m>R_2</m> in parallel with <m>C</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_2//C \amp= R_2//\frac{1}{j\omega C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{R_2}{j\omega C}}{R_2 + \frac{1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2}{j\omega CR_2 + 1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>H(j\omega)</m> and reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{\frac{R_2}{j\omega CR_2 + 1}}{R_1+\frac{R_2}{j\omega CR_2 + 1}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2}{j\omega CR_2R_1 + R_1 + R_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{1}{CR_1}}{j\omega + \frac{R_1+R_2}{CR_1R_2}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and an open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the open connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R2, and the open connect at node c. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and a short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the short connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R2, and the short connect at node c. Voltage Vout(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a passive, first-order, low-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="transfer-functions-exercise2-diagram" />. Then, determine the filter type.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="transfer-functions-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, inductor L, capacitor C, and resistor R. The high-potential end of Vin connects to L at node a. L and C connect at node b. C and R connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and R connect at node d. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega\frac{R}{L}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\frac{R}{L} + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is passive, second-order, band-pass filter.</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>H(\omega)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{R}{R+j\omega L + \frac{1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega CR}{j\omega CR - \omega^2 LC + 1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega\frac{R}{L}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\frac{R}{L} + \frac{1}{LC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, short, open, and resistor R. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the short at node a. The short and the open connect at node b. The open and R connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and R connect at node d. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, open, short, and resistor R. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the open at node a. The open and the short connect at node b. The short and R connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin and R connect at node d. Voltage Vout(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [open]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is passive, second-order, band-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="transfer-functions-exercise3-diagram" />. Then, determine the filter type.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="transfer-functions-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vin, R, and C connect at node a. R, C, and L connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin connects to L at node c. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$, *-*]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=$R$]										(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(0,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + j\omega \frac{1}{RC}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\frac{1}{RC} + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is a passive, second-order, high-pass filter.</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent impedance of the resistor and capacitor in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R//C \amp= R//\frac{1}{j\omega C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{R}{j\omega C}}{R + \frac{1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R}{j\omega CR + 1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>H(j\omega)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \frac{j\omega L}{j\omega L + \frac{R}{j\omega CR + 1}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-\omega^2 LCR + j\omega L}{-\omega^2 LCR + j\omega L + R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-\omega^2 + j\omega \frac{1}{RC}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\frac{1}{RC} + \frac{1}{LC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, resistor R, and short. The high-potential end of Vin and R connect at node a. R and the short connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin connects to the short at node c. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with voltage source vin connected to an open. Vout (infinity) drops over the source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a passive, second-order, high-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="transfer-functions-exercise4-diagram" />. Then, determine the filter type.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="transfer-functions-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistors R1 and R2, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vin connects to C at node a. C, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and L connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, R1, and L connect at node d. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\left(\frac{R_2}{L}+\frac{1}{R_1C}\right) + \frac{R_1+R_2}{R_1LC}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is a passive, second-order, high-pass filter.</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>H(j\omega)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \left(\frac{R_1//(j\omega L + R_2)}{\frac{1}{j\omega C} + R_1//(j\omega L+R_2)}\right)\left(\frac{j\omega L}{R_2+j\omega L}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{\frac{(j\omega L + R_2)R_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}{\frac{1}{j\omega C} + \frac{(j\omega L + R_2)R_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}\right)\left(\frac{j\omega L}{R_2+j\omega L}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{\frac{R_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}{\frac{1}{j\omega C} + \frac{(j\omega L + R_2)R_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}\right)\left(j\omega L\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\frac{j\omega LR_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}{\frac{1}{j\omega C} + \frac{(j\omega L + R_2)R_1}{j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega LR_1}{j\omega LR_1 + R_1R_2 + \frac{L}{C} + \frac{R_2+R_1}{j\omega C}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-\omega^2 LR_1C}{-\omega^2 LR_1C + j\omega CR_1R_2 + j\omega L + R_1 + R_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\left(\frac{R_2}{L}+\frac{1}{R_1C}\right) + \frac{R_1+R_2}{R_1LC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, open, resistors R1 and R2, and short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the open at node a. The open, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and the short connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, R1, and the short connect at node d. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [open]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, short, resistors R1 and R2, and open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the short at node a. The short, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and the open connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, R1, and the open connect at node d. Voltage Vout(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a passive, second-order, high-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a transfer function for the circuit shown in <xref ref="transfer-functions-exercise5-diagram" />. Then, determine the filter type.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="transfer-functions-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of an op-amp, three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), two capacitors (C1 and C2) and voltage source Vin. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, C1, and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. R2, R3, and C2 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node c. R3, C2, and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. R2, C1, and the low-potential end of Vin connect at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				(opamp.+);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*]						($(opamp.+)-(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$, -*]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\omega)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [short]					($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$) to [short]					($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [C, l^=$C_2$, *-*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{j\omega\frac{1}{C_1R_1}+\frac{R_2+R_3}{C_1C_2R_1R_2R_3}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\left(\frac{1}{C_2R_3}+\frac{1}{C_1R_1}\right)+\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_3}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is an active, second-order, low-pass filter.</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the voltage at the non-inverting node, denoted as <m>v_x(\omega)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{v_x(\omega)}{v_{in}(\omega)} \amp= \frac{\frac{1}{j\omega C_1}}{R_1+\frac{1}{j\omega C_1}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{j\omega CR_1 +1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting node. Everything will be expressed as generic phasor for now.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-\frac{1}{R_2}\vec{V_X} \amp= \frac{\vec{V_X}-\vec{V_O}}{R_3//Z_{C2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{R_3//Z_{C2}}{R_2}\vec{V_X} \amp= \vec{V_X}-\vec{V_O}</mrow>
							<mrow>\vec{V_O} \amp= \left( 1 + \frac{R_3//Z_{C2}}{R_2}\right)\vec{V_X}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{\vec{V_O}}{\vec{V_I}} \amp= \left( 1 + \frac{R_3//Z_{C2}}{R_2}\right)\frac{\vec{V_X}}{\vec{V_I}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Reduce the impedance defined in the KCL equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\vec{Z} \amp= 1 + \frac{R_3//Z_{C2}}{R_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1 + \frac{\frac{R_3}{R_2j\omega C_2}}{R_3+\frac{1}{j\omega C_2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1 + \frac{\frac{R_3}{R_2}}{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1 + \frac{R_3}{R_2}}{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the value of <m>v_x/v_{in}</m> into the KCL equation. Reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>H(j\omega) \amp= \left( \frac{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1 + \frac{R_3}{R_2}}{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1} \right)\left(\frac{1}{j\omega CR_1 +1}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega C_2R_3 + 1 + \frac{R_3}{R_2}}{-\omega^2 C_2R_3C_1R_1 + j\omega C_1R_1 + j\omega C_2R_3 + 1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{j\omega\frac{1}{C_1R_1}+\frac{R_2+R_3}{C_1C_2R_1R_2R_3}}{-\omega^2 + j\omega\left(\frac{1}{C_2R_3}+\frac{1}{C_1R_1}\right)+\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_3}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of an op-amp, three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), and voltage source Vin. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. R2 and R3 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node c. R3 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. R2 and the low-potential end of Vin connect at node e. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]			(opamp.+);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$, -*]		($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(0)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [short]					($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$) to [short]				($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-transfer-functions-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of an op-amp, two resistors (R1 and R2), two shorts (SH1 and SH2) and voltage source Vin. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, SH1, and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. R2 and SH2 connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node c. SH2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. R2, SH1, and the low-potential end of Vin connect at node e. Voltage Vout(infinity) drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]						($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]			(opamp.+);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [short, *-*]						($(opamp.+)-(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$, -*]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\infty)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-2.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4.5,0)$) to [short]					($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.+)-(4.5,2.5)$) to [short]				($(opamp.+)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short]					($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is an active, second-order, low-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Filters</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise1">
				<statement>
					<p>Given the circuit shown in <xref ref="filters-exercise1-diagram" />, determine the filter type. Then, calculate the quality factor of the filter.</p>

					<figure xml:id="filters-exercise1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-filters-exercise1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Parallel RLC circuit with voltage source Vin. Voltage Vout is measured over the capacitor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>The circuit is a passive, second-order, low-pass filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>Q = \frac{\sqrt{L}}{R\sqrt{C}}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is not zero.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with L replaced by a short and C replaced by an open. Vout(0) is measured over the open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with L replaced by an open and C replaced by a short. Vout(infinity) is measured over the short.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open]											(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v_=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a low-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
					
					<p>This is a series RLC circuit with a homogeneous differential equation given by <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diffeq" />. The resonant frequency is equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}}{\frac{R}{L}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{L}{R\sqrt{LC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{\sqrt{L}}{R\sqrt{C}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise2">
				<statement>
					<p>Given the circuit shown in <xref ref="filters-exercse2-diagram" />, determine the filter type. Then, calculate the cutoff frequency and quality factor of the circuit. The circuit parameters are <m>L = 0.1~\textrm{H}</m>, <m>R = 100~\Omega</m>, and <m>C=40~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="filters-exercse2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-filters-exercise2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercse2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistor R, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vin connects to C at node a. C, R, and L connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R, and L connect at node c. Voltage Vout drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>The circuit is a passive, second-order, high-pass filter.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= 337~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>Q \amp= 2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, open, resistor R, and short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the open at node a. The open, R, and the short connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R, and the short connect at node c. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [open]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v_=$v_{out}(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, short, resistor R, and open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the short at node a. The short, R, and the open connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vin, R, and the open connect at node c. Voltage Vout(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\infty)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a high-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
					
					<p>This is a parallel RLC circuit with a homogeneous differential equation given by <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq" />.</p>
					
					<p>The transfer function of this circuit is</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + \frac{1}{RC}j\omega + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in parameter values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2}{-\omega^2 + 250j\omega + 250000}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0" /> to alculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.1)(40 \times 10^{-6})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 500~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{500}{250}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Set <m>|H(j\omega)|</m> equal to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m> and solve for <m>\omega</m> to calculate the cutoff frequency of the circuit.</p>
			
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>|H(j\omega_c)| = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp= \frac{-\omega_c^2}{\sqrt{(a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2}}</mrow>
							<mrow>2(\omega_c^2)^2 \amp= (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2 + (a_1\omega_c)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2\omega_c^2 + (a_0-\omega_c^2)^2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Let <m>x = \omega_c^2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>2x^2 \amp= a_1^2x + (a_0-x)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= a_1^2x + a_0^2 - 2a_0x + x^2</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= x^2 + (2a_0 - a_1^2)x - a_0^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= x^2 + 437500 x - (250000)^2</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= 337~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise3">
				<statement>
					<p>Given the circuit shown in <xref ref="filters-exercise3-diagram" />, determine the filter type. Then, calculate the center frequency, bandwidth, and quality factor.</p>

					<figure xml:id="filters-exercise3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-filters-exercise3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, 1kohm resistor, 10mH inductor, and 0.01uF capacitor. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor and inductor connect at node b. The inductor and capacitor connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects to the capacitor at node d. Vout drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [L, l_=10~mH]									(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=0.01~$\mu$F]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.25,4) to [open, v^=$v_{out}(\omega)$]					(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>The circuit is a passive, second-order, band-stop filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= 100000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= 100000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>Q \amp= 1</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, 1kohm resistor, short, and open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor and short connect at node b. The short and open connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects to the open at node d. Vout(0) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]										(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [open]										(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.25,4) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(0)$]						(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of voltage source Vin, 1kohm resistor, open, and short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor and open connect at node b. The open and short connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects to the short at node d. Vout(infinity) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]								(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [open]											(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [short]											(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.25,4) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\infty)$]					(3.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a band-stop filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>
					
					<p>The transfer function of this circuit is</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + \frac{1}{LC}}{\omega^2 + \frac{1}{RC}j\omega + \frac{1}{LC}}</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Plug in parameter values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>H(j\omega) = \frac{-\omega^2 + 1 \times 10^{10}}{\omega^2 + 100000j\omega + 1 \times 10^{10}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-cutoff-frequency-second-order" /> to calculate the center frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= \sqrt{1\times10^{10}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-bandwidth-a1" /> to calculate the bandwidth.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\beta = 100000~\textrm{rad/s}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-quality-factor" /> to calculate the quality factor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>Q \amp= \frac{100000}{100000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise4">
				<statement>
					<p>Given the circuit shown in <xref ref="filters-exercise4-diagram" />, determine the filter type and calculate the cutoff frequency.</p>

					<figure xml:id="filters-exercise4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-filters-exercise4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of an op-amp voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and C connect at node b. C, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin connects t node e. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node f. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\omega)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [C, l^=$C$]		(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]		($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]					($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]					($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]		($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>The circuit is an active, first-order, high-pass filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_c = \frac{1}{R_1C}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of an op-amp voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and an open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and the open connect at node b. The open, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin connects t node e. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node f. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(0)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [open]						(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$] ($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]				($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of an op-amp voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and a short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1 and the short connect at node b. The short, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node c. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vin connects t node e. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node f. Voltage Vout drops from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\infty)$]		($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [short]						(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]			($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(4,2.5)$) to [short, -o]					($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a high-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>

					<p>Derive the differential equation (in standard form) corresponding to the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-\frac{R_2}{R_1}\frac{d}{dt}v_{in}(t) = \frac{d}{dt}v_{out}(t) + \frac{1}{R_1C}v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-cutoff-frequency-first-order" /> to calculate the cutoff frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_c = \frac{1}{R_1C}</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise5">
				<statement>
					<p>Given the circuit shown in <xref ref="filters-exercise5-diagram" />, determine the filter type and calculate the cutoff frequency. The component values are <m>R_1 = 30~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 20~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, and <m>C = 5~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="filters-exercise5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch9-filters-exercise5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and C connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2, C, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects at node d. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\omega)$]	($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\omega)$]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]					($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [C, l^=$C$, *-*]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,2.5)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			($(opamp.out)+(0,3)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>The circuit is an active, first-order, low-pass filter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\omega_c = 10~\textrm{rad/s}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega=0</m>. <m>v_{out}(0)</m> is not zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and an open. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the open connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2, the open, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects at node d. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node e. Voltage Vout(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(0)$]		($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=30~k$\Omega$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(0)$]			($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]					($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$]			($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>\omega \rightarrow \infty</m>. <m>v_{out}(\infty)</m> is zero.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch9-filters-exercise5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vin, resistors R1 and R2, and short. The high-potential end of Vin connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the short connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node b. R2, the short, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vin connects at node d. The non-inverting input of the op-amp connects to ground at node e. Voltage Vout drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, -o]							($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(1,0)$) to [open, v_=$v_{out}(\infty)$]		($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=30~k$\Omega$]			(opamp.-);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(\infty)$]			($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,2.5)$) to [short, -o]					($(opamp.out)+(1,-1.99)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-]							($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short]						($(opamp.out)+(0,1.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is a low-pass filter. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-filtertype-table" />.)</p>

					<p>Derive the differential equation (in standard form) corresponding to the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-\frac{1}{R_1C}\frac{d}{dt}v_{in}(t) = \frac{d}{dt}v_{out}(t) + \frac{1}{R_2C}v_{out}(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch9-cutoff-frequency-first-order" /> to calculate the cutoff frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_c \amp= \frac{1}{R_2C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{(20000~\Omega)(5\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		
	</exercises>

</chapter>